WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.228
GRAY: Good morning.

00:00:15.228 --> 00:00:18.686
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.090
I'm Paul Gray, chairman of the
Institute's governing board,

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:30.680
and it is my privilege and
pleasure to welcome all of you

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.900
to this 1995 Technology Day.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.450
Before I begin
the introductions,

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:42.710
however, there's a question
I want to put to all of you,

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.320
or at least to all of you
who were at Symphony Hall

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:45.980
last night.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.515
Wasn't that an evening, huh?

00:00:47.515 --> 00:00:51.880
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.610
We have displayed
here on the stage,

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:04.800
or we had a moment ago, the
photographs of Jay Stratton

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.870
and Jerry Wiesner.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:11.010
And you will have noticed that
this Technology Day program is

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:17.130
dedicated to the memory of
Jay and Jerry, both of whom

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.460
passed away in this last
year, and both of whom

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.950
were deeply involved
throughout their lives

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.300
with MIT, and in
particular with that effort

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.920
that is the focus
of our activities

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.520
this morning, the Second
World War at this institution.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.470
And it seemed appropriate
for the committee

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.250
to make that dedication.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.280
The Technology Day
program this morning

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.410
focuses on World War II and MIT.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:51.210
The Institute played important
roles in the war effort,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.450
and it was profoundly
and irreversibly changed

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.180
by the war, as was the whole
fabric of American society.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.210
In this morning's program,
50 years, give or take

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.570
a month or two, from
the end of the war,

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.900
we reflect on the
origins of that conflict,

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.810
on the Institute
during the war years,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.110
and on the continuing
influence of the World War II

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.480
experience on society, on
this nation's universities,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.890
and on this special place.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.860
Our principal and keynote
speaker this morning

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.790
is a distinguished
biographer and historian

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.720
whose work has illuminated
the American political scene

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.240
in the 20th century.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.770
Doris Kearns Goodwin earned her
PhD in government at Harvard,

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.110
where she was a
Woodrow Wilson fellow,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.810
and where she served as
professor of government

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.910
for 10 years.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:54.330
In the 1960s, she
served as assistant

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.790
to President Lyndon
Johnson, whom

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.380
she assisted in the
preparation of his memoirs.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.200
In the last 18 years,
she has published

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.000
three important, highly
acclaimed big books, each

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.730
of which became a bestseller.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:17.480
In 1976, Lyndon Johnson
and the American Dream.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.910
In 1987, The Fitzgeralds
and the Kennedys.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:27.950
And most recently, in
1994, No Ordinary Time--

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:32.060
Franklin and Eleanor Roosevelt,
the American Home Front

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.640
During World War II.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.280
For this most
recent book, she was

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.760
awarded just a few months
ago the 1995 Pulitzer

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.850
Prize for history.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.730
Dr. Goodwin appears frequently
in public affairs programs

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.160
on television, and
has made appearances

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.230
in documentaries for PBS
concerning LBJ, the Kennedy

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:59.120
family, FDR, and, as a
measure of her breadth,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.280
Ken Burns' series on
the history of baseball.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.244
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.460
Dr. Goodwin is
eminently qualified

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.710
to put World War
II in perspective

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.260
for us at the outset of
this morning's program.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:16.131
Please join me in welcoming
Doris Kearns Goodwin to MIT.

00:04:16.131 --> 00:04:20.059
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:28.897
GOODWIN: Thank you.

00:04:28.897 --> 00:04:32.334
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.800
Let me say before I begin that
it has been my great privilege

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.340
in these last few months to have
two loves of mine come together

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.880
at the same time.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.360
First, more obviously,
my love of baseball,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.630
reflected in this 18-hour
history of baseball

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:51.520
that Ken Burns put
on the air where

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.390
he needed an irrational female
fan, so he kept showing me

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:58.720
over and over again, so that
when my book on Franklin

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.442
and Eleanor came out at the
same time, embarrassingly,

00:05:01.442 --> 00:05:03.400
it took me more years to
write the book than it

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.072
took the war about which it
was written to be fought.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:07.780
It did finally come
out at the same time,

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.610
but more people have
asked me about baseball

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.235
than about Franklin
and Eleanor as I've

00:05:11.235 --> 00:05:13.280
wandered around the country.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.910
However, these two loves
are not really as separate

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.210
as they might seem, for I
often root my love of history

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.000
to the day when my father
bought me my first big red score

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.994
book when I was
only six years old,

00:05:23.994 --> 00:05:26.410
and taught me that mysterious
and wonderful art of keeping

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:28.240
score play by play,
inning by inning

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.116
while he was at work
during the day so

00:05:30.116 --> 00:05:31.490
that, when he came
home at night,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.360
I could record for him the
history of that day's Brooklyn

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.890
Dodgers game.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.390
And when you're
only six or seven

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.910
and your father thinks you're
doing OK as an historian,

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.610
it's a great impetus
to do it later in life.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.290
He made it even
more magical for me,

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.880
for he never told
me that all of this

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.920
was actually described in
the newspapers the next day.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.650
So I thought, without
me, he would never

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.910
know what happened to our
beloved Brooklyn Dodgers.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:57.970
But to the main story
at hand, it seems to me

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:01.090
that the story of the
homefront in World War II

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:04.420
is really the story of the
transformation of a nation,

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.600
a nation whose economy
in 1939 and 1940

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.940
was still paralyzed
in depression,

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:14.500
with 17% unemployment, a nation
in which only one out of four

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.870
Americans graduated
from high school,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:20.800
one in 20 completed college, in
which the average worker earned

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.520
less than $1,000 a year and
the majority of citizens

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.080
lived in small towns stratified
along class and ethnic lines.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:32.980
It is the story of an army
which, in 1940, stood only 18th

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:35.800
in the world, trailing
not only Germany, Britain,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.430
and France, but also
Belgium, Spain, and Holland,

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:43.150
of a military establishment
that possessed only 400 combat

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.740
planes, a mere one-day supply
in the current European war,

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.100
and almost no modern
tanks, weapons, or guns.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:54.910
And yet, following the fall of
Western Europe to Nazi Germany,

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.730
and then the Japanese
attack on Pearl Harbor,

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:00.790
Franklin Roosevelt was able to
mobilize the American people

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:04.480
to work together for a common
cause against a common enemy,

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:07.120
forging an unparalleled
partnership between government

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.420
and business, which provided
generous loans and incentives

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.010
to build new
factories to produce

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.980
the weapons and the planes and
the tanks, which stimulated

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.340
15 million Americans to
leave their hometowns

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.280
and to congregate where the
war plants were in the north

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.920
and in the west, so that
somehow, one year after Pearl

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.690
Harbor, this democracy was so
mobilized that America had not

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.640
only caught up to Nazi Germany,

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.940
but was outproducing also
the Axis powers combined

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:36.160
and all the Allied
powers combined,

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.740
so that our weapons were
being used by our allies

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.450
in all the corners of the world.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:43.600
It is also the story
of the forgotten years

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.380
of the civil rights revolution,
a watershed experience in which

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:50.200
the seeds of the protest
movements of the succeeding

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.970
decades were sown.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.700
And it is the story of
more than 19 million women

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.700
who poured into factory jobs
during the war previously held

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.000
by men, performing beyond
everyone's expectations

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.270
as truck drivers, welders,
riveters, and stevedores,

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:07.060
creating new expectations
and new problems

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.370
that would ultimately set
the stage for the women's

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:11.560
movement in the decades ahead.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.530
And it is the story of
the GI Bill of Rights,

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:17.050
which carried more than two
million veterans into college

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:21.220
after the war, so much so that
50% of the college enrollment

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:24.100
was veterans in the
late '40s, transforming

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:26.980
higher education,
research institutions,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:29.110
and lifting the
educational horizons

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.510
of an entire generation.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:36.220
It is this story I wish to tell
you today, using as my vehicle

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:38.470
the remarkable relationship
between Franklin

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.559
Roosevelt and his wife,
Eleanor, during the war,

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:44.200
hoping that this personal
narrative will bring you

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:48.190
the larger story of the
homefront and bring it to life.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:49.840
Let me say at the
outset that, if I

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.830
have to point to one quality of
Franklin Roosevelt's that made

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.980
and shaped his leadership of
the homefront during the war,

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.920
it would be his absolute
confidence in himself,

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:02.200
in his country, and in
the democratic system

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:03.520
of government.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.650
Even in the grimmest
days of 1940,

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.740
after France fell to Germany
and England stood alone,

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.870
Roosevelt had
absolutely no doubt

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:14.290
that, once the dormant energies
of democracy were mobilized,

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.870
the US would fully
meet the German threat,

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.300
that the uncoerced
energy of a free people

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:23.110
would inevitably outperform
a totalitarian regime.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:24.580
It was this confidence
that allowed

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.310
him to ship our limited supplies
to England when England stood

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:30.092
alone against Germany,
and it was this confidence

00:09:30.092 --> 00:09:32.050
that allowed him to invest
more than $2 billion

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:36.200
in a theoretical enterprise
to build the atomic bomb.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.110
And more importantly, he
was able to communicate

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.930
his confidence and faith
to the American people,

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:45.772
helping to energize a massive
shift to a wartime economy,

00:09:45.772 --> 00:09:47.480
and helping to bring
about the commitment

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.510
of the American citizens
to working three shifts, 24

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:53.600
hours a day, to produce
the unimaginable quantities

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.690
of weapons that eventually
did win the war.

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.820
He helped to create the
dedication of scientists

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.160
and researchers and
universities like this

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.560
to produce the groundbreaking
discoveries that improved

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.230
radar, created large-scale
production of penicillin,

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:10.010
developed plasma, proximity
fuses, and the jet engines.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:12.200
His strength came,
in my judgment,

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:15.440
in leading the people step by
step, never getting too far

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.440
ahead of them, but always moving
them forward little by little

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.150
toward his goals.

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:21.770
And in shaping
public opinion, he

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:24.200
understood intuitively
the importance

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.240
of rationing his
appearances, something

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.330
our current political leaders
seem not to have understood,

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:33.410
in order to guarantee and
guard the dignity of the office

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.780
and to ensure that
each appearance has

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.400
a dramatic effect.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.860
Thus, he limited his
celebrated fireside chats

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:43.460
to only two or three a year,
only 30 in the entire 12 years

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.050
of his presidency.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.480
And the result was, as
Saul Bellow describes

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.690
in a recent memoir, he would
describe walking down a Chicago

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:52.190
street in the middle
of the war when

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:55.340
one of Roosevelt's rare
fireside chats was on the air.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:57.350
He said that every single
car had pulled over

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.935
to the curb to listen, every
single house had it on,

00:10:59.935 --> 00:11:02.060
and you could see inside
the windows people sitting

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.435
in their living rooms, in
their kitchen with their radios

00:11:04.435 --> 00:11:05.360
before them.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.610
He said that it was such
a moment that you felt not

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:09.530
only connected to
Roosevelt's voice,

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:11.180
and he said you could keep
walking down the street

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.763
and not miss a word of what he
was saying because everyone was

00:11:13.763 --> 00:11:16.310
listening, but you felt
connected to everyone else

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.620
in the country because you
realized they, too, were

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.870
experiencing that same moment.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.690
And when a leader is able to
create that sense of connection

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:25.190
between the fellow
Americans, it's

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:28.070
probably the greatest
strength that leader has.

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:31.070
I think my favorite Roosevelt
fireside chat took place

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:33.230
in February of
1942, when we were

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:35.780
at one of the lowest depths
of the war, several months

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.700
after Pearl Harbor, losing
battles in the Pacific.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:41.210
He asked everyone in the country
before he gave his address

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:42.860
to get a map out
so that they could

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:46.036
follow the progress of the
war in this far-flung place.

00:11:46.036 --> 00:11:47.660
Well, suddenly, there
was this huge run

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.630
on maps all across the country,
so much so that the New York

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:52.760
Times interviewed
the guy who ran C.S.

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:54.890
Hammond's map store in New York.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:56.367
And he had been
there for 25 years.

00:11:56.367 --> 00:11:58.700
He said he had never sold so
many maps in his whole time

00:11:58.700 --> 00:11:59.600
that he was there.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.683
And then he added this
wonderful comment, he said,

00:12:01.683 --> 00:12:04.160
even my wife of 25
years, who hates maps,

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:06.830
asked me to bring a map home.

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.230
So then I started thinking,
what kind of a marriage

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:11.030
did these two people have?

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:14.529
This is when you go off
the track as a biographer.

00:12:14.529 --> 00:12:16.070
But nevertheless,
she's sitting there

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:17.736
at the kitchen table
like everyone else,

00:12:17.736 --> 00:12:20.120
listening to Roosevelt
talk very soberly about why

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.815
it will take many months before
the tide of the war will turn,

00:12:22.815 --> 00:12:25.190
explaining to them what the
battles are that are going on

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:26.340
in the Pacific.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:27.920
But then he calls
on American history

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.810
to give strength and confidence
to the American people.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.350
He recalls what it was like
for Washington in the winters

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:35.870
at Valley Forge, what it was
like for the pioneers going

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:37.490
over the Rocky
Mountains, what it

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.740
was like in the early
days of the Civil War.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:42.380
And he's so certain that
democracy will win out

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.440
over the totalitarian powers
that his speech produces

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:47.126
thousands of
telegrams at the end,

00:12:47.126 --> 00:12:49.250
telling him that he has to
go on the air every day.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.480
It's the only way morale
will be sustained.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:54.560
But he writes back saying,
intuitively understanding,

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.750
if my speeches ever
become routine,

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:58.650
they will lose
their effectiveness.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.910
So he understood that prestige
and that majesty, in a sense,

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:04.880
of the presidency, and how
to time his appearances when

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:07.160
they were absolutely necessary.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.560
Indeed, Winston Churchill,
the incomparable leader

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:12.800
himself of England, fully
comprehended the importance

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:16.430
of Roosevelt's confidence
in mobilizing his people.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:20.090
Churchill once said to encounter
Roosevelt for the first time,

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:23.090
with all his buoyant sparkle,
his iridescent personality,

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:25.580
and his sublime confidence
was like opening

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.050
your first bottle of champagne.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:29.660
Now, this was an
extraordinary comment

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:31.940
coming from a man long
accustomed himself

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:33.740
to being the center
of attention,

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.890
but Churchill had come to know
and admire Roosevelt greatly,

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:39.620
having spent weeks and even
months at a time actually

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.410
living in the White House,
spending his time in a room

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:44.960
diagonally across from
Roosevelt's bedroom,

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:47.750
bringing his valets, his
stewards, and his habits

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.270
with him, which included
starting to drink early

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:53.090
in the morning wine, moving
to Scotch and whiskey

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.890
in the middle of the
day and the afternoon,

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.480
and ending up at brandy before
he went to bed at night,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:01.010
and somehow saving England
in the process of all this.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.320
He indeed became part
of an intimate circle

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:05.390
of friends who lived
in those small family

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:08.820
quarters of the White House with
the Roosevelts during the war,

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:11.930
including Franklin's secretary,
Missy LeHand, who really was,

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.660
in many ways, his other
wife when Eleanor traveled,

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:17.450
Franklin's closest advisor
and friend, Harry Hopkins, who

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:20.870
occupied a bedroom right next
to his, Eleanor's closest

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:22.850
friend, Lorena Hickok,
who helped her to become

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:25.183
the First Lady that she was,
and lived in a bedroom next

00:14:25.183 --> 00:14:28.490
to Eleanor's study, Franklin's
mother, Sarah, who occupied

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:31.100
the best guest suite
whenever she arrived,

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:33.050
and of course Winston Churchill.

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:36.020
I found myself so intrigued by
all these wonderful characters

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.060
living in such close
quarters that I

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.670
kept imagining the fabulous
conversations they must have

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.340
had at night, and wished
so often that I could just

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:45.710
see those family
quarters, to picture who

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:48.020
had lived where 50 years ago.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.900
I happened to mention this on a
radio program in Washington one

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:52.880
night, which Hillary
Clinton heard.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.560
So she called me up
at the radio station

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.726
and invited me to sleep
overnight in the White House

00:14:56.726 --> 00:14:58.520
so that we could figure
out where everybody

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:00.601
had slept 50 years ago.

00:15:00.601 --> 00:15:02.600
So I had this wonderful
night in the White House

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.970
where, after a state dinner,
my husband and I wandered

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:07.160
through the rooms with
President and Mrs. Clinton,

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.618
and figured out every single
place where everyone had been.

00:15:09.618 --> 00:15:11.570
And the best part
of all was that we

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:14.450
realized we were staying in
Winston Churchill's bedroom.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.400
So there was no way I
could sleep that night.

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.620
I just kept picturing
Winston Churchill behind me

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:19.950
the whole time.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:22.460
In fact, one of my
favorite stories of the war

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.290
took place in that very bedroom.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:27.290
In January of 1942, when
Churchill was staying there,

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.230
he and Roosevelt were set
to sign a document later

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:34.040
that afternoon putting the
associated nations, 23 of them,

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:35.660
against the Axis powers.

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.730
But no one liked the
word associated nations

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:39.500
because it had no
rhythm or ring.

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:42.200
So early that morning,
Roosevelt came up with the idea

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.200
that he was so excited about to
call themselves United Nations,

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:46.820
which of course it became.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.577
So he had himself wheeled
into Churchill's bedroom

00:15:49.577 --> 00:15:51.410
to tell him the news,
but Churchill had just

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:54.800
stepped forth from the tub
with absolutely nothing on.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:56.600
So Roosevelt said,
I'm so sorry, I'll

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:57.770
come back in a few minutes.

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:00.770
But Churchill, with that amazing
ability to speak spontaneously,

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:03.026
said, oh, no, the prime
minister of Great Britain

00:16:03.026 --> 00:16:05.525
has nothing to hide from the
president of the United States.

00:16:05.525 --> 00:16:07.288
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:07.288 --> 00:16:09.152
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:15.052
So somehow, while Roosevelt
describes United Nations,

00:16:15.052 --> 00:16:16.510
Churchill thinks
it's a great idea.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:18.700
He then quotes in full
memory from a poem

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:21.830
by Lord Byron where the term
United Nations was used.

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:23.590
So whenever I got into
the tub that night,

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:25.080
then Churchill was really there.

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:27.430
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:27.430 --> 00:16:29.800
Indeed, I believe through
all the turmoil of the worst

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.690
days of the war,
a central quality

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:34.150
of Roosevelt's character
was his capacity

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:36.490
to relax with his
friends and associates,

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:39.400
to cast off his worries
and simply enjoy himself,

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:41.410
thus replenishing
his energies to face

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:43.660
the struggles of
the following day.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.550
Every night, he had
the same ritual.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.440
He had a cocktail hour
with his associates

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.910
on that second
floor, and the rule

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:51.149
was you were not allowed
to talk about politics.

00:16:51.149 --> 00:16:52.690
You could talk
gossip, you could tell

00:16:52.690 --> 00:16:54.231
who was involved
with whom, you could

00:16:54.231 --> 00:16:57.100
discuss movies and Hollywood,
anything but politics.

00:16:57.100 --> 00:16:59.410
When Eleanor was away,
his secretary Missy LeHand

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:00.310
presided.

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.410
She listened adoringly
to the same old stories

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.630
and jokes he liked to
tell over and over again.

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:07.089
When Eleanor did come, she
somehow couldn't help herself

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:09.430
but bring up slum
clearance or civil rights,

00:17:09.430 --> 00:17:11.939
but most of the time
the rules prevailed.

00:17:11.939 --> 00:17:13.730
And then if he were
anxious during the day,

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:15.579
Missy would arrange
a poker game at night

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:17.619
with his cabinet, which
he loved to relax,

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.930
and could again forget
the cares of state.

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:22.270
One of my favorite stories
is he had an annual poker

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:25.057
game on the night that
Congress was set to adjourn.

00:17:25.057 --> 00:17:27.640
And the rule was that whenever
the Speaker of the House called

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:29.140
to say they were
adjourning, whoever

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:31.325
was ahead at that moment
would win the poker game.

00:17:31.325 --> 00:17:32.950
On one particular
night, he was playing

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.890
with Secretary of the Treasury
Morgenthau and several others.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.450
And at 9:30, when
the Speaker called

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:38.970
to say they were
adjourning, Morgenthau

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.540
that was way, way
ahead of Roosevelt,

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:42.039
so Roosevelt took
the phone and just

00:17:42.039 --> 00:17:44.440
pretended it was someone
else on the phone.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.720
So they kept playing,
and they kept playing,

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:49.570
until midnight when finally
Roosevelt pulled ahead,

00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:52.000
so he asked the aide to bring
him the phone immediately.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:54.370
Oh, Mr. Speaker, you're
adjourning, that's fine.

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:56.410
He brings all the chips,
he wins all the money.

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:58.870
Everything's great until the
next morning, when Morgenthau

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:02.230
reads in the newspaper that the
Congress had adjourned at 9:30.

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:04.360
They said that he was so
angry that he actually

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:06.760
resigned his post as cabinet
member until, of course,

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:09.490
Roosevelt cajoled
him into staying.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:11.950
Roosevelt was also able
to relax with movies.

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:14.200
He loved adventure movies
and mystery movies.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:16.020
Again, when Eleanor
did choose the movies,

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:17.980
it would somehow become
The Grapes of Wrath

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:20.050
or a documentary
on civil rights.

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:21.811
And he could relax
on long fishing trips

00:18:21.811 --> 00:18:24.310
in the middle of the war, 10-day
fishing trips when he could

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:25.919
be out of touch with people.

00:18:25.919 --> 00:18:27.460
It was on one of
these fishing trips,

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:30.520
in fact, when, in the midst
of Britain's financial crisis,

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:32.170
through the solitude
and the time,

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.410
his creative juices
came to fruition,

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:38.020
and he was able to come up with
a brilliant idea of Lend-Lease.

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.990
And then, from his home in Hyde
Park, where he went 200 times

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:43.960
during his presidency, he
received the enormous solace

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:46.150
of just remembering
a root, a place where

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:48.670
he had known since he
was a child, all of which

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:52.670
allowed him, I think, to make
the presidency not a burden,

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.580
as so many other
leaders have found it,

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:57.850
but rather a continual
source of vitality.

00:18:57.850 --> 00:18:59.680
And even more
importantly, in some ways,

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:03.160
this peace, this equanimity
that he felt within himself

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:05.890
freed up resources,
psychic resources,

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:08.980
that allowed him to be
extremely receptive to the needs

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:10.150
of others.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:12.400
He had, the British
philosopher Isaiah Berlin

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:15.670
once suggested, an exceptionally
sensitive awareness,

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:18.790
conscious or subconscious,
of the desires, the hopes,

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:21.220
the fears, the loves, the
hatreds of the human beings

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.170
who composed his democracy.

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:25.210
This uncanny awareness,
Berlin argued,

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:27.580
was the source of
Roosevelt's genius.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:29.920
It was almost as if the
inner currents, the tremors

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:32.020
of human society were
registering themselves

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:36.590
within his nervous system with a
kind of seismological accuracy.

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:38.410
Now, where this uncanny
knowledge came from

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:40.000
is partly a mystery.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:41.770
As a child, his
mother, Sarah, claimed

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:44.200
Roosevelt was remarkably
intuitive, able

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.210
to anticipate the
desires of his parents

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.400
even before he was
told what to do,

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.440
able to sense the
tensions in the family

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:52.510
when his father became
an invalid, knowing

00:19:52.510 --> 00:19:54.520
exactly how to respond.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.610
Then, too, his bout with polio
undoubtedly made him even more

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:00.490
aware of what other people
were thinking and feeling,

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:04.120
ever determined to make sure
that no one felt pity for him.

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:06.430
The polio produced a
plowing up of his nature,

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:09.340
Frances Perkins, his Labor
Secretary, once said.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:11.800
He became much more
patient, more tolerant,

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:14.800
more warm-hearted, more
empathetic to others

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.430
for whom fate had also
dealt an unkind hand.

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:20.530
But I think Franklin
Roosevelt would be the first

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:23.050
to admit that,
beyond his intuition

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:25.270
and beyond his
experience of polio,

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:27.730
his extraordinary knowledge
of the hopes and the fears

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:29.680
and the needs of
the American people

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:33.160
came as a result of his wife
Eleanor's remarkable ramblings

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:36.670
around the country, for she was,
as he said over and over again

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:40.060
after his polio, his
eyes and his ears.

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.686
Their partnership was
indeed unparalleled.

00:20:42.686 --> 00:20:45.310
She was the first First Lady to
travel the country from one end

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:47.530
to the other, serving
as a voice for people

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:49.990
without power, for poor,
for migrant workers,

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:52.549
for blacks, for women,
the first to testify

00:20:52.549 --> 00:20:54.340
before a congressional
committee, the first

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:57.010
to write a syndicated
column, and the first to hold

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:59.140
regular press conferences
where she made

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.530
a simple rule that only female
reporters could cover her press

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:05.350
conferences, which meant that
every newspaper in the country

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:07.870
had to hire usually its
first female reporter.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:09.814
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:09.814 --> 00:21:13.216
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:18.100
Now, this partnership was
all the more remarkable

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:19.780
when one realizes
that it was born

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.512
in the pain of Eleanor's
discovery in 1918

00:21:22.512 --> 00:21:24.970
that Franklin was having an
affair with a young woman named

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:26.290
Lucy Mercer.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:28.540
When Eleanor came upon
a packet of love letters

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.100
Lucy had written
to her husband, she

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.620
said that the bottom
dropped out of her world.

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.240
She offered him a
divorce at once,

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:37.360
but after long discussions,
they agreed to stay together.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:40.180
He promised he would never
see Lucy Mercer again.

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:42.010
And more importantly,
this catastrophe

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:44.680
in their married life
reconstituted the marriage,

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:47.260
giving Eleanor the freedom
to go outside the marriage

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:48.700
to find fulfillment.

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.610
She became involved in teaching
and settlement housework.

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:53.050
She got close to
a group of women

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:55.660
activists who taught her that
she had a whole range of talent

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:58.270
she had never known before,
for public speaking,

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:01.060
for organizing, for
articulating a cause.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:02.830
And then her activism
became critical

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:05.386
when he contracted
polio three years later

00:22:05.386 --> 00:22:06.760
and had to spend
weeks and months

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:08.290
at a time at Warm
Springs, Georgia,

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:11.560
desperately trying to walk
on his own power again.

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.630
She kept his political
hopes alive by going

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:15.490
from one meeting to
the other so that, when

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.184
he came back and won the
governorship, she was there.

00:22:18.184 --> 00:22:19.600
And then when he
became president,

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:21.280
her travels multiplied.

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:23.531
She traveled 200 days
a year, so much so

00:22:23.531 --> 00:22:25.405
that the Washington Post
once had a headline,

00:22:25.405 --> 00:22:29.020
"Eleanor spends night in White
House," exclamation point.

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:31.060
Visiting southern
blacks, migrant workers,

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:34.330
CCC camps, bringing him back
an honest appraisal of which

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:37.060
of his programs were working
and which were failing,

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:40.120
the kind of evaluation
that leaders rarely get.

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:42.680
Then the war came along and
threatened this partnership,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.265
which had worked so brilliantly
during the Depression, for now

00:22:45.265 --> 00:22:47.140
the president was spending
more and more time

00:22:47.140 --> 00:22:48.790
with military men,
with businessmen,

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:51.220
with conservatives, with
southern congressmen,

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.410
and Eleanor felt that
she had lost her partner,

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:56.090
and in so doing lost
her sense of self.

00:22:56.090 --> 00:22:58.300
She cycled into one of
her periodic depressions,

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:00.790
coming out of it only when
she realized there was still

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:04.600
a critical fight to be fought to
try and make the war a vehicle

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:07.405
for social reform at home so
that, when the soldiers came

00:23:07.405 --> 00:23:09.280
home at the end of the
war, the country would

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:11.500
be a more socially just place.

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.050
It meant she had to become
an agitator, often working

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:15.610
at cross purposes
with her husband,

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:18.070
for his sole concern
was to win the war.

00:23:18.070 --> 00:23:20.680
She had to be
relentless, often a pest,

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:22.510
but he understood the
importance of what

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:26.050
she was pushing him to do, and
his presidency was immeasurably

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:27.940
enriched as a result.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:31.210
Nowhere was her influence
greater than on civil rights.

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:33.760
At the start of the war, blacks
were openly discriminated

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.380
against in the factories.

00:23:35.380 --> 00:23:37.540
But through the combination
of A. Philip Randolph

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.700
threatening a march on
Washington and Eleanor's

00:23:39.700 --> 00:23:42.520
negotiations, Franklin
Roosevelt agreed

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:44.650
to create the first
Fair Employment Practice

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:46.600
Commission with sanctions
to force companies

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:48.239
to open their doors to blacks.

00:23:48.239 --> 00:23:50.530
And by the end of the war,
more than two million blacks

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:53.020
had jobs at all levels of
skills in factories they never

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:54.266
would have had before.

00:23:54.266 --> 00:23:56.140
I was able to interview
one of these men, who

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.780
became the first black motorman
in the Philadelphia mass

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.160
transit system.

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:02.136
And he told me he had
scored 95 on the test,

00:24:02.136 --> 00:24:03.760
and he was so excited
his first morning

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:06.250
on the job he got to the
station, to the bus station,

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:08.590
early, but there were no
buses running, no trolleys,

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:10.270
no trains, no subways.

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:11.840
He didn't know
what had happened.

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.620
He went home to discover that
the 10,000 white workers had

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:17.110
all gone on strike because
he, the first black man,

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:19.000
was coming to work that day.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:20.620
So the strike went
on for four days,

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:23.500
paralyzing Philadelphia's
war production system,

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:26.074
until finally Roosevelt
took decisive action.

00:24:26.074 --> 00:24:27.490
He issued a very
simple statement.

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:29.590
He told the striking workers,
if they did not come back

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:30.964
to work on Monday,
they would all

00:24:30.964 --> 00:24:33.190
be drafted on Tuesday morning.

00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:35.390
They came back to work.

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:37.180
And on Tuesday
morning, William Barber

00:24:37.180 --> 00:24:39.940
became the first black
motorman in Philadelphia.

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:41.230
So, too, in the military.

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:42.850
Whereas at the start
of the war blacks

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:45.910
were serving in the lowest level
jobs in the Army and the Navy,

00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:48.070
by the end, through the
combination of the growing

00:24:48.070 --> 00:24:50.759
civil rights movement
and Eleanor's agitation,

00:24:50.759 --> 00:24:53.050
they were serving in every
branch of the Army and Navy,

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:56.080
as pilots, tank men,
doctors, paratroopers,

00:24:56.080 --> 00:25:00.340
and the number of black officers
had grown from five in 1940

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:03.220
to over 7,000 in 1945.

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:05.912
It took so many memos on
Eleanor's part on civil rights

00:25:05.912 --> 00:25:07.870
to poor General Marshall
in the War Department,

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:09.460
who was trying to
create an army,

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:11.830
that he finally had to
create a separate general

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.740
just to deal with Eleanor
Roosevelt's communications.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:16.420
But her pressure worked.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:18.881
And in many ways, it seems
to me that her contributions

00:25:18.881 --> 00:25:20.380
on civil rights are
some of the most

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:23.140
affirming moments in the
history of the homefront.

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:24.850
But she was also far
ahead of her time

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:27.220
in championing the movement
of women into the factories

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:28.289
during the war.

00:25:28.289 --> 00:25:29.830
Through her speeches
and her columns,

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:31.690
she countered the
early resistance

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:34.021
on the part of factory
owners to employing women.

00:25:34.021 --> 00:25:36.520
They never thought the women
could operate these complicated

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:37.020
machines.

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:39.942
They thought women would disrupt
the camaraderie at the factory.

00:25:39.942 --> 00:25:41.900
But once more and more
men went off to the war,

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:43.390
they had to turn to women.

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.210
And eventually, by the middle
of the war, 50% and 60%

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:47.770
of the shipyards
and the factories

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:51.120
that were making the planes,
the jobs were held by women.

00:25:51.120 --> 00:25:54.010
And the amazing thing was
the productivity went way up.

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:56.140
So these factory owners
had to do a big study

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:57.550
to figure out how
it had happened

00:25:57.550 --> 00:25:59.383
that women had been
able to learn to operate

00:25:59.383 --> 00:26:00.910
these complicated machines.

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:03.520
And I love the answer that came
back on one of the studies.

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:04.816
They said it was very simple.

00:26:04.816 --> 00:26:06.190
When a woman was
asked to operate

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:08.140
a new piece of
machinery, unlike a man,

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:09.490
she would ask directions.

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:11.378
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:11.378 --> 00:26:14.682
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:19.720
I think any of us who
have ever driven endlessly

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:23.620
around without directions being
asked know what that's about.

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:26.110
Well then, once Eleanor
saw the importance of women

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:27.616
to the war effort,
it was she who

00:26:27.616 --> 00:26:28.990
convinced government
and business

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:31.600
to create a full-fledged
partnership to create daycare

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:33.999
centers operating
24 hours a day,

00:26:33.999 --> 00:26:36.040
almost like a Head Start
program for the children

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:37.780
during that period of time.

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:39.220
They worked out a
situation where,

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:41.560
if you worked a day shift, a
hot meal was waiting for you

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:43.018
at the end of the
day to bring home

00:26:43.018 --> 00:26:45.580
to your family, all of which
allowed that productivity

00:26:45.580 --> 00:26:48.160
to continue unmatched,
and was an example

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.700
of the unparalleled partnership
of Franklin and Eleanor.

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:54.330
Now, to be sure, Roosevelt's
sensitive antennae,

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:58.150
in part helped by Eleanor,
produced occasional problems.

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:00.730
So skillful was he in sensing
what other people wanted

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:03.640
him to say that he often made
people feel he was agreeing

00:27:03.640 --> 00:27:05.860
with them and what they
were saying when he really

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:07.270
wasn't agreeing at all.

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:09.790
Perhaps in the long run,
one disillusioned aide

00:27:09.790 --> 00:27:12.580
said fewer friends would
have been lost by bluntness

00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:15.490
than by the misunderstandings
that arose from his engaging

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:17.470
and charming ambiguity.

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:20.470
Moreover, so desirous was he
of making other people like him

00:27:20.470 --> 00:27:22.510
that he was unable
to fire anyone,

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:24.940
neither his isolationist
Secretary of War Henry

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:27.460
Woodring until the crisis
finally occurred in Western

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:30.160
Europe, nor his drunken valet
who was supposed to help him

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:32.500
every morning out of
bed into the bathroom,

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:35.080
nor the dramatic housekeeper
who refused to prepare

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:37.990
the meals for him that he liked,
saying that she knew better

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:39.850
what was good for him.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:42.190
One must also concede
more serious failures

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:44.020
of perception and
vision that led

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:47.110
to his forcible relocation
of the Japanese-Americans

00:27:47.110 --> 00:27:49.030
and the lack of a
more decisive response

00:27:49.030 --> 00:27:51.940
to the extermination
of the European Jews.

00:27:51.940 --> 00:27:54.430
And yet, in the end, I believe
Roosevelt's strengths far

00:27:54.430 --> 00:27:55.930
outweighed his weaknesses.

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:58.060
Despite clashing interests
and disparate goals,

00:27:58.060 --> 00:27:59.830
he kept the American
people moving forward

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:01.240
together through
the two greatest

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:03.730
crises of this century, first
the Depression, and then

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:05.020
the war.

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:06.910
And always he kept his
strength on the side

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:09.520
of freedom and his trust
in the uncoerced energy

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:11.195
of the ordinary citizen.

00:28:11.195 --> 00:28:12.820
Through the whole of
Franklin's career,

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:16.120
Eleanor once said, despite
maneuvering and flexibility

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:18.370
and some deviousness,
there was never

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:20.950
any deviation from
his original objective

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:24.820
to make life better for the
average man, woman, and child.

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:27.520
Indeed, it was Eleanor's
abiding love and deep respect

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:30.670
for Roosevelt's great strengths
as a leader that allowed her,

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:32.740
in the months after
his death, to come

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:36.370
to terms with the deep hurts
in their personal relationship.

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:39.520
During the war, Roosevelt had
suffered a series of losses.

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:41.590
His secretary, Missy
LeHand, though she was only

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:44.020
in her early 40s, suffered
a devastating stroke,

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:46.870
and was never able to
speak intelligibly again.

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.140
Three months later, his mother,
Sarah, died at Hyde Park.

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:51.640
At that point, he
turned to Eleanor

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:54.056
and asked her essentially to
stay home and stop traveling.

00:28:54.056 --> 00:28:56.230
He needed her even more
as a companion than as

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:57.230
a political partner.

00:28:57.230 --> 00:28:59.230
It's one of those moments
when, as a biographer,

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.660
you want to reach back in time
and say, Eleanor, just do it.

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:03.701
I know that you love him
and I know he loves you,

00:29:03.701 --> 00:29:05.560
and I know he won't
live much longer.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:08.620
But I understand fully why she
couldn't make that commitment.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:10.430
She'd been so hurt
so many years before,

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:13.930
and had built up slowly a sense
of herself in the outside world

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:15.550
that she couldn't give up.

00:29:15.550 --> 00:29:17.300
So she started
traveling more and more,

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:18.550
and in his
loneliness, he finally

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:20.049
brought their eldest
daughter, Anna,

00:29:20.049 --> 00:29:22.600
in to take Missy's place as
his hostess in the White House

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:24.100
when Eleanor was away.

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:26.110
It became complicated
for, when Eleanor

00:29:26.110 --> 00:29:27.730
would return from
her trips, sometimes

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:30.271
she would feel displaced by her
own daughter, with whom she'd

00:29:30.271 --> 00:29:30.970
been very close.

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:34.300
She had confided in Anna early
on the story of Lucy Mercer,

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:36.850
and Anna had strongly
taken her mother's side.

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:38.440
But now, in many
ways, Anna was now

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:40.450
becoming her father's daughter.

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:42.610
Everything got more
complicated in that last year

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:45.670
of Roosevelt's life,
when, in spring of 1944,

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.171
he was diagnosed with
congestive heart failure.

00:29:48.171 --> 00:29:50.170
He was sent to Bernard
[? Baruch's ?] plantation

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:53.140
to recuperate for a month,
and it was there essentially

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:56.770
that he saw Lucy Mercer for
the first time since 1918.

00:29:56.770 --> 00:29:58.480
She'd married a very
wealthy older man

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:01.690
named Winthrop Rutherfurd,
lived in South Carolina not far

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:04.600
from [? Baruch's ?] plantation,
and Rutherfurd had just died

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:06.459
several months before that.

00:30:06.459 --> 00:30:08.500
I'm convinced that, in
seeing Lucy at this point,

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:10.570
it was simply a
friendship in his life.

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:12.520
It may have rekindled
him a memory of what

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:15.445
it was like when he was young,
before his polio, a memory

00:30:15.445 --> 00:30:17.320
of what it was like when
his body was strong,

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:20.080
before the heart disease that
was now deteriorating him day

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:20.979
by day.

00:30:20.979 --> 00:30:23.020
But he didn't trust that
Eleanor would understand

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:24.500
that it was simply a
friendship, and must

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:25.916
have remembered
the pledge that he

00:30:25.916 --> 00:30:27.760
had made so many years before.

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:30.217
So he realized the only way
he could see Lucy regularly

00:30:30.217 --> 00:30:32.050
would be to have her
come to the White House

00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:33.480
when Eleanor was away.

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:35.830
And the only person he trusted
with that delicate task

00:30:35.830 --> 00:30:37.319
was his daughter, Anna.

00:30:37.319 --> 00:30:39.610
You can imagine the dilemma
that Anna found herself in,

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:41.276
knowing what it would
mean to her mother

00:30:41.276 --> 00:30:42.430
if she ever found out.

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:44.950
But Anna could see that her
father was dying in a way

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:48.910
that Eleanor never did, and as
a result Anna made the decision

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:50.830
to allow Lucy to come
to the White House six

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:53.320
times in that last year
of Roosevelt's life,

00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:55.044
always having dinner
with Roosevelt.

00:30:55.044 --> 00:30:57.460
And I've checked, and Eleanor
was doing what she wanted to

00:30:57.460 --> 00:30:58.420
at that period of time.

00:30:58.420 --> 00:31:00.670
She was with women when they
won awards for excellence

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:02.586
in the factory, with
civil rights leaders when

00:31:02.586 --> 00:31:05.680
the laws came down desegregating
the [? PX's ?] in the Army

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:06.369
camp.

00:31:06.369 --> 00:31:08.410
So it all might have worked
out with no one being

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:10.360
hurt but for the fact
that Lucy happened

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:13.210
to be in Warm Springs,
Georgia when Franklin died.

00:31:13.210 --> 00:31:15.670
She knew enough to leave, but
when Eleanor flew down later

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:18.130
that night, a spinster cousin
who was there and always been

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.380
jealous of Eleanor,
always loved Franklin,

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:22.810
when asked to describe what
had happened in that last day,

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:24.610
as you would do at
a moment of death,

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:26.494
told her that Lucy
had been there.

00:31:26.494 --> 00:31:28.660
And even more, that Lucy
had been at the White House

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:31.090
many times that last year,
and that her daughter

00:31:31.090 --> 00:31:34.160
Anna had been the one to
make those visits possible.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:36.040
I can't even imagine
the dignity that Eleanor

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:38.230
was able to muster within
herself that allowed her

00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:41.650
to go with her husband's
body on the famous train trip

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:44.620
from Warm Springs to Washington
DC, never letting the world

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:47.032
know the hurt she must
have been feeling inside.

00:31:47.032 --> 00:31:49.240
When she got to the White
House, she confronted Anna.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:51.448
All that Anna could say was
I didn't know what to do,

00:31:51.448 --> 00:31:54.544
I loved you both so much and
felt caught in a crossfire.

00:31:54.544 --> 00:31:56.710
And Anna later said her
mother was so angry with her

00:31:56.710 --> 00:31:58.660
that she was sure that
their relationship had

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:00.400
been destroyed forever.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.640
But then, in the summer of
1945, after his death in April,

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:05.700
something happened
in Eleanor's heart,

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:08.170
as she began traveling
the country again.

00:32:08.170 --> 00:32:09.910
For everywhere
she went, people--

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:12.430
porters, taxi drivers,
elevator operators--

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:14.650
told her how much they
loved her husband,

00:32:14.650 --> 00:32:17.740
how much better their lives were
as a result of his leadership.

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:20.380
Blacks shared with her a sense
of the mastery of the jobs

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:22.270
that they had felt, the
pride and the courage

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:23.860
they took in the battlefield.

00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:25.390
Women talked of the
new independence

00:32:25.390 --> 00:32:27.220
they had found during
the war, the pleasure

00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:28.840
and the sociability
of the workplace,

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:30.946
the feeling of accomplishment.

00:32:30.946 --> 00:32:32.320
Though the factories
were already

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:34.930
firing women to make room
for the returning men,

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:37.840
though the daycare centers were
being shut down unceremoniously

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:40.280
as the war was
drawing to a close,

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:42.010
there had been a
permanent shifting away

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:44.211
from the women's consciousness,
a powerful turning

00:32:44.211 --> 00:32:45.460
point in the history of women.

00:32:45.460 --> 00:32:47.950
Work had proved liberating,
a new consciousness

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:50.964
had been formed, and there
would be no going back.

00:32:50.964 --> 00:32:53.380
She talked to veterans who
were going to college on the GI

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:56.350
Bill of Rights, where, despite
overcrowding in housing

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:57.850
and classrooms,
they were determined

00:32:57.850 --> 00:33:00.910
to make the most of their most
extraordinary opportunity.

00:33:00.910 --> 00:33:03.065
She talked to laborers
who took pride in the fact

00:33:03.065 --> 00:33:05.440
that unions were stronger at
the end of the war than ever

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:08.440
before, despite Roosevelt's
partnership with business

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:11.207
that had so worried Eleanor
at the start of the war.

00:33:11.207 --> 00:33:13.540
She'd been fighting him on
so many fronts during the war

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:16.160
that she had lost sight
of the larger picture,

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:18.490
but now she realized that
the war had indeed become

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:20.920
a vehicle for social
reform in more ways

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:22.800
than she ever could
have imagined.

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:25.420
The small-town America where
people clung to their roots,

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:27.700
immobilized within their
ethnic and income class,

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:29.410
had passed into history.

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:31.210
Between the 50
million Americans who

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:33.840
had moved to a different part
of the country to find work

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:36.580
and the 12 million more who
entered the armed forces,

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:38.530
more than 20% of
the population had

00:33:38.530 --> 00:33:41.747
participated in what became
known as the Great Migration.

00:33:41.747 --> 00:33:43.330
And the habit of
mobility, which would

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:45.190
prove both liberating
and fragmenting,

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:46.960
had become ingrained.

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:50.080
No segment of American society
had been left untouched.

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:52.750
More than 17 million new
jobs had been created.

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:55.090
Industrial production
had gone up 100%.

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:56.860
Corporate profits had doubled.

00:33:56.860 --> 00:34:01.460
And the GNP had jumped from
100 billion to 215 billion.

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:04.120
The society of a few haves
and a multitude of have nots

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:05.800
had been transformed.

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:08.739
A redistribution of income had
produced a genuine middle class

00:34:08.739 --> 00:34:10.250
for the first time.

00:34:10.250 --> 00:34:13.179
Moreover, the pent-up demand,
desire matched by money,

00:34:13.179 --> 00:34:16.630
would fuel a postwar boom that
would expand that middle class

00:34:16.630 --> 00:34:17.880
still further.

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:20.530
And the American economy had
not been merely revitalized,

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:21.540
it had been altered.

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:23.350
A revised social
contract had been

00:34:23.350 --> 00:34:25.870
defined between government
and the business community,

00:34:25.870 --> 00:34:27.850
a new partnership like
the new partnership

00:34:27.850 --> 00:34:30.070
between the government
and the universities that

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:33.520
would alter the face of the
country in the decades ahead.

00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:36.310
As Eleanor began to absorb the
extent of all these changes,

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:39.040
she began to feel, she later
said, in a somewhat romantic

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:41.260
image, as if a giant
transference of energy

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:43.510
had taken place, that
at the start of the war

00:34:43.510 --> 00:34:45.940
Roosevelt was strong and
productive and vital,

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:48.550
and the country was weak,
unprepared, and isolationist.

00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:51.166
But by the end, through his
projection of his energy

00:34:51.166 --> 00:34:52.540
onto the country,
the country had

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:55.480
gotten stronger and stronger,
while he was drained of energy

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:57.010
and got weaker and weaker.

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:59.170
And finally, he had
died, but the country

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:02.947
had emerged stronger and more
socially just than ever before.

00:35:02.947 --> 00:35:05.530
As she listened to the emotion
in the voices of all the people

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:08.170
who talked with her about
what he had done for them,

00:35:08.170 --> 00:35:10.570
she was able to
reach within herself

00:35:10.570 --> 00:35:12.280
and to forgive him
fully for resuming

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:15.102
that friendship with Lucy in
the last year of his life.

00:35:15.102 --> 00:35:16.810
And then, just as the
war came to an end,

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:18.310
after six long
years of struggle,

00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:20.560
to go to her daughter
Anna and forgive her,

00:35:20.560 --> 00:35:22.682
as well, affording
a reconciliation

00:35:22.682 --> 00:35:24.640
between mother and daughter
that re-established

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:26.800
a close relationship
between the two women

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:29.600
that lasted for the
rest of their lives.

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:31.930
So in conclusion, as I
look at this complex story

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:34.120
of the American
homefront during the war,

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:37.630
I can only say I feel empathy
for both Franklin and Eleanor.

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:39.130
I'm absolutely
convinced they never

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:40.214
meant to hurt one another.

00:35:40.214 --> 00:35:42.296
They were simply trying
to get through their lives

00:35:42.296 --> 00:35:44.140
with the best possible
mixture of respect

00:35:44.140 --> 00:35:47.920
and affection through
friendship, work, and love.

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:50.440
Sure, it is possible to
look from the outside in,

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:53.680
as the media would immediately
do today, and accuse Roosevelt

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:55.960
of infidelity for resuming
his friendship with Lucy,

00:35:55.960 --> 00:35:58.030
accuse him perhaps
even of harassment

00:35:58.030 --> 00:36:01.390
for his close relationship with
his secretary Missy LeHand,

00:36:01.390 --> 00:36:04.150
and accuse the daughter Anna
of betrayal of her mother.

00:36:04.150 --> 00:36:06.820
But every one of those
labels, in my judgment,

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:08.680
would totally miss
the mark of trying

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:12.090
to understand the lives of
these large individuals.

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:14.910
For in the end, I believe
the real challenge of history

00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:16.980
is to resist the
tendency so prevalent

00:36:16.980 --> 00:36:19.590
today to label, to
stereotype, to expose,

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:22.440
to denigrate, and instead
to bring common sense

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:25.710
and empathy to our subjects
so that the past can truly

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:28.260
come alive, if only
for a few moments,

00:36:28.260 --> 00:36:31.510
in all of its beauty, glory,
sadness, and complexity.

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:33.227
I thank you so very much.

00:36:33.227 --> 00:36:37.203
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:14.990 --> 00:37:18.440
GRAY: Doris, thank you so much
for that wonderfully insightful

00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:22.820
portrait of America and
of American leadership

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:25.550
during the war.

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:28.820
Your comments,
particularly on the way

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:32.240
in which the war and
the leadership provided

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:39.190
by the Roosevelts changed the
social assumptions for blacks

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:41.500
and women, I think
are particularly

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:44.670
important for us to understand.

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:48.870
And I hope that many people,
including many in the media,

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:52.580
listen to your plea at
the end to step away

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:56.680
from the stereotypes
and to express empathy

00:37:56.680 --> 00:37:59.150
and understanding for
those in leadership

00:37:59.150 --> 00:38:00.456
positions in our nation.

00:38:03.240 --> 00:38:08.600
One wonders what the world
of 1995 would be like

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:11.780
had not Franklin Roosevelt
been in the White House

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:16.130
in the 1940s, and had not
Winston Churchill been at 10

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.090
Downing Street.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:22.240
I suspect, Doris,
that, as there was

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:25.820
a run on maps in
February of 1942,

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:30.690
there will soon be a run on No
Ordinary Time in June of 1995.

00:38:30.690 --> 00:38:34.169
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:47.950
I will now introduce the next
four speakers, after which

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:50.740
we will proceed
without interruption

00:38:50.740 --> 00:38:53.320
or without further
words from me.

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:56.950
Because all four are familiar
figures at the Institute,

00:38:56.950 --> 00:39:00.800
I shall be brief in
these introductions.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:02.840
Now, early publicity
for this program,

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:05.290
including particularly the
poster that many of you

00:39:05.290 --> 00:39:09.190
have seen, indicated
that Sheila E. Widnall,

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:12.610
Secretary of the Air
Force and professor

00:39:12.610 --> 00:39:16.690
of Aeronautics and
Astronautics at MIT, on leave,

00:39:16.690 --> 00:39:19.230
would be a participant.

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:23.220
Unfortunately, the
gnomes in the Pentagon

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:26.490
concluded that her appearance
would somehow constitute

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:29.030
a conflict of interest.

00:39:29.030 --> 00:39:33.290
How that can be devised is
beyond me, but in any case,

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:36.760
Sheila had no choice
but to withdraw.

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:40.630
That withdrawal saddened
her fully as much

00:39:40.630 --> 00:39:42.760
as it did all of us
who were involved

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:43.850
in planning this program.

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:51.640
Our next speaker,
Robert C. Seamans Jr.,

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:56.080
has been associated with
MIT for more than 50 years.

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:59.160
He earned two degrees from the
Institute, a master's degree

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:02.880
in aeronautics in
1942 and an [? SCD, ?]

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:08.680
a doctor of science, in
instrumentation in 1951.

00:40:08.680 --> 00:40:12.160
During the next 40 years,
he shuttled back and forth

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:14.680
between Cambridge
and Washington.

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:18.970
I list just some of his
activities in that time.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:24.190
Deputy administrator of
NASA during that key time

00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:28.710
in which NASA was so involved
in the Apollo program.

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:31.020
Hunsaker Professor of
Aeronautics and Astronautics

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:33.210
at MIT.

00:40:33.210 --> 00:40:35.840
Secretary of the Air Force.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:39.610
President of the National
Academy of Engineering.

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:42.490
First administrator of the
Energy Research and Development

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:47.310
Agency, which became later
the Department of Energy.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:50.070
The [? Lutz ?] Professor of
Environment and Public Policy

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:51.930
at MIT.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.670
Dean of Engineering at MIT.

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.216
And now, senior lecturer in
Aeronautics and Astronautics

00:40:58.216 --> 00:41:00.100
here.

00:41:00.100 --> 00:41:05.295
Bob will speak from his
personal experience about MIT

00:41:05.295 --> 00:41:06.170
during the war years.

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:11.990
Now, the speaker
following Bob, who

00:41:11.990 --> 00:41:14.390
will comment on the ways
in which the war influenced

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:17.680
the Institute, deserves
no introduction.

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:20.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:22.014
The third speaker--

00:41:22.014 --> 00:41:24.798
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:28.940
--the third speaker is
Lester C. Thurow, Jerome

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:31.700
and Dorothy Lemelson Professor
of Management and Economics

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:34.640
here, dean of the Sloan
School of Management

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:39.680
from 1987 to 1993, and,
as Lester would put it,

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:42.570
economics educator.

00:41:42.570 --> 00:41:46.170
Following his graduation from
Williams College in 1960,

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:50.160
Lester went to Oxford
as a Rhodes scholar,

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:55.690
and earned his PhD in
economics at Harvard in 1964.

00:41:55.690 --> 00:41:58.480
After serving a term as a
member of the staff of President

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:01.840
Lyndon Johnson's Council
of Economic Advisers,

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:03.400
he taught at Harvard
in economics,

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:08.890
before coming to MIT to
join our faculty in 1968.

00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:11.830
Lester is a prolific
author, and his four books

00:42:11.830 --> 00:42:15.150
have received very
wide attention.

00:42:15.150 --> 00:42:17.490
His most recent
book, Head to Head--

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:20.700
The Coming Economic Battle Among
Japan, Europe, and America,

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:23.850
published in 1992,
describes what

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:26.850
Americans must do to
regain competitiveness

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:30.210
in the world economy.

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:32.996
Lester has been on leave
from MIT this last year,

00:42:32.996 --> 00:42:34.620
and it would surprise
me if there's not

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:37.390
another book in the works.

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:40.140
He writes a regular column
for the Boston Globe.

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:42.870
Lester will speak today
about the economic impact

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:46.830
of the war on American society.

00:42:46.830 --> 00:42:50.430
Our fourth and final
speaker is Charles Vest,

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:53.400
who needs practically no
introduction to this group

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:56.700
of loyal alumni and friends.

00:42:56.700 --> 00:42:58.830
Chuck is a graduate in
mechanical engineering

00:42:58.830 --> 00:43:02.950
from West Virginia University
and the University of Michigan,

00:43:02.950 --> 00:43:05.940
where he served as Dean
of Engineering and later

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:09.920
as Provost and Vice President
for Academic Affairs,

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:16.150
before he was called to come to
Cambridge in the summer of 1990

00:43:16.150 --> 00:43:19.610
to assume the
Institute's presidency.

00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:22.480
Chuck is the 15th president
of the Massachusetts Institute

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:24.140
of Technology.

00:43:24.140 --> 00:43:26.720
He will reflect this morning
on the future of relationships

00:43:26.720 --> 00:43:30.050
between the federal government
and this nation's universities,

00:43:30.050 --> 00:43:35.600
relationships forged largely
out of the cauldron of the war.

00:43:35.600 --> 00:43:40.580
It is now my pleasure to invite
Bob Seamans to the lectern.

00:43:40.580 --> 00:43:44.468
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:53.720 --> 00:43:57.120
- Good morning.

00:43:57.120 --> 00:44:01.380
- While I move the
podium up, I would

00:44:01.380 --> 00:44:08.360
like to say that I'm very
much in debt to the MIT museum

00:44:08.360 --> 00:44:13.160
for going through reams
and reams of cartons

00:44:13.160 --> 00:44:17.370
and notebooks and
loose photographs

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:20.040
in order to come up
with the material

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:24.400
that I'm going to use
here this morning.

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:30.090
I should also note that, when
you look at these slides,

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:32.434
they'll seem rather drab.

00:44:32.434 --> 00:44:33.850
And of course, you
have to realize

00:44:33.850 --> 00:44:41.970
that, back in the early '40s,
Kodachrome was not in wide use.

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:47.160
So now let me see if we
can get a slide here.

00:44:47.160 --> 00:44:48.420
Good.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:52.210
This is MIT prior
to World War II.

00:44:55.800 --> 00:44:57.374
Not too clear the campus.

00:44:57.374 --> 00:44:59.040
You won't be able to
see it too clearly,

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:03.250
so I'm going to take
you on a walk around it.

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:07.980
I will note that MIT
started in Boston,

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:11.130
so I'll proceed across
the Mass Avenue Bridge.

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.860
And as we come across
Memorial Drive,

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:16.500
we'll see the main
building on the right.

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:19.960
We'll see the Graduate
House on the left,

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:24.600
which is now [? Ashdown. ?]
This is followed by the Coop.

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:27.210
And then we come to
Bexley Hall, then

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:31.170
a very nondescript building
that had such places in it

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:33.670
as Walton's, where
you could get somewhat

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:38.850
of a luncheon for $0.82,
and then the Armory.

00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:41.610
Directly across
from the Armory was

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:43.860
the Guggenheim
Aeronautical Laboratory,

00:45:43.860 --> 00:45:49.283
building 33, which actually
had open parking behind it.

00:45:49.283 --> 00:45:51.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:51.450 --> 00:45:57.540
As we stop at the light that's
usually red on Vassar Street,

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:00.060
we note to the left the
Metropolitan Storage,

00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:01.320
and on the--

00:46:01.320 --> 00:46:03.680
almost directly ahead,
but somewhat on the right,

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:06.120
the Whittemore Shoe
Polish Building,

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:11.420
which the Instrumentation Lab
would occupy very shortly.

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:16.760
We head north, we go by lots
and lots of empty space.

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:20.330
And we could note, if
we are history-minded,

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:23.900
that it was here that, in
1915, the first building was

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:26.840
put on this campus.

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:29.030
It was Jerry
Hunsaker's wind tunnel,

00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:33.580
roughly a year before MIT
moved across en masse.

00:46:33.580 --> 00:46:38.370
Beyond the Main Street,
proceed down towards Kendall,

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:40.530
turn the corner onto Ames.

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:48.340
We go by the buildings, the
dormitories on the East Campus,

00:46:48.340 --> 00:46:53.910
and we end up with Walker
Memorial on our right

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:57.090
and the president's
house on the left.

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:01.980
Now, it was in June 1941 that I
had completed my studies here,

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:04.440
except for a thesis.

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:06.945
So one nice, sunny day,
rather than go out sailing,

00:47:06.945 --> 00:47:09.570
I thought I might so go in town
and have a chat with Doc Draper

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:13.190
to see what I might do
in the fall on a thesis

00:47:13.190 --> 00:47:16.290
when he, said would you like
to work on vibration measuring

00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:16.790
equipment?

00:47:16.790 --> 00:47:17.873
And I said that'd be fine.

00:47:17.873 --> 00:47:19.497
Would you like to
get paid for it?

00:47:19.497 --> 00:47:20.330
Well, a new thought.

00:47:20.330 --> 00:47:21.413
I said that'd be terrific.

00:47:21.413 --> 00:47:23.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:26.320
He said, OK, then you've
got to start this week.

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:31.370
So that's when I started
getting paid at MIT.

00:47:31.370 --> 00:47:35.450
I think my first salary
was $1,200 a year.

00:47:35.450 --> 00:47:42.530
The person I was working with
down in the basement of 33,

00:47:42.530 --> 00:47:45.500
right near where
the crash came out,

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:50.087
was named [? Barney ?]
[? Oldfield. ?] And I noticed

00:47:50.087 --> 00:47:52.670
he was coming in less and less,
and then suddenly I heard that

00:47:52.670 --> 00:47:57.940
he'd been in the reserve and he
was going to the Signal Corps.

00:47:57.940 --> 00:47:59.700
This is the end of August.

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:03.704
And at that point,
Doc Draper came around

00:48:03.704 --> 00:48:05.870
and he said, how would you
like to be an instructor?

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:07.760
And I said, well, I'd have
to go up in front of a class.

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:09.218
And he said, well,
you may have to.

00:48:09.218 --> 00:48:11.918
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:14.260 --> 00:48:16.720
But I accepted the challenge.

00:48:16.720 --> 00:48:18.940
And then there was a
custom in those days

00:48:18.940 --> 00:48:20.830
that, at the first
faculty meeting,

00:48:20.830 --> 00:48:23.320
the new faculty and
the new instructors

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:26.500
were introduced by Karl Compton.

00:48:26.500 --> 00:48:28.930
And what I didn't know
was each was supposed

00:48:28.930 --> 00:48:31.300
to stand up and,
in one sentence,

00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:34.240
describe who they were
and what they were doing.

00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:37.160
When it came my turn,
I sort of blurted out,

00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:41.560
I said, I'm nothing but
a Harvard man interested

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:44.280
in vibration equipment.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:48.880
And about two weeks later, I
ran across my father in town,

00:48:48.880 --> 00:48:51.790
and he said, by the way, do
you know that other Harvard man

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:53.440
at MIT who has the jitters?

00:48:53.440 --> 00:48:56.814
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:03.080 --> 00:49:05.210
Now, our world
changed dramatically

00:49:05.210 --> 00:49:07.580
in about two months.

00:49:07.580 --> 00:49:14.510
In December 1941,
President Compton

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:17.810
wasn't using that house
that I've mentioned

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:19.820
as much as he used to.

00:49:19.820 --> 00:49:23.190
He was traveling a
great deal of the time.

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:27.050
And here he is touring the
South Pacific in his role

00:49:27.050 --> 00:49:30.470
as Chief of Field Services
in the Office of Scientific

00:49:30.470 --> 00:49:31.830
Research and Development, OSRD.

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:36.330
OSRD was headed
by Vannevar Bush,

00:49:36.330 --> 00:49:38.450
who was the former dean
of engineering and vice

00:49:38.450 --> 00:49:40.660
president of MIT.

00:49:40.660 --> 00:49:44.720
Van had left MIT before
the war to become

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:47.970
president of the Carnegie
Institution of Washington.

00:49:47.970 --> 00:49:51.170
And it was from his
office there that he

00:49:51.170 --> 00:49:56.330
ran the scientific and technical
effort during World War II,

00:49:56.330 --> 00:49:58.250
reporting directly
to the person we've

00:49:58.250 --> 00:50:02.540
heard so much about and so
eloquently this morning,

00:50:02.540 --> 00:50:06.130
President Roosevelt.

00:50:06.130 --> 00:50:10.240
Compton's presidential
report of October 1945

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:12.930
was written while
in the field, and is

00:50:12.930 --> 00:50:15.940
the basis for much of my talk.

00:50:15.940 --> 00:50:19.180
In the report, he notes the
total participation of MIT

00:50:19.180 --> 00:50:21.370
in World War II.

00:50:21.370 --> 00:50:24.820
30% of the staff took
leaves of absence,

00:50:24.820 --> 00:50:30.680
and 25%, a quarter of all alumni
from MIT were in the military,

00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:35.160
including 92 generals
and 35 admirals.

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:38.730
On campus, there were many
special educational courses

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:42.810
and 400 research and
development contracts.

00:50:42.810 --> 00:50:46.530
MIT was reimbursed five million
by the government for education

00:50:46.530 --> 00:50:50.960
and training, and 93 million for
the research and development.

00:50:50.960 --> 00:50:52.635
And that was in '45 dollars.

00:50:57.260 --> 00:51:01.400
Now, there were many
course completion photos

00:51:01.400 --> 00:51:03.390
similar to this one
of postgraduates

00:51:03.390 --> 00:51:07.310
of naval construction
and engineering.

00:51:07.310 --> 00:51:11.100
Captain Joyce was head
of the military training

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:14.810
here at MIT at that time,
and Professor Manning

00:51:14.810 --> 00:51:18.570
of the naval architecture
in the foreground.

00:51:18.570 --> 00:51:23.720
I myself ended up with 13 such
photos, each time with Captain

00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:28.850
Joyce and 50 V12 officers who
had taken my course in aircraft

00:51:28.850 --> 00:51:30.622
instrumentation.

00:51:30.622 --> 00:51:33.020
These are six-week courses
that ended on Friday.

00:51:33.020 --> 00:51:35.350
The following Monday, the
next group would come in.

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:37.490
And so it went for 13 times.

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:44.940
Now, the scope of what went
on at MIT and in education

00:51:44.940 --> 00:51:47.260
is really hard to get across.

00:51:47.260 --> 00:51:50.080
This is sort of an
attempt, a photograph taken

00:51:50.080 --> 00:51:53.350
from inside MIT looking
out on the Killian Court.

00:51:53.350 --> 00:51:57.939
And you can see large numbers,
in this case of enlisted men

00:51:57.939 --> 00:51:59.230
who went through training here.

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:06.270
All told, there were 2,300
in the Navy V12 and Army

00:52:06.270 --> 00:52:08.760
specialized training
groups, there

00:52:08.760 --> 00:52:13.740
were 7,275 in the Army and Navy
who attended the radar school

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:17.460
down Atlantic Avenue, and
there were nearly 1,000

00:52:17.460 --> 00:52:20.100
in meteorology and
weather forecasting.

00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:28.600
Now, MIT, of course, was
more than the classrooms.

00:52:28.600 --> 00:52:33.730
Interest in aeronautics expanded
during the '20s and '30s,

00:52:33.730 --> 00:52:37.930
with such notables as Donald
Douglas and Jimmy Doolittle

00:52:37.930 --> 00:52:40.900
receiving their
doctor's degrees.

00:52:40.900 --> 00:52:44.620
Dr. [? Hunsaker ?] returned
to MIT in the mid '30s as head

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:48.310
of mechanical engineering
and of the new aeronautical

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:51.160
engineering department.

00:52:51.160 --> 00:52:53.980
He had much to do, obviously,
with the construction

00:52:53.980 --> 00:52:56.770
of this much larger wind
tunnel than he had first

00:52:56.770 --> 00:52:59.180
built on the campus.

00:52:59.180 --> 00:53:04.340
This slide is from the
roof of the Aero building

00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:08.030
facing towards
MIT's main building.

00:53:08.030 --> 00:53:10.630
And the design of this wind
tunnel and so on came out

00:53:10.630 --> 00:53:15.830
of the leadership of
Professor Joe Bicknell.

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:19.780
The tunnel was put
in operation in 1939.

00:53:19.780 --> 00:53:23.060
The 400 government contracts
I mentioned earlier

00:53:23.060 --> 00:53:26.930
did not include the
275 orders specifically

00:53:26.930 --> 00:53:29.550
for wind tunnel work.

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.100
The tunnel operated
on two shifts

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:35.250
throughout the war,
seven days a week,

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:38.940
testing many of the aircraft
designs used in World War II

00:53:38.940 --> 00:53:45.160
and weeding out many that
should not have been built.

00:53:45.160 --> 00:53:50.260
Now, while at MIT, Vannevar
Bush did a lot of things.

00:53:50.260 --> 00:53:54.670
Here he is second from
the left in this slide.

00:53:54.670 --> 00:53:59.230
Among other things, he developed
the first differential analyzer

00:53:59.230 --> 00:54:02.880
used to solve
differential equations.

00:54:02.880 --> 00:54:05.140
It was made from
gears and shafts

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:06.840
and mechanical integrators.

00:54:06.840 --> 00:54:10.990
It was called the
Great Brass Brain.

00:54:10.990 --> 00:54:14.440
He's leaning on the main
console of the second analyzer

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:16.580
of his design.

00:54:16.580 --> 00:54:18.430
In this design, the
gears and shafts

00:54:18.430 --> 00:54:22.500
were replaced by
amplifiers and servos.

00:54:22.500 --> 00:54:24.810
This machine, called the
Rockefeller Differential

00:54:24.810 --> 00:54:29.520
Analyzer, was used to compute
range tables and tenor

00:54:29.520 --> 00:54:35.110
patterns, and many designed
equations and so on.

00:54:35.110 --> 00:54:38.610
For example, for Doc Draper's
fire control systems.

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:40.860
The photo also includes
Professor [? Phillips, ?] head

00:54:40.860 --> 00:54:44.010
of math, on the left, Professor
[? Hazen, ?] head of electrical

00:54:44.010 --> 00:54:47.430
engineering, and then Professor
Caldwell on the right,

00:54:47.430 --> 00:54:49.950
head of the Center of Analysis.

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:52.380
Not shown is the person you
worked with when you used

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:56.670
the machine, named
Frank [? Rizzu. ?]

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:58.690
You'd go in the room and
it would be operating,

00:54:58.690 --> 00:55:00.106
and there are
typewriters-- that's

00:55:00.106 --> 00:55:03.080
how you got the data out--
clacking away, clickety click.

00:55:03.080 --> 00:55:10.670
And all of a sudden, you'd find
that the machine had stopped.

00:55:10.670 --> 00:55:13.940
Frank would meet us all over
the place, consoles everywhere.

00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:16.400
He'd go over to one machine,
look at it carefully,

00:55:16.400 --> 00:55:19.240
kick the bottom panel, and away
the machine would go again.

00:55:19.240 --> 00:55:21.216
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:29.614 --> 00:55:34.145
Now, I seem to
have slid a slide.

00:55:36.840 --> 00:55:40.440
I wish that time permitted
a full discussion

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:42.900
of all the special facilities
pressed into service

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:45.180
during World War II.

00:55:45.180 --> 00:55:46.800
Professor Evans,
[? Rob Lee ?] Evans,

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:50.130
cyclotron was used to produce
radioactive tracers from

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:53.370
medicine and metallurgy
as a means for preserving

00:55:53.370 --> 00:55:55.250
[? whole ?] blood.

00:55:55.250 --> 00:55:57.090
In the Laboratory
for Insulation,

00:55:57.090 --> 00:56:00.270
Dr. von Hippel developed
insulating and dielectric

00:56:00.270 --> 00:56:05.130
materials for operation in
high-frequency electric fields.

00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:07.990
In 1940, MIT had plans
for a new building

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:11.896
for the chemical engineering
department, but no funds.

00:56:11.896 --> 00:56:13.270
Under an agreement
with the Army,

00:56:13.270 --> 00:56:16.180
the building was constructed
and used for chemical warfare

00:56:16.180 --> 00:56:20.110
services during the war in the
leadership of such individuals

00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:23.080
as Professor Whitman
and Gilliland.

00:56:23.080 --> 00:56:26.590
Deadly devices, such as
flamethrowers and incendiaries,

00:56:26.590 --> 00:56:29.860
were tested here, as well
as production processes

00:56:29.860 --> 00:56:32.470
for phosgene and mustard gas.

00:56:32.470 --> 00:56:34.840
Such work was done in a
specially protected area

00:56:34.840 --> 00:56:39.015
inside the building, with some
of the space well below ground.

00:56:41.580 --> 00:56:44.950
Now, shortly after the
war, much of MIT's effort

00:56:44.950 --> 00:56:47.320
was revealed to the public.

00:56:47.320 --> 00:56:50.020
Here's a flamethrower
operating on MIT's campus

00:56:50.020 --> 00:56:52.690
behind the Smith House,
one of the eateries

00:56:52.690 --> 00:56:55.510
we had on Memorial Drive.

00:56:55.510 --> 00:56:57.460
I'm told extra oil
was added to the fuel

00:56:57.460 --> 00:57:01.330
to provide a little more smoke
for demonstration purposes.

00:57:01.330 --> 00:57:04.750
I'll make no comment on
whether an environmental impact

00:57:04.750 --> 00:57:09.090
statement would have allowed
this to have taken place today.

00:57:12.990 --> 00:57:16.530
The Radiation Lab at MIT
was the national center

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:18.570
for work on radar and
other devices that

00:57:18.570 --> 00:57:21.660
relied on electromagnetic
wave propagation.

00:57:21.660 --> 00:57:26.130
There were similar centers
for other such areas

00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:29.010
at other universities
throughout the country.

00:57:29.010 --> 00:57:32.910
The lab was started in
1940, well before the war.

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:37.800
At its peak, it had 1,200 staff
and 2,700 support personnel,

00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:40.800
and occupied 15
acres of floor space

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:45.700
on campus, and many outlying
facilities here and abroad.

00:57:45.700 --> 00:57:47.910
Sir Robert Watson-Watt
was the inventor

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:50.700
of the magnetron, the
key element, of course,

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:51.930
in radar systems.

00:57:51.930 --> 00:57:55.470
There are two such
magnetrons, shown

00:57:55.470 --> 00:58:00.672
in this slide, one held
by Sir Henry [? Tizard, ?]

00:58:00.672 --> 00:58:02.130
who headed the
British mission that

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:05.370
brought the secret of the
magnetron and the magnetron

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:08.560
to this country in 1940.

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:12.180
He's shown here at MIT's
mid-century convocation,

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:14.040
with [? Leto ?]
[? Bridge ?] on the right,

00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:18.130
who directed the laboratory, and
Alfred [? Loomis, ?] chairman

00:58:18.130 --> 00:58:23.040
of the OSRD-affiliated microwave
committee that had government

00:58:23.040 --> 00:58:24.930
oversight of the
laboratory's program.

00:58:27.535 --> 00:58:29.160
Now, I'd like to
first just run quickly

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:33.600
through the process that took
place in this laboratory.

00:58:33.600 --> 00:58:39.050
It had to start with ideas, with
analyses and design studies.

00:58:39.050 --> 00:58:42.720
And next, as you can
see in this slide,

00:58:42.720 --> 00:58:44.880
you had to build up breadboards.

00:58:44.880 --> 00:58:48.120
And these are being inspected
by two very key players,

00:58:48.120 --> 00:58:52.110
Professors
[? Zacharias ?] and Hill.

00:58:52.110 --> 00:58:54.660
Next, you had to
run tests to see

00:58:54.660 --> 00:58:56.160
how the equipment is working.

00:58:56.160 --> 00:58:58.500
And when I ran
across this slide,

00:58:58.500 --> 00:58:59.710
I thought it was terrific.

00:58:59.710 --> 00:59:03.480
I've never seen such a kludge
of equipment in my life.

00:59:03.480 --> 00:59:06.900
It's appended to the side
of one of the radiation lab

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:09.000
temporary buildings.

00:59:09.000 --> 00:59:11.697
And building 20, which
you can see if you

00:59:11.697 --> 00:59:12.780
want to take a look at it.

00:59:12.780 --> 00:59:14.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:17.680
Now, this has a big
pendulum underneath,

00:59:17.680 --> 00:59:21.960
which could be swung around,
and the stabilization device

00:59:21.960 --> 00:59:23.490
was riding on it.

00:59:23.490 --> 00:59:24.930
And with the mirrors
up above, you

00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:29.750
could tell whether stabilization
was taking place or not.

00:59:29.750 --> 00:59:33.410
The laboratory had a
plethora of radomes

00:59:33.410 --> 00:59:36.020
protruding from many rooftops.

00:59:36.020 --> 00:59:40.430
Properly designed, they were
transparent to the microwave

00:59:40.430 --> 00:59:46.100
equipment inside, and so it was
possible to test the equipment

00:59:46.100 --> 00:59:50.870
with signals coming
from either the ground

00:59:50.870 --> 00:59:54.830
or from overhead flights.

00:59:54.830 --> 00:59:57.000
Now, the laboratory's
output could

00:59:57.000 --> 01:00:01.380
be in the form of reports,
design drawings, trained

01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:05.520
personnel, or hardware
delivered to test sites

01:00:05.520 --> 01:00:08.160
or often to the front lines.

01:00:08.160 --> 01:00:11.820
And so I thought this was
a pretty revealing slide.

01:00:11.820 --> 01:00:14.460
It shows actually
eight trucks loaded

01:00:14.460 --> 01:00:18.300
to the gills ready to
take equipment, possibly

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:21.510
out to Bedford, possibly other
places in the United States.

01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:23.370
Maybe over to be shipped--

01:00:23.370 --> 01:00:25.440
maybe over to the harbor
to be shipped overseas.

01:00:28.255 --> 01:00:39.110
Now, here you see a van, on
top of which is Lee Davenport

01:00:39.110 --> 01:00:41.900
and [? Evon ?] [? Getting ?]
with the project officer,

01:00:41.900 --> 01:00:43.800
Colonel Warner.

01:00:43.800 --> 01:00:48.620
They're on top of a
van for the SCR-584.

01:00:48.620 --> 01:00:53.690
This set was produced by GE,
Chrysler, and Westinghouse.

01:00:53.690 --> 01:00:57.320
It was used for tracking,
for fire control

01:00:57.320 --> 01:01:01.610
of incoming targets,
incoming enemy targets.

01:01:01.610 --> 01:01:07.910
The dollar value of the
SCR-584 production I'm told

01:01:07.910 --> 01:01:10.790
exceeded the cost
of Boulder dam.

01:01:10.790 --> 01:01:13.856
It was used on many
theaters of the war.

01:01:17.800 --> 01:01:22.900
The antenna of the microwave
early warning radar, MEW,

01:01:22.900 --> 01:01:27.300
is shown here with
B-24s overhead.

01:01:27.300 --> 01:01:33.430
Five sets were built by MIT
prior to industrial production.

01:01:33.430 --> 01:01:36.190
Several days after
the first installation

01:01:36.190 --> 01:01:39.310
in southern England,
American heavy bombers

01:01:39.310 --> 01:01:40.930
were somewhat off course.

01:01:40.930 --> 01:01:45.230
They were detected 250 miles
to the south of England.

01:01:45.230 --> 01:01:47.590
But they were detected
with this equipment,

01:01:47.590 --> 01:01:54.740
and were brought back to
their proper bases in England.

01:01:54.740 --> 01:01:56.230
Now, to make use
of this equipment,

01:01:56.230 --> 01:02:00.490
there had to be some place
where the information could

01:02:00.490 --> 01:02:03.370
be received, could be
analyzed, and could be

01:02:03.370 --> 01:02:06.080
dispatched where it was needed.

01:02:06.080 --> 01:02:08.890
This happens to be the
Eighth Air Force MEW control

01:02:08.890 --> 01:02:12.730
room, where enemy aircraft
could be detected and tracked

01:02:12.730 --> 01:02:15.940
by observing the radar
scopes, and then, as required,

01:02:15.940 --> 01:02:20.080
plotted up on maps and
used by the controllers

01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:23.940
to vector friendly aircraft
into position for attack.

01:02:28.940 --> 01:02:30.790
This is Loran ground station.

01:02:30.790 --> 01:02:33.730
They often had to be placed
in remote, unfriendly places,

01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:35.546
such as this one.

01:02:35.546 --> 01:02:40.020
A navigation fix was obtained by
receiving simultaneous signals

01:02:40.020 --> 01:02:45.540
from three of these, one master
and two slaves [INAUDIBLE]

01:02:45.540 --> 01:02:48.990
appropriately to such places
as Greenland and Iceland

01:02:48.990 --> 01:02:50.670
and so on.

01:02:50.670 --> 01:02:54.980
The system proved useful for
both aircraft and ships at sea.

01:02:54.980 --> 01:02:57.570
The Navy's convoys
found this system

01:02:57.570 --> 01:03:00.660
particularly advantageous
when zigzagging

01:03:00.660 --> 01:03:03.200
on the northern routes
in adverse weather

01:03:03.200 --> 01:03:05.156
with submarines in the vicinity.

01:03:07.970 --> 01:03:13.670
Luis Alvarez headed
the radiation lab team

01:03:13.670 --> 01:03:17.240
that developed the ground
control approach, GCA,

01:03:17.240 --> 01:03:19.760
for landing aircraft in fog
and haze, of which there

01:03:19.760 --> 01:03:22.320
was plenty in England.

01:03:22.320 --> 01:03:25.140
The ground controller could
see the aircraft's deviation

01:03:25.140 --> 01:03:29.260
from the approach path, and
could so advise the pilot.

01:03:29.260 --> 01:03:33.670
He could talk him in
to a blind landing.

01:03:33.670 --> 01:03:37.480
This slide shows
a B-17 located out

01:03:37.480 --> 01:03:43.780
at the Bedford airport, part
of the radiation laboratory's

01:03:43.780 --> 01:03:46.180
installation there.

01:03:46.180 --> 01:03:48.640
You can see the
radome coming down

01:03:48.640 --> 01:03:55.570
under the nose of the B-17,
just aft of the gunner's turret.

01:03:55.570 --> 01:03:58.000
This so-called H2X
navigation system

01:03:58.000 --> 01:04:02.020
that was installed there was
coupled with a Norden bombsight

01:04:02.020 --> 01:04:05.020
permitted bombing
through the overcast.

01:04:05.020 --> 01:04:07.690
The first 12 installations
were built and installed

01:04:07.690 --> 01:04:11.830
by radiation lab personnel
on B-17s like this one

01:04:11.830 --> 01:04:14.340
here at the Bedford airport.

01:04:14.340 --> 01:04:17.120
General Eaker sent 12 of
his most experienced crews

01:04:17.120 --> 01:04:22.020
to MIT to train and
test this equipment.

01:04:22.020 --> 01:04:23.730
They then flew the
aircraft to England

01:04:23.730 --> 01:04:27.840
to serve as pathfinders
on all major raids

01:04:27.840 --> 01:04:38.050
into Germany from November
8, 1943 to March 15, 1944.

01:04:38.050 --> 01:04:39.850
During World War
II, Doc Draper was

01:04:39.850 --> 01:04:41.950
director of the
instrumentation lab,

01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:44.950
and later became head
of the Aero Department.

01:04:44.950 --> 01:04:49.030
The key to the fire control
system was not the magnetron,

01:04:49.030 --> 01:04:51.010
it was the
single-degree-of-freedom gyro

01:04:51.010 --> 01:04:53.850
that he's holding in his hand.

01:04:53.850 --> 01:04:56.490
The first breadboard
fire control system

01:04:56.490 --> 01:05:00.870
was built for the British,
prior to America's entry

01:05:00.870 --> 01:05:03.290
into World War II.

01:05:03.290 --> 01:05:08.200
With our declaration of war, all
property of the United States

01:05:08.200 --> 01:05:13.520
became part and parcel
of our own military.

01:05:13.520 --> 01:05:17.120
And they took over this
piece of equipment,

01:05:17.120 --> 01:05:18.370
which was put in a black box.

01:05:18.370 --> 01:05:21.340
It was called the shoe box.

01:05:21.340 --> 01:05:23.350
Our own Navy wanted to
see how it operated.

01:05:23.350 --> 01:05:26.230
It was out at the
Watertown Arsenal.

01:05:26.230 --> 01:05:29.560
And the night before it
was to be demonstrated,

01:05:29.560 --> 01:05:32.140
somebody made a mistake and
put the wrong damping fluid

01:05:32.140 --> 01:05:37.990
in the gyro, so as the
target, which was on a cable,

01:05:37.990 --> 01:05:40.620
went across one
side of the arsenal,

01:05:40.620 --> 01:05:42.730
and the 20-caliber
started firing

01:05:42.730 --> 01:05:45.010
with somebody looking
through the site,

01:05:45.010 --> 01:05:48.670
the Navy was very impressed
that all the bullets

01:05:48.670 --> 01:05:50.280
seemed to hit the target.

01:05:50.280 --> 01:05:52.030
What they didn't find
out until much later

01:05:52.030 --> 01:05:55.330
was that the person who
was doing the firing

01:05:55.330 --> 01:05:58.520
had done it enough beforehand so
he didn't have to use the site.

01:05:58.520 --> 01:06:00.422
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:00.422 --> 01:06:01.380
It was non-operational.

01:06:01.380 --> 01:06:03.501
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:05.740 --> 01:06:09.070
This design evolved into
the Mark 14 Gunsight,

01:06:09.070 --> 01:06:11.830
which was mounted directly on
50-caliber and 20-millimeter

01:06:11.830 --> 01:06:13.420
guns.

01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:16.780
50,000 were produced by the
Sperry Gyroscope Company

01:06:16.780 --> 01:06:19.690
and its associates, including
[? Dole ?] [? Cam, ?] a local

01:06:19.690 --> 01:06:22.786
company that evolved from a
machine shop during the war.

01:06:27.620 --> 01:06:31.180
The gunsights were updated
as the enemy became

01:06:31.180 --> 01:06:33.520
more proficient and skillful.

01:06:33.520 --> 01:06:36.460
Radar ranging was added
first, and then the gunsight

01:06:36.460 --> 01:06:40.300
was redesigned to include
a six-power telescope.

01:06:40.300 --> 01:06:43.010
This new sight, the Mark
15, is shown in this slide.

01:06:43.010 --> 01:06:45.040
It's on the roof of the
Whittemore Shoe Polish

01:06:45.040 --> 01:06:49.560
Building, looking
across at the warehouse.

01:06:49.560 --> 01:06:52.960
The sight is off-mounted away
from the gun on a hand control

01:06:52.960 --> 01:06:55.630
pedestal.

01:06:55.630 --> 01:06:58.170
Better to aim the sight.

01:06:58.170 --> 01:06:59.820
The gun mounts move
with a pedestal

01:06:59.820 --> 01:07:02.190
using drives engineered
in part by Gordon

01:07:02.190 --> 01:07:05.526
Brown and his
servomechanism laboratory.

01:07:05.526 --> 01:07:07.150
This [? director ?]
system was used

01:07:07.150 --> 01:07:12.580
with 20 and 40-millimeter
quads and three-inch 70 guns.

01:07:12.580 --> 01:07:14.230
Kamikazes particularly
learned how

01:07:14.230 --> 01:07:18.010
to spook the system by attacking
with the sun behind them.

01:07:18.010 --> 01:07:20.890
Late in the war, radar signals
were introduced into the sight

01:07:20.890 --> 01:07:24.090
so the firing could take place
without visual tracking, when

01:07:24.090 --> 01:07:26.380
the target could
not be observed,

01:07:26.380 --> 01:07:29.500
either because of the sun or
because of smoke from gunfire.

01:07:33.220 --> 01:07:37.100
Unfortunately, time
doesn't permit my

01:07:37.100 --> 01:07:41.060
doing justice to all of the
research and development

01:07:41.060 --> 01:07:44.570
underway at MIT
during World War II.

01:07:44.570 --> 01:07:46.610
Aircraft propulsion was
the province of Eddie

01:07:46.610 --> 01:07:48.830
and [? Faye ?] Taylor,
who specialized

01:07:48.830 --> 01:07:52.450
on liquid and air-cooled
internal combustion engine.

01:07:52.450 --> 01:07:55.330
Work was conducted at MIT on
hiding important facilities

01:07:55.330 --> 01:07:57.970
by camouflage, as well
as ferreting them out

01:07:57.970 --> 01:08:00.910
at night with airborne
flash photography, pioneered

01:08:00.910 --> 01:08:03.350
by Doc Edgerton.

01:08:03.350 --> 01:08:05.090
Food processing
and packaging was

01:08:05.090 --> 01:08:07.441
important to all of services.

01:08:07.441 --> 01:08:09.690
[? Bernie ?] [? Proctor ?]
and [? John ?] [? Sluter ?]

01:08:09.690 --> 01:08:13.010
developed pre-cooked frozen
foods that could be thawed out

01:08:13.010 --> 01:08:17.420
during a long flight in a B-17.

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:19.340
Professor Van de Graaff
developed and built

01:08:19.340 --> 01:08:23.000
five high-voltage generators
for the Navy used for inspection

01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:25.810
of castings and munitions.

01:08:25.810 --> 01:08:27.939
Other areas of activity
listed on this chart

01:08:27.939 --> 01:08:31.689
include work on metal
extraction, new alloys,

01:08:31.689 --> 01:08:35.229
surgical sutures,
synthetic rubber, vitamins,

01:08:35.229 --> 01:08:38.300
and long-range
weather forecasting.

01:08:38.300 --> 01:08:39.779
It was also Professor
[? Holland ?]

01:08:39.779 --> 01:08:43.050
[? Williams' ?] work on fuels
and combustion and Professor

01:08:43.050 --> 01:08:46.210
[? Keyes ?] and [? Collins ?] on
liquid oxygen, its production,

01:08:46.210 --> 01:08:49.520
transportation, and storage
on submarines and aircraft

01:08:49.520 --> 01:08:53.310
and for use in welding
and in hospitals.

01:08:53.310 --> 01:08:57.155
It's important to note
that MIT was not working

01:08:57.155 --> 01:08:59.910
in any way on the atomic bomb.

01:08:59.910 --> 01:09:03.850
That is not listed
on this chart.

01:09:03.850 --> 01:09:08.890
As President Compton
stated in his 1945 report,

01:09:08.890 --> 01:09:13.420
the great commitment to radar
obviated the opportunity

01:09:13.420 --> 01:09:17.970
to participate in the most
portentous scientific program

01:09:17.970 --> 01:09:18.595
in all history.

01:09:21.100 --> 01:09:26.620
This final slide shows
MIT post World War II.

01:09:26.620 --> 01:09:28.930
The open area along
Vassar and Main Street

01:09:28.930 --> 01:09:32.229
has been filled with temporary
buildings used by the radiation

01:09:32.229 --> 01:09:35.019
lab during World War II.

01:09:35.019 --> 01:09:37.060
After the war, they were
used to great advantage,

01:09:37.060 --> 01:09:40.810
as the MIT student body
dramatically expanded.

01:09:40.810 --> 01:09:44.770
Many of these students were
supported by the GI Bill.

01:09:44.770 --> 01:09:47.020
Temporary housing was
added on the West Campus,

01:09:47.020 --> 01:09:49.689
as you can see, to
accommodate the large number

01:09:49.689 --> 01:09:51.550
of married students,
many with children.

01:09:54.350 --> 01:09:56.000
I hope I've made it
clear to all of you

01:09:56.000 --> 01:09:59.180
that MIT contributed
to America's war effort

01:09:59.180 --> 01:10:02.900
in many, many ways, in
many, many disciplines.

01:10:02.900 --> 01:10:05.450
Let me conclude by quoting
from Karl Compton's

01:10:05.450 --> 01:10:07.286
presidential report.

01:10:07.286 --> 01:10:09.620
"The performance of the
Massachusetts Institute

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:14.420
of Technology through our
alumni, staff, and organization

01:10:14.420 --> 01:10:17.090
has demonstrated more
vividly than ever

01:10:17.090 --> 01:10:20.330
before the essential
soundness of her conception,

01:10:20.330 --> 01:10:24.410
the public value of her
work, and the justification

01:10:24.410 --> 01:10:27.320
for her continued
endeavor to pioneer

01:10:27.320 --> 01:10:31.130
in the oncoming lines of
technological progress.

01:10:31.130 --> 01:10:34.820
It is in times of stress
that strength is proved,

01:10:34.820 --> 01:10:39.230
but God grant that our future
demonstrations of strength

01:10:39.230 --> 01:10:42.020
may be made with full
effect under the stress

01:10:42.020 --> 01:10:45.230
of a strong urge to be
useful in peace, and never

01:10:45.230 --> 01:10:48.880
again under the dread
compulsion of war."

01:10:48.880 --> 01:10:49.860
And he--

01:10:49.860 --> 01:10:53.290
[APPLAUSE]

01:10:58.700 --> 01:11:01.220
--and now, as I
try to figure out

01:11:01.220 --> 01:11:05.060
how to lower the podium
for our next speaker,

01:11:05.060 --> 01:11:08.840
because he may have a
little difficulty looking

01:11:08.840 --> 01:11:12.220
at his notes, here it goes down.

01:11:12.220 --> 01:11:15.110
The next speaker would
not introduce himself.

01:11:15.110 --> 01:11:17.310
I'll tell you who it is.

01:11:17.310 --> 01:11:19.130
It's Dr. Paul Gray.

01:11:19.130 --> 01:11:23.886
And he's going to tell us about
MIT's response to World War II.

01:11:23.886 --> 01:11:24.386
Paul?

01:11:24.386 --> 01:11:28.814
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:46.070 --> 01:11:47.840
GRAY: Thank you very much, Bob.

01:11:47.840 --> 01:11:50.490
This is approximately the
halfway point in the program,

01:11:50.490 --> 01:11:53.870
so before I begin,
let me invite everyone

01:11:53.870 --> 01:11:56.700
to stand in place for about
a one-minute stretch, OK?

01:11:56.700 --> 01:11:59.532
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:12:01.900 --> 01:12:05.190
GRAY: Thank you very much.

01:12:05.190 --> 01:12:07.710
Before I begin my
remarks, I would

01:12:07.710 --> 01:12:11.130
like to express my appreciation
to three people who

01:12:11.130 --> 01:12:15.150
helped enormously in putting
this material together.

01:12:15.150 --> 01:12:18.480
First is Helen Samuels,
the Institute archivist

01:12:18.480 --> 01:12:21.060
who guided me in a search
through for materials

01:12:21.060 --> 01:12:22.950
in the archives.

01:12:22.950 --> 01:12:26.910
Second is Michael Yates
of the MIT Museum, who

01:12:26.910 --> 01:12:30.240
helped in providing
me with some slides,

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:32.640
some photographs I
will use this morning.

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:36.900
And third is Lydia Snover of
the MIT Planning Office, who

01:12:36.900 --> 01:12:39.300
assembled some of
the data I will show,

01:12:39.300 --> 01:12:41.344
and also prepared
those illustrations.

01:12:41.344 --> 01:12:42.760
I'm indebted to
all three of them.

01:12:45.870 --> 01:12:51.050
The 134-year history of
the Massachusetts Institute

01:12:51.050 --> 01:12:54.530
of Technology is
marked, I believe,

01:12:54.530 --> 01:12:59.200
by three discontinuities,
transformations

01:12:59.200 --> 01:13:04.300
which, in scale or
character or import,

01:13:04.300 --> 01:13:06.280
made all the
difference in shaping

01:13:06.280 --> 01:13:09.040
the future of this place.

01:13:09.040 --> 01:13:14.560
First, the move in 1916 from
Copley Square to Cambridge

01:13:14.560 --> 01:13:18.430
liberated the Institute,
then known as Boston Tech,

01:13:18.430 --> 01:13:20.830
from its constrained
physical environment,

01:13:20.830 --> 01:13:25.700
and permitted expansion of its
programs and its facilities.

01:13:25.700 --> 01:13:29.150
That move, made possible by
the extraordinary support

01:13:29.150 --> 01:13:32.090
of a very small band
of loyal alumni,

01:13:32.090 --> 01:13:34.970
and by the generosity
of George Eastman,

01:13:34.970 --> 01:13:39.230
also marked the first steps
in MIT's transformation

01:13:39.230 --> 01:13:44.930
from a commuter college to
a residential environment.

01:13:44.930 --> 01:13:49.720
Second, the appointment in
1930 of Karl Taylor Compton

01:13:49.720 --> 01:13:53.290
as president began the
transformation of the Institute

01:13:53.290 --> 01:13:55.630
from an undergraduate
college focused

01:13:55.630 --> 01:13:59.230
on engineering,
architecture, and technology

01:13:59.230 --> 01:14:03.250
to a science-based university
by strengthening greatly

01:14:03.250 --> 01:14:05.150
the sciences.

01:14:05.150 --> 01:14:07.700
KT knew that the
future greatness of MIT

01:14:07.700 --> 01:14:10.370
depended on developing
first-rate programs

01:14:10.370 --> 01:14:14.696
of education and research in
science and applied science.

01:14:14.696 --> 01:14:19.190
And he acted on that conviction.

01:14:19.190 --> 01:14:21.450
The third discontinuity
is, of course,

01:14:21.450 --> 01:14:24.110
the one on which we focus
today, the Second World

01:14:24.110 --> 01:14:29.110
War and its effects
on the Institute.

01:14:29.110 --> 01:14:32.140
The war-driven
transformation of MIT,

01:14:32.140 --> 01:14:34.990
and of essentially all
American universities,

01:14:34.990 --> 01:14:38.950
began early in the 1940s
with the mobilization

01:14:38.950 --> 01:14:41.200
of scientific and
engineering talent

01:14:41.200 --> 01:14:43.270
in the interest of
national defense,

01:14:43.270 --> 01:14:47.520
as Bob Seamans has
just described.

01:14:47.520 --> 01:14:50.750
It was appropriate that
Bob gave so much attention

01:14:50.750 --> 01:14:52.760
to the radiation
laboratory, which in a way

01:14:52.760 --> 01:14:57.080
was the largest, most
prominent, but certainly not

01:14:57.080 --> 01:14:59.790
the only activity of that kind.

01:14:59.790 --> 01:15:03.450
The radiation
laboratory developed

01:15:03.450 --> 01:15:08.980
in so many forms radar as an
important tool of warfare.

01:15:08.980 --> 01:15:12.120
It was said after the war
by I.I. Rabi, I believe,

01:15:12.120 --> 01:15:15.240
that, while the atomic bomb
may have ended the war,

01:15:15.240 --> 01:15:18.590
radar won it.

01:15:18.590 --> 01:15:20.060
While the radiation
laboratory was

01:15:20.060 --> 01:15:24.440
the largest and most prominent
war-related activity here,

01:15:24.440 --> 01:15:26.780
engaging nearly
4,000 individuals

01:15:26.780 --> 01:15:32.260
at its peak in 1945, it was
not the only such effort.

01:15:32.260 --> 01:15:34.660
As Bob has shown, work
in support of the war

01:15:34.660 --> 01:15:38.230
took place across the
Institute, involving almost

01:15:38.230 --> 01:15:41.400
all the academic elements.

01:15:41.400 --> 01:15:44.190
These activities, running
under forced draft

01:15:44.190 --> 01:15:47.460
in an atmosphere of crisis
and deep commitment,

01:15:47.460 --> 01:15:52.340
changed MIT fundamentally
and irreversibly.

01:15:52.340 --> 01:15:54.400
Karl Taylor Compton
put it this way

01:15:54.400 --> 01:15:56.810
in the 1945 report
of the president.

01:15:56.810 --> 01:16:01.920
And I quote, "the term
epoch is not customarily

01:16:01.920 --> 01:16:06.090
used to designate so short
a period as five years,

01:16:06.090 --> 01:16:11.340
yet in many ways the term is
appropriate to these war years.

01:16:11.340 --> 01:16:13.350
In these five
years, the Institute

01:16:13.350 --> 01:16:17.100
spent on its war
contracts as much money

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:19.740
as it had spent on
its normal activities

01:16:19.740 --> 01:16:23.570
during the previous
80 years of existence.

01:16:23.570 --> 01:16:25.820
This is a sobering thought.

01:16:25.820 --> 01:16:28.430
It makes one wonder what
tremendous things could

01:16:28.430 --> 01:16:33.230
be accomplished in peacetime if
the same energy, determination,

01:16:33.230 --> 01:16:38.029
and resources were marshaled
to fashion a better world."

01:16:38.029 --> 01:16:38.570
End of quote.

01:16:41.110 --> 01:16:44.020
Now, you understand that I
had no personal experience

01:16:44.020 --> 01:16:47.060
with the World War
II years at Tech.

01:16:47.060 --> 01:16:50.060
When I arrived in
1950, the nation

01:16:50.060 --> 01:16:56.210
was focused on two other
wars, one hot and one cold.

01:16:56.210 --> 01:16:58.090
However, as I've
tried to understand

01:16:58.090 --> 01:17:01.210
the consequences
of the war for MIT,

01:17:01.210 --> 01:17:02.830
it has seemed to
me useful to think

01:17:02.830 --> 01:17:08.750
about the effects of that period
as principally of three kinds.

01:17:08.750 --> 01:17:11.720
First, the war
and the GI Bill it

01:17:11.720 --> 01:17:16.490
produced expanded and changed,
democratized the MIT student

01:17:16.490 --> 01:17:18.760
body.

01:17:18.760 --> 01:17:21.000
Second, the wartime
problems presented

01:17:21.000 --> 01:17:24.120
intellectual challenges
which were vastly different

01:17:24.120 --> 01:17:27.210
from the knowledge base of
pre-war engineering education

01:17:27.210 --> 01:17:31.500
and practice, thereby creating
a mandate for great change

01:17:31.500 --> 01:17:34.750
in engineering curricula.

01:17:34.750 --> 01:17:37.180
Third, the significant
benefits that

01:17:37.180 --> 01:17:39.100
flowed from the
wartime partnership

01:17:39.100 --> 01:17:42.940
between educational institutions
and the federal government

01:17:42.940 --> 01:17:45.940
led to the creation of
peacetime partnerships

01:17:45.940 --> 01:17:48.880
and to the evolution of
the research universities

01:17:48.880 --> 01:17:50.305
for which MIT is a paradigm.

01:17:52.772 --> 01:17:54.480
In the time remaining
to me this morning,

01:17:54.480 --> 01:17:58.820
I shall briefly describe
these three effects.

01:17:58.820 --> 01:18:03.850
First, growth and change
in the student body.

01:18:03.850 --> 01:18:05.736
In the years just
before the war,

01:18:05.736 --> 01:18:07.360
the number of students
at the Institute

01:18:07.360 --> 01:18:12.280
was around 3,100, of which
about 2,400 were undergraduates

01:18:12.280 --> 01:18:15.510
and 700 were graduate students.

01:18:15.510 --> 01:18:18.960
Not surprisingly, the number
of matriculated students

01:18:18.960 --> 01:18:21.600
decreased sharply
during the war years,

01:18:21.600 --> 01:18:27.660
reaching a low of
about 1,200 in 1944-45.

01:18:27.660 --> 01:18:29.260
Where'd we go?

01:18:29.260 --> 01:18:31.740
There.

01:18:31.740 --> 01:18:37.290
This starts over here
1939, runs across to 1950.

01:18:37.290 --> 01:18:39.780
Little over 3,000 in
the pre-war years.

01:18:39.780 --> 01:18:43.590
Dropped in '44, '45,
down still in '46,

01:18:43.590 --> 01:18:50.360
but then up above 5,000 in
those years starting in 1947.

01:18:50.360 --> 01:18:54.540
The enrollments never again
fell below the level of 5,000,

01:18:54.540 --> 01:18:59.190
except for one year
during the Korean War.

01:18:59.190 --> 01:19:01.770
More importantly
than these numbers,

01:19:01.770 --> 01:19:03.810
these students were
sharply different

01:19:03.810 --> 01:19:07.360
from their counterparts
of the pre-war years.

01:19:07.360 --> 01:19:12.910
KT says it best in his report
of the president for 1945-46,

01:19:12.910 --> 01:19:16.021
the first full academic year
after the end of the war.

01:19:20.320 --> 01:19:23.170
I'd like that picture, please,
of Compton in slide number two.

01:19:27.340 --> 01:19:28.896
Next one.

01:19:28.896 --> 01:19:31.580
There we are.

01:19:31.580 --> 01:19:37.721
These are KT's words from the
1945-46 president's report.

01:19:37.721 --> 01:19:39.600
"If you were to
introduce yourself

01:19:39.600 --> 01:19:42.210
to a student whom you
chance to meet while walking

01:19:42.210 --> 01:19:45.300
through the Institute
corridors, the probability

01:19:45.300 --> 01:19:48.630
is that he would be an
undergraduate, older by two

01:19:48.630 --> 01:19:52.880
to four years than a pre-war
student of the same class.

01:19:52.880 --> 01:19:56.480
He would explain to you with
courtesy and poise borne

01:19:56.480 --> 01:20:00.500
of military experience
that he is a veteran,

01:20:00.500 --> 01:20:02.900
and that his objective
here is to secure

01:20:02.900 --> 01:20:05.600
a first-class
professional education as

01:20:05.600 --> 01:20:08.237
quickly as possible.

01:20:08.237 --> 01:20:10.320
If you were to express an
interest in his personal

01:20:10.320 --> 01:20:13.740
situation, you might discover
that he is one of the married

01:20:13.740 --> 01:20:17.580
veterans-- more than 30% of
the veterans are married--

01:20:17.580 --> 01:20:20.320
and that his family
includes a youngster."

01:20:20.320 --> 01:20:22.730
"If you inquire
about his studies,

01:20:22.730 --> 01:20:25.920
you will find that his
attitude is mature, serious,

01:20:25.920 --> 01:20:29.800
and hard-headed,
and his grades high.

01:20:29.800 --> 01:20:32.050
Since the educational
expenses of the veterans

01:20:32.050 --> 01:20:34.120
are financed largely
by the government,

01:20:34.120 --> 01:20:35.860
applicants have
not been restricted

01:20:35.860 --> 01:20:38.920
by financial considerations.

01:20:38.920 --> 01:20:42.610
The students, therefore,
come from all walks of life,

01:20:42.610 --> 01:20:46.030
and for the first time
we have a student body

01:20:46.030 --> 01:20:51.700
for which ability, preparation,
personality, and character have

01:20:51.700 --> 01:20:55.550
been the only requirements
for admission."

01:20:55.550 --> 01:20:58.540
Now, it's notable that, while
20 years would pass before

01:20:58.540 --> 01:21:02.470
the concepts of [? need-blind ?]
admissions and student aid

01:21:02.470 --> 01:21:06.340
sufficient to meet full need
would emerge here at MIT,

01:21:06.340 --> 01:21:11.920
KT laid out the rationale for
those ideas in his 1946 report,

01:21:11.920 --> 01:21:15.850
at a time when this place was
much enriched by the greater

01:21:15.850 --> 01:21:19.780
social and economic diversity
represented by the veteran

01:21:19.780 --> 01:21:22.240
students.

01:21:22.240 --> 01:21:24.520
Treasurer's report data
show that, for the year

01:21:24.520 --> 01:21:30.610
1946-47 and 1947-48,
more than half

01:21:30.610 --> 01:21:33.040
of the tuition receipts
of the Institute

01:21:33.040 --> 01:21:36.010
originated with the
Veterans Administration.

01:21:36.010 --> 01:21:45.910
And that fraction remained
quite high through 1952.

01:21:45.910 --> 01:21:49.360
If Dr. John Horton
is in the audience,

01:21:49.360 --> 01:21:51.470
there is an emergency
page for him.

01:21:51.470 --> 01:21:57.870
Would he please
call 201-425-6937.

01:21:57.870 --> 01:22:00.585
201-425-6937.

01:22:00.585 --> 01:22:01.590
Dr. John Horton.

01:22:04.960 --> 01:22:08.290
The increased number of
students at MIT in these years

01:22:08.290 --> 01:22:11.470
required increases in
the faculty, as well,

01:22:11.470 --> 01:22:14.350
and the count of
professors of all ranks

01:22:14.350 --> 01:22:18.250
rose from fewer than 300 in
the immediate pre-war years

01:22:18.250 --> 01:22:25.160
to 400 in 1947,
and to 500 by 1953.

01:22:25.160 --> 01:22:26.900
The Institute
struggled in the years

01:22:26.900 --> 01:22:29.780
just after the war to
find space to accommodate

01:22:29.780 --> 01:22:32.120
the sudden increase in
educational and research

01:22:32.120 --> 01:22:34.020
activities.

01:22:34.020 --> 01:22:35.480
Of course, the
temporary buildings

01:22:35.480 --> 01:22:37.520
thrown up to house the
radiation laboratory

01:22:37.520 --> 01:22:40.330
were pressed into service.

01:22:40.330 --> 01:22:41.830
We're going the wrong way here.

01:22:41.830 --> 01:22:43.371
I'll get it straight
before I'm done.

01:22:51.580 --> 01:22:52.300
One back, please.

01:22:55.440 --> 01:22:57.690
There we are.

01:22:57.690 --> 01:23:00.960
Building 20 is still
there on Vassar Street,

01:23:00.960 --> 01:23:03.861
full of academic programs
and research activities.

01:23:03.861 --> 01:23:06.687
[APPLAUSE]

01:23:08.580 --> 01:23:10.950
I say to you, though, if you
have a particular affection

01:23:10.950 --> 01:23:15.360
for building 20, and
if your pattern is

01:23:15.360 --> 01:23:17.670
to return to MIT only
every five years,

01:23:17.670 --> 01:23:20.610
go walk around today, because it
may not be there in five years.

01:23:20.610 --> 01:23:22.602
[LAUGHTER]

01:23:30.090 --> 01:23:34.080
Building 22, in the left
foreground of this photograph,

01:23:34.080 --> 01:23:36.270
was converted to
a barracks, where

01:23:36.270 --> 01:23:39.900
100 freshmen lived for periods
ranging from days to months.

01:23:39.900 --> 01:23:43.260
It, happily, is long gone.

01:23:43.260 --> 01:23:45.360
To meet the needs
of married veterans,

01:23:45.360 --> 01:23:49.020
the Institute built, in
1946, with its own funds,

01:23:49.020 --> 01:23:53.620
the village known as Westgate,
which housed 100 families.

01:23:53.620 --> 01:23:56.700
Here in the foreground, it
was the first veterans project

01:23:56.700 --> 01:23:58.450
undertaken by an
American college.

01:24:01.010 --> 01:24:03.350
These changes in
the MIT student body

01:24:03.350 --> 01:24:08.270
that resulted from the war and
the GI Bill were, I submit,

01:24:08.270 --> 01:24:11.000
preamble to the
further democratization

01:24:11.000 --> 01:24:13.880
of this place, which
began 20 years later,

01:24:13.880 --> 01:24:16.250
and which is so evident today.

01:24:16.250 --> 01:24:19.070
Now, 2/5 of the
undergraduates and a quarter

01:24:19.070 --> 01:24:22.020
of the graduate
students are women.

01:24:22.020 --> 01:24:24.600
African-Americans,
Hispanic-Americans,

01:24:24.600 --> 01:24:26.490
Asian-Americans,
Native Americans,

01:24:26.490 --> 01:24:30.600
and large numbers of foreign
citizens study at MIT.

01:24:30.600 --> 01:24:32.850
The human face of the
Institute reflects

01:24:32.850 --> 01:24:39.010
ever more fully the society
and the world in which we live.

01:24:39.010 --> 01:24:43.350
I come now to the revolution
in engineering curricula.

01:24:43.350 --> 01:24:45.900
The applications of technology
to the defense effort

01:24:45.900 --> 01:24:48.630
led engineers into new
fields of engineering science

01:24:48.630 --> 01:24:49.980
and technology--

01:24:49.980 --> 01:24:53.190
microwaves, waveguides,
feedback control systems,

01:24:53.190 --> 01:24:56.010
analog computation,
and radar technology,

01:24:56.010 --> 01:24:58.470
to mention just a few.

01:24:58.470 --> 01:25:00.960
Many engineers educated
in the '20s and '30s

01:25:00.960 --> 01:25:04.170
found such topics
enormously challenging.

01:25:04.170 --> 01:25:07.350
The underlying physical and
mathematical foundations

01:25:07.350 --> 01:25:11.930
necessary for effective work in
these new areas were not there.

01:25:11.930 --> 01:25:14.840
Further, the increased pace
of technology development,

01:25:14.840 --> 01:25:17.090
driven by the war
effort, resulted

01:25:17.090 --> 01:25:20.180
in the introduction of
much quite new technology

01:25:20.180 --> 01:25:23.060
in the years just after the war.

01:25:23.060 --> 01:25:25.430
The invention of the
transistor in 1948

01:25:25.430 --> 01:25:28.520
changed everything for
electrical engineers,

01:25:28.520 --> 01:25:31.460
as did the widespread
use of jet engines,

01:25:31.460 --> 01:25:33.800
and the transition
to supersonic flight

01:25:33.800 --> 01:25:37.560
for aeronautical and
mechanical engineers.

01:25:37.560 --> 01:25:39.810
These changes in
applied technology

01:25:39.810 --> 01:25:43.080
led the engineering faculty
at MIT and elsewhere

01:25:43.080 --> 01:25:46.170
to undertake dramatic revisions
of programs for engineering

01:25:46.170 --> 01:25:50.490
education, shifting the emphasis
to engineering science and more

01:25:50.490 --> 01:25:52.530
mathematics.

01:25:52.530 --> 01:25:54.630
At the Institute,
this revolution

01:25:54.630 --> 01:25:57.600
was led principally
by Gordon Brown, who

01:25:57.600 --> 01:26:00.180
served as head of the electrical
engineering department

01:26:00.180 --> 01:26:04.380
from 1952 to 1959, and
as dean of engineering

01:26:04.380 --> 01:26:08.620
from 1959 to 1968.

01:26:08.620 --> 01:26:10.900
Gordon, who headed the
servomechanisms laboratory

01:26:10.900 --> 01:26:12.850
during the war,
held strong views

01:26:12.850 --> 01:26:16.090
about the changes necessary
in the undergraduate education

01:26:16.090 --> 01:26:17.890
of engineers.

01:26:17.890 --> 01:26:19.870
Out went the electrical
machinery laboratory

01:26:19.870 --> 01:26:23.530
and the steam laboratory,
in came new core programs

01:26:23.530 --> 01:26:26.020
emphasizing the underlying
physical and mathematical

01:26:26.020 --> 01:26:29.470
foundations, the newly
developed technology,

01:26:29.470 --> 01:26:32.524
and engineering design.

01:26:32.524 --> 01:26:33.940
Any who knew Gordon
in those years

01:26:33.940 --> 01:26:35.410
will remember that
he liked to say

01:26:35.410 --> 01:26:38.340
the only thing constant around
here is the rate of change.

01:26:38.340 --> 01:26:42.060
And he was a major
contributor to that change.

01:26:42.060 --> 01:26:44.170
It is, I believe, fair to
say that the engineering

01:26:44.170 --> 01:26:48.160
school at MIT, which was in the
vanguard in these revisions,

01:26:48.160 --> 01:26:51.250
set the pace for engineering
education in the United States

01:26:51.250 --> 01:26:54.710
throughout the 1950s
and 1960s, and in fact

01:26:54.710 --> 01:26:57.560
in many other nations, as well.

01:26:57.560 --> 01:27:00.460
Now, 40 years later, the
engineering departments

01:27:00.460 --> 01:27:04.450
here are again looking closely
at the structure and content

01:27:04.450 --> 01:27:07.090
of the degree
programs, and listening

01:27:07.090 --> 01:27:11.320
to the perceived needs of the
employers of our graduates.

01:27:11.320 --> 01:27:14.700
The recently introduced
masters of engineering programs

01:27:14.700 --> 01:27:16.440
in the school are
one manifestation

01:27:16.440 --> 01:27:19.530
of that effort, as was
the program called Leaders

01:27:19.530 --> 01:27:21.810
for Manufacturing,
introduced by engineering

01:27:21.810 --> 01:27:25.780
and Sloan a few years ago.

01:27:25.780 --> 01:27:28.450
I come now to the third
element of the transformation,

01:27:28.450 --> 01:27:32.970
the partnership between
universities and government.

01:27:32.970 --> 01:27:35.189
The war clearly
demonstrated the benefits

01:27:35.189 --> 01:27:37.230
that flowed out of government
support of research

01:27:37.230 --> 01:27:39.060
and development,
conducted largely

01:27:39.060 --> 01:27:42.500
by university-based people.

01:27:42.500 --> 01:27:45.310
Vannevar Bush, an electrical
engineering graduate

01:27:45.310 --> 01:27:49.420
of the class of 1916 at
MIT, was a central figure

01:27:49.420 --> 01:27:52.210
both in the coordination
of wartime efforts

01:27:52.210 --> 01:27:54.460
and in the shaping of
an ongoing relationship

01:27:54.460 --> 01:27:57.980
between the federal government
and the universities.

01:27:57.980 --> 01:28:02.330
Bush had served during the 1930s
as dean of engineering at MIT,

01:28:02.330 --> 01:28:04.580
and was vice president
of the Institute

01:28:04.580 --> 01:28:07.910
in a time in which there
was but one vice president.

01:28:07.910 --> 01:28:10.940
And later, he was president
of the Carnegie Institution

01:28:10.940 --> 01:28:12.900
of Washington.

01:28:12.900 --> 01:28:18.570
Van was an advisor to six MIT
presidents, including this one.

01:28:18.570 --> 01:28:20.340
During the war years,
Van Bush served

01:28:20.340 --> 01:28:22.740
as Director of the Office
of Scientific Research

01:28:22.740 --> 01:28:25.440
and Development, the
federal agency which

01:28:25.440 --> 01:28:28.350
coordinated the forced draft
research and development

01:28:28.350 --> 01:28:33.660
efforts undertaken to aid
in prosecution of the war.

01:28:33.660 --> 01:28:36.090
In November 1944,
more than nine months

01:28:36.090 --> 01:28:38.580
before the end of
the war, Bush was

01:28:38.580 --> 01:28:41.370
asked by President
Roosevelt to consider

01:28:41.370 --> 01:28:43.410
the nature of the
government-university

01:28:43.410 --> 01:28:46.650
partnership in the
postwar period.

01:28:46.650 --> 01:28:50.260
FDR's letter to Bush stated
the challenge in these terms,

01:28:50.260 --> 01:28:52.530
and I quote.

01:28:52.530 --> 01:28:54.890
"The Office of Scientific
Research and Development,

01:28:54.890 --> 01:28:58.140
of which you are the director,
presents a unique experiment

01:28:58.140 --> 01:29:00.720
of teamwork and
cooperation in coordinating

01:29:00.720 --> 01:29:03.660
scientific research and
in applying existing

01:29:03.660 --> 01:29:06.060
scientific knowledge
to the solutions

01:29:06.060 --> 01:29:08.510
of the technical problems
paramount in war."

01:29:23.380 --> 01:29:25.630
"There is no reason why
the lessons found in this

01:29:25.630 --> 01:29:30.340
experiment cannot be profitably
employed in times of peace.

01:29:30.340 --> 01:29:33.400
The information, the techniques,
and the research experience

01:29:33.400 --> 01:29:37.150
developed by OSRD, and by
the thousands of scientists

01:29:37.150 --> 01:29:39.850
in the universities and
in private industry,

01:29:39.850 --> 01:29:42.580
should be used in the
days of peace ahead

01:29:42.580 --> 01:29:44.980
for the improvement
of national health,

01:29:44.980 --> 01:29:48.070
for the creation of new
enterprise, bringing new jobs,

01:29:48.070 --> 01:29:51.060
and for the betterment of
national standards of living."

01:29:51.060 --> 01:29:53.180
End quote.

01:29:53.180 --> 01:29:54.800
Van Bush brought
together a panel

01:29:54.800 --> 01:29:57.170
to undertake this
broadly stated task,

01:29:57.170 --> 01:30:00.440
and they made their
response to President Truman

01:30:00.440 --> 01:30:04.310
in the form of a report
dated July 5th, 1945,

01:30:04.310 --> 01:30:05.780
entitled Science--

01:30:05.780 --> 01:30:08.270
The Endless Frontier.

01:30:08.270 --> 01:30:11.360
This report expressed
two priorities.

01:30:11.360 --> 01:30:12.260
I quote again.

01:30:12.260 --> 01:30:15.800
"First, we must have plenty
of men and women trained

01:30:15.800 --> 01:30:18.080
in science, for
upon them depends

01:30:18.080 --> 01:30:20.390
both the creation
of new knowledge

01:30:20.390 --> 01:30:23.550
and its application
to practical purposes.

01:30:23.550 --> 01:30:25.740
Second, we must
strengthen the centers

01:30:25.740 --> 01:30:28.470
of basic research,
which are principally

01:30:28.470 --> 01:30:32.800
the colleges, universities,
and research institutes.

01:30:32.800 --> 01:30:34.960
These institutions
provide the environment

01:30:34.960 --> 01:30:36.940
which is most conducive
to the creation

01:30:36.940 --> 01:30:39.340
of new scientific
knowledge, and least

01:30:39.340 --> 01:30:42.640
under pressure for
immediate tangible results."

01:30:42.640 --> 01:30:44.720
End quote.

01:30:44.720 --> 01:30:48.470
Van understood instinctively,
and expressed in Science--

01:30:48.470 --> 01:30:52.520
The Endless Frontier, the
importance of tying together,

01:30:52.520 --> 01:30:56.510
completely tying together
graduate education

01:30:56.510 --> 01:30:58.490
and front-line research.

01:30:58.490 --> 01:31:01.070
He recognized that
both prospered best

01:31:01.070 --> 01:31:04.220
when they were done in the same
environment by the same people.

01:31:04.220 --> 01:31:08.200
An extremely
important proposition.

01:31:08.200 --> 01:31:10.570
Science-- The Endless
Frontier provided the basis

01:31:10.570 --> 01:31:13.857
for ongoing federal support
in scientific research,

01:31:13.857 --> 01:31:15.940
and for the growth and
development of the research

01:31:15.940 --> 01:31:18.030
universities.

01:31:18.030 --> 01:31:20.730
It led to the establishment by
the Congress of the National

01:31:20.730 --> 01:31:23.550
Institutes of Health and the
National Science Foundation,

01:31:23.550 --> 01:31:26.520
two agencies which are now
in the vanguard of research

01:31:26.520 --> 01:31:29.540
and student support
in higher education.

01:31:29.540 --> 01:31:31.610
And to the encouragement
of continued research

01:31:31.610 --> 01:31:34.880
support by the Department of
Defense and the Atomic Energy

01:31:34.880 --> 01:31:36.690
Agency.

01:31:36.690 --> 01:31:39.180
It promoted the development
of policies and practices

01:31:39.180 --> 01:31:41.430
which permitted the
reimbursement of institutions

01:31:41.430 --> 01:31:44.520
of higher education for the
research which they undertook

01:31:44.520 --> 01:31:46.730
on behalf of the government.

01:31:46.730 --> 01:31:49.620
Support of university and
college-based research

01:31:49.620 --> 01:31:54.360
took two forms, fellowships
and traineeships, in some cases

01:31:54.360 --> 01:31:57.180
entirely portable,
awarded on the basis

01:31:57.180 --> 01:32:00.540
of intellectual merit,
and direct support

01:32:00.540 --> 01:32:04.530
of research programs on the
basis of contracts and grants,

01:32:04.530 --> 01:32:07.110
including considerable
support of a highly

01:32:07.110 --> 01:32:09.870
discretionary nature.

01:32:09.870 --> 01:32:13.590
Bush's report set the stage for
federal patronage of research

01:32:13.590 --> 01:32:17.790
in colleges and universities,
and for graduate education

01:32:17.790 --> 01:32:19.950
in science and engineering.

01:32:19.950 --> 01:32:22.410
And the support which it
produced grew steadily.

01:32:25.160 --> 01:32:27.260
Colleges and universities
found their development

01:32:27.260 --> 01:32:30.500
during the 1950s limited
by the scarcity of support

01:32:30.500 --> 01:32:34.340
for facilities which their
expanded mission required.

01:32:34.340 --> 01:32:37.160
It took another war,
one of a different kind,

01:32:37.160 --> 01:32:39.020
to enable a new phase
in the development

01:32:39.020 --> 01:32:42.470
of this relationship, one in
which the need for facilities

01:32:42.470 --> 01:32:44.750
was addressed.

01:32:44.750 --> 01:32:46.790
Before the end of
the 1940s, the shape

01:32:46.790 --> 01:32:49.820
of the coming conflict between
the Western democracies

01:32:49.820 --> 01:32:53.090
and the Soviet bloc
nations became evident.

01:32:53.090 --> 01:32:55.640
In a speech at Westminster
College in Fulton, Missouri,

01:32:55.640 --> 01:33:00.350
in 1946, Winston Churchill
described the nature

01:33:00.350 --> 01:33:04.650
of what he called the Iron
Curtain, which divided Europe.

01:33:04.650 --> 01:33:07.100
The Soviet attempt to starve
the Allies out of Berlin

01:33:07.100 --> 01:33:10.730
in its blockade of all surface
transportation from the West

01:33:10.730 --> 01:33:13.310
to the partitioned
city marked the first

01:33:13.310 --> 01:33:15.650
of many East-West
confrontations and tests

01:33:15.650 --> 01:33:18.320
of power, which were to
characterize the Cold

01:33:18.320 --> 01:33:21.050
War of the next four decades.

01:33:21.050 --> 01:33:23.690
It was soon followed
by the war in Korea,

01:33:23.690 --> 01:33:26.930
in which a UN force, with the
United States in the vanguard,

01:33:26.930 --> 01:33:29.450
opposed the North
Korean invaders who

01:33:29.450 --> 01:33:34.790
were surrogates for the USSR and
the People's Republic of China.

01:33:34.790 --> 01:33:37.280
For most of the
1950s, the leaders

01:33:37.280 --> 01:33:39.470
and the people of
this nation assumed

01:33:39.470 --> 01:33:42.980
that the technical and military
superiority demonstrated

01:33:42.980 --> 01:33:45.500
during the Second
World War would prevent

01:33:45.500 --> 01:33:48.530
any significant heating
up of the Cold War,

01:33:48.530 --> 01:33:51.200
and would, in any case,
permit the Western democracies

01:33:51.200 --> 01:33:53.620
to prevail.

01:33:53.620 --> 01:33:56.710
This smug, self-congratulatory
assuredness

01:33:56.710 --> 01:34:00.520
was shattered in October
1957, when the Soviet

01:34:00.520 --> 01:34:03.610
Union put Sputnik, a
grapefruit-sized Earth

01:34:03.610 --> 01:34:06.070
satellite, in orbit.

01:34:06.070 --> 01:34:09.160
The disillusionment was enhanced
when two American efforts

01:34:09.160 --> 01:34:12.460
to follow suit ended in
spectacular explosions

01:34:12.460 --> 01:34:14.590
on launchpads.

01:34:14.590 --> 01:34:17.050
Suddenly, it seemed
to many in this nation

01:34:17.050 --> 01:34:20.860
that we were only second best
in the competition with the USSR

01:34:20.860 --> 01:34:23.920
in space, perhaps in
Cold War weaponry,

01:34:23.920 --> 01:34:27.460
as well, and possibly in science
and engineering education

01:34:27.460 --> 01:34:29.890
to boot.

01:34:29.890 --> 01:34:31.960
Within days of the
launch of Sputnik,

01:34:31.960 --> 01:34:35.320
President Eisenhower appointed
Dr. James Rhyne Killian

01:34:35.320 --> 01:34:40.300
Jr., then president of MIT,
as his science advisor,

01:34:40.300 --> 01:34:43.480
and the first presidential
science advisory mechanism,

01:34:43.480 --> 01:34:46.990
the President's Science Advisory
Committee, PSAC for short,

01:34:46.990 --> 01:34:49.000
was formed.

01:34:49.000 --> 01:34:51.550
Several efforts to improve
the teaching of mathematics

01:34:51.550 --> 01:34:55.130
and science in the schools were
created, and, in due course,

01:34:55.130 --> 01:34:57.610
the physical science study
committee curriculum,

01:34:57.610 --> 01:35:00.700
the new math, and new versions
of biology and chemistry

01:35:00.700 --> 01:35:02.350
appeared.

01:35:02.350 --> 01:35:05.210
The Congress, in the National
Defense Education Act,

01:35:05.210 --> 01:35:08.030
created and funded
programs of student aid

01:35:08.030 --> 01:35:10.220
intended to draw
more young people

01:35:10.220 --> 01:35:14.000
into the study of science
and engineering in college.

01:35:14.000 --> 01:35:16.790
The sense of hazard created
by the technical achievements

01:35:16.790 --> 01:35:20.630
of the USSR, and by a few
episodes of the Cold War,

01:35:20.630 --> 01:35:23.090
including the erection
of the Berlin Wall

01:35:23.090 --> 01:35:26.630
and the crisis over Soviet
nuclear missiles in Cuba,

01:35:26.630 --> 01:35:30.830
produced an astonishing period
of growth for higher education.

01:35:30.830 --> 01:35:33.650
Funds for sponsored research
in colleges and universities

01:35:33.650 --> 01:35:37.760
increased by more than a factor
of five in constant dollars

01:35:37.760 --> 01:35:44.780
during the decade from 1958 to
1968, as shown in this figure.

01:35:44.780 --> 01:35:48.150
The ordinate here is
on a logarithmic scale,

01:35:48.150 --> 01:35:51.370
so that exponentials
become straight lines.

01:35:51.370 --> 01:35:53.580
And you see essentially
exponential growth

01:35:53.580 --> 01:35:57.320
from 1958 to 1968.

01:35:57.320 --> 01:35:59.900
The slope of that line
corresponds to a growth rate

01:35:59.900 --> 01:36:04.000
of about 18% per year.

01:36:04.000 --> 01:36:06.240
The support of graduate
students through fellowships

01:36:06.240 --> 01:36:10.440
and traineeships grew strongly,
as did grant and loan programs

01:36:10.440 --> 01:36:13.380
in support of
undergraduate students.

01:36:13.380 --> 01:36:16.140
Most important, federal
agencies made grants

01:36:16.140 --> 01:36:19.080
for the construction
of research facilities,

01:36:19.080 --> 01:36:20.860
and for major
items of equipment,

01:36:20.860 --> 01:36:23.490
which enabled the remarkable
expansion of the research

01:36:23.490 --> 01:36:25.720
universities.

01:36:25.720 --> 01:36:28.240
At MIT, the partnership
with the federal government

01:36:28.240 --> 01:36:31.880
produced very rapid growth
on several dimensions.

01:36:31.880 --> 01:36:33.990
The volume of
sponsored research,

01:36:33.990 --> 01:36:36.320
including work done at
the Lincoln Laboratory

01:36:36.320 --> 01:36:37.940
and the Instrumentation
Laboratory,

01:36:37.940 --> 01:36:41.390
which became the Draper
Laboratory in 1970, as well

01:36:41.390 --> 01:36:44.420
as on this campus, increased
in constant dollars

01:36:44.420 --> 01:36:49.710
by a factor of almost three from
1958 to 1968, as shown here.

01:36:49.710 --> 01:36:52.040
The top line is total federal
research and development

01:36:52.040 --> 01:36:55.500
at MIT, about $300 million.

01:36:55.500 --> 01:36:57.670
These are all 1994
dollars, by the way.

01:36:57.670 --> 01:36:59.490
It seemed sensible
to me to put all

01:36:59.490 --> 01:37:02.580
of these numbers in
current dollar terms.

01:37:02.580 --> 01:37:08.400
1994 dollars, about 300
million total in 1958,

01:37:08.400 --> 01:37:12.990
up to just over 700
million in 1968.

01:37:12.990 --> 01:37:17.370
The red line shows
campus research support.

01:37:17.370 --> 01:37:18.810
This is research
done principally

01:37:18.810 --> 01:37:21.210
on the campus by graduate
students and faculty,

01:37:21.210 --> 01:37:24.284
up by a larger fraction,
of course, over that time.

01:37:24.284 --> 01:37:26.700
And the defense laboratories,
Lincoln and Instrumentation,

01:37:26.700 --> 01:37:31.170
taken together, the blue line.

01:37:31.170 --> 01:37:34.380
By 1970, the number of
enrolled students at MIT

01:37:34.380 --> 01:37:37.920
had increased to 8,000,
approximately half of whom

01:37:37.920 --> 01:37:39.840
were graduate students.

01:37:39.840 --> 01:37:43.600
The faculty of the Institute
grew to just over 1,000.

01:37:43.600 --> 01:37:46.210
Many academic facilities
were added to the campus,

01:37:46.210 --> 01:37:48.310
with a peak of
research-related construction

01:37:48.310 --> 01:37:52.450
activity in the 1960s, which saw
the construction of the Space

01:37:52.450 --> 01:37:53.950
Center.

01:37:53.950 --> 01:37:55.960
Here is the Space
Center on Vassar Street,

01:37:55.960 --> 01:37:58.432
just coming out of the ground.

01:37:58.432 --> 01:38:03.640
The physical plant, central
steam plant in the background.

01:38:03.640 --> 01:38:05.890
And the Center for Materials
Research and Engineering,

01:38:05.890 --> 01:38:09.790
building 13, behind
the great dome.

01:38:09.790 --> 01:38:11.230
Occupying yet
another parking lot.

01:38:13.760 --> 01:38:16.070
The American presence,
military presence,

01:38:16.070 --> 01:38:20.210
in Southeast Asia, which
began early in the 1960s,

01:38:20.210 --> 01:38:22.520
accelerated rapidly
in the administration

01:38:22.520 --> 01:38:26.900
of President Lyndon B.
Johnson, from 1963 to 1968.

01:38:26.900 --> 01:38:29.540
By the late 1960s,
military expenditures

01:38:29.540 --> 01:38:33.710
were in continuous and severe
conflict with LBJ's programs

01:38:33.710 --> 01:38:36.500
for the Great Society,
in which he envisioned

01:38:36.500 --> 01:38:41.150
an end to poverty, homelessness,
and all avoidable human misery

01:38:41.150 --> 01:38:42.830
in America.

01:38:42.830 --> 01:38:46.250
That conflict, which
was intense by 1968,

01:38:46.250 --> 01:38:48.080
led to an abrupt
change in the growth

01:38:48.080 --> 01:38:51.110
of federal support of higher
education in that year,

01:38:51.110 --> 01:38:52.050
as is evident here.

01:38:54.524 --> 01:38:55.940
We got it upside
down, I'm afraid.

01:38:55.940 --> 01:38:58.808
[LAUGHTER]

01:39:02.154 --> 01:39:05.200
There we are.

01:39:05.200 --> 01:39:08.200
This, again, logarithmic
scale on the ordinate.

01:39:08.200 --> 01:39:15.100
1954 the first
year, over to 1994.

01:39:15.100 --> 01:39:20.470
40 years of federal support
of university-based research.

01:39:20.470 --> 01:39:22.985
This is the total federal
support of all universities

01:39:22.985 --> 01:39:23.860
in the United States.

01:39:23.860 --> 01:39:26.880
And again, it's
constant 1994 dollars.

01:39:26.880 --> 01:39:31.420
You see here this period
of 15 or so years' duration

01:39:31.420 --> 01:39:33.700
of very rapid growth,
double-digit growth,

01:39:33.700 --> 01:39:35.310
exponential growth
during that period,

01:39:35.310 --> 01:39:39.150
an abrupt change in
1969, 1970, essentially

01:39:39.150 --> 01:39:45.720
flat during the Nixon and into
the Carter administration,

01:39:45.720 --> 01:39:48.750
turning up a little at the
beginning of the Reagan

01:39:48.750 --> 01:39:52.170
administration, growing
here again exponentially,

01:39:52.170 --> 01:39:55.914
but at a much lower rate, 6%
or 7% a year, and then flat

01:39:55.914 --> 01:39:58.080
or turning down a little
in these most recent years.

01:40:04.020 --> 01:40:09.860
While support was flat in
real terms in the 1970s,

01:40:09.860 --> 01:40:12.530
college and university-based
research enterprise

01:40:12.530 --> 01:40:16.101
continued to grow in
size and complexity.

01:40:18.926 --> 01:40:21.300
There was no resumption of
real growth in federal support

01:40:21.300 --> 01:40:23.070
or research in colleges
and universities

01:40:23.070 --> 01:40:25.290
until early in the
Reagan administration,

01:40:25.290 --> 01:40:29.220
and growth from 1983
to 1989 was about 7%

01:40:29.220 --> 01:40:33.650
per year, which has not been
matched in these recent years.

01:40:33.650 --> 01:40:35.510
The conflict in
federal priorities

01:40:35.510 --> 01:40:39.630
not only reversed the trend of
federal support of research,

01:40:39.630 --> 01:40:41.810
they also set the
economy of the nation

01:40:41.810 --> 01:40:45.210
on the course of steadily
growing inflation.

01:40:45.210 --> 01:40:48.690
This pattern, which lasted
until the early 1980s,

01:40:48.690 --> 01:40:51.540
produced cost pressures on
colleges and universities

01:40:51.540 --> 01:40:54.840
which were intense, and
which led to a decade or more

01:40:54.840 --> 01:40:58.390
of large increases
in tuitions and fees.

01:40:58.390 --> 01:41:00.490
These pressures, coupled
with a steady decline

01:41:00.490 --> 01:41:03.760
in real terms of federal
support for student aid,

01:41:03.760 --> 01:41:05.620
has led to the
present severe demand

01:41:05.620 --> 01:41:08.500
for the use of institutional
discretionary funds

01:41:08.500 --> 01:41:11.420
in support of scholarships.

01:41:11.420 --> 01:41:14.810
Now, clearly the interval
from 1950 to 1968

01:41:14.810 --> 01:41:17.120
reflected a period of
extraordinary growth

01:41:17.120 --> 01:41:20.160
not approached in
the decades since.

01:41:20.160 --> 01:41:22.320
Many in the research
university community

01:41:22.320 --> 01:41:26.460
think of the 1960s as the
golden years, a decade in which

01:41:26.460 --> 01:41:28.950
the partnership between the
federal government and higher

01:41:28.950 --> 01:41:31.590
education achieved a
real rate of growth

01:41:31.590 --> 01:41:34.530
of research support,
which, while obviously not

01:41:34.530 --> 01:41:37.590
sustainable over the long
term, might be regarded

01:41:37.590 --> 01:41:39.780
as a standard against
which other efforts could

01:41:39.780 --> 01:41:41.680
be measured.

01:41:41.680 --> 01:41:43.750
Many informed
persons in government

01:41:43.750 --> 01:41:47.590
regard this decade of remarkable
growth as the exception,

01:41:47.590 --> 01:41:51.160
rather than any kind of
rule, as a unique interval

01:41:51.160 --> 01:41:54.670
which resulted from the sense
of unlimited possibilities

01:41:54.670 --> 01:42:00.030
that was so characteristic
of the 1950s and early 1960s.

01:42:00.030 --> 01:42:06.190
They regard it as an aberration
unlikely ever to occur again.

01:42:06.190 --> 01:42:10.000
With the end of the Cold War,
and the threats and unifying

01:42:10.000 --> 01:42:13.570
theme it presented, with the
disintegration of the Soviet

01:42:13.570 --> 01:42:17.110
Union, and now with a new
Congress in Washington,

01:42:17.110 --> 01:42:20.290
we are seeing the beginnings of
a conflict of a different kind,

01:42:20.290 --> 01:42:23.410
a war on the federal deficit.

01:42:23.410 --> 01:42:25.660
And the associated
conflicts and reassessment

01:42:25.660 --> 01:42:28.390
of national priorities
will have consequences

01:42:28.390 --> 01:42:31.240
for higher education,
which will be, I believe,

01:42:31.240 --> 01:42:34.270
fully as significant as the
consequences of World War

01:42:34.270 --> 01:42:38.340
II and the Cold War.

01:42:38.340 --> 01:42:39.810
While my purpose
in these remarks

01:42:39.810 --> 01:42:42.600
has been to sketch out the
several dimensions of what

01:42:42.600 --> 01:42:47.160
has been a transformation in
the character and scale of MIT,

01:42:47.160 --> 01:42:49.980
a transformation over
these last 50 years

01:42:49.980 --> 01:42:53.730
that was a consequence of
World War II and the Cold War,

01:42:53.730 --> 01:42:56.910
which followed
hard on its heels.

01:42:56.910 --> 01:42:59.580
With the Cold War ended,
we face security threats

01:42:59.580 --> 01:43:01.440
of very different kinds--

01:43:01.440 --> 01:43:03.990
small-scale, increasingly
violent conflicts,

01:43:03.990 --> 01:43:07.290
as in the former
Yugoslavia, growing gaps

01:43:07.290 --> 01:43:09.030
between the haves
and the have nots

01:43:09.030 --> 01:43:13.650
of the world, and of
this nation, terrorism,

01:43:13.650 --> 01:43:18.130
and the nightmare of potential
nuclear weapons proliferation.

01:43:18.130 --> 01:43:20.320
None of these threats
appears to our government

01:43:20.320 --> 01:43:24.040
to require or to justify
the support of education

01:43:24.040 --> 01:43:26.440
or university-based
research, which was

01:43:26.440 --> 01:43:28.990
so evident during the Cold War.

01:43:28.990 --> 01:43:32.560
And the struggle to reshape the
federal budget, a struggle so

01:43:32.560 --> 01:43:35.740
evident in these last
weeks, seems all too likely

01:43:35.740 --> 01:43:38.530
to force dramatic changes
in support of education

01:43:38.530 --> 01:43:41.430
and basic research.

01:43:41.430 --> 01:43:44.910
35 years ago, Glenn Seaborg
and his colleagues on PSAC,

01:43:44.910 --> 01:43:47.490
the President's Science Advisory
Committee, put it this way,

01:43:47.490 --> 01:43:50.925
in a report entitled Scientific
Progress, the Universities

01:43:50.925 --> 01:43:52.050
and the Federal Government.

01:43:52.050 --> 01:43:56.310
And I quote, "whether
the quantity and quality

01:43:56.310 --> 01:44:00.000
of basic research and graduate
education in the United States

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:03.510
will be adequate or
inadequate depends primarily

01:44:03.510 --> 01:44:06.570
on the government of
the United States.

01:44:06.570 --> 01:44:09.090
From this responsibility,
the federal government

01:44:09.090 --> 01:44:11.310
has no escape.

01:44:11.310 --> 01:44:14.700
Either it will find the
policies and the resources which

01:44:14.700 --> 01:44:17.250
permit our universities
to flourish

01:44:17.250 --> 01:44:20.340
and their duties to be
adequately discharged,

01:44:20.340 --> 01:44:23.460
or no one will."

01:44:23.460 --> 01:44:26.160
"The federal government at
times seems to those of us

01:44:26.160 --> 01:44:30.390
in universities to be a fickle,
untrustworthy, unreliable,

01:44:30.390 --> 01:44:33.780
and unsympathetic partner,
set on regarding higher

01:44:33.780 --> 01:44:37.380
education as just one more
special interest group,

01:44:37.380 --> 01:44:41.100
and given to construing the
research partnership as similar

01:44:41.100 --> 01:44:45.584
in nature to the procurement
of combat boots or Jeeps.

01:44:45.584 --> 01:44:47.750
And I know, because government
officials and members

01:44:47.750 --> 01:44:49.580
of Congress have
from time to time

01:44:49.580 --> 01:44:52.220
explained it quite
forcefully to me,

01:44:52.220 --> 01:44:53.990
that the universities
seem at times

01:44:53.990 --> 01:44:57.140
to be arrogant,
imperfect, unreasonably

01:44:57.140 --> 01:45:01.070
demanding entities, remarkably
insensitive to political and

01:45:01.070 --> 01:45:03.420
fiscal realities."

01:45:03.420 --> 01:45:06.770
"And there's much truth
in both perspectives.

01:45:06.770 --> 01:45:10.550
Nevertheless, the partnership is
vital to the national welfare,

01:45:10.550 --> 01:45:13.460
and its accomplishments
over the past five decades

01:45:13.460 --> 01:45:16.490
have been both extraordinary
in scope and of great benefit

01:45:16.490 --> 01:45:18.400
to the nation.

01:45:18.400 --> 01:45:20.350
The productivity
of the relationship

01:45:20.350 --> 01:45:22.900
is evident in all
those areas anticipated

01:45:22.900 --> 01:45:29.110
in 1944 by President Roosevelt
in his charge to Van Bush--

01:45:29.110 --> 01:45:32.920
improved health, new
industries and new jobs,

01:45:32.920 --> 01:45:36.430
and improvements in
living standards."

01:45:36.430 --> 01:45:40.200
As Seaborg put it in
that same report in 1960,

01:45:40.200 --> 01:45:42.690
with all their
irritating faults,

01:45:42.690 --> 01:45:46.590
universities are essential
agencies of our national hopes,

01:45:46.590 --> 01:45:49.674
and they must be
treated accordingly.

01:45:49.674 --> 01:45:51.840
The federal government,
with all its contradictions,

01:45:51.840 --> 01:45:56.980
surprises, and persistent myopia
about the longer term future

01:45:56.980 --> 01:46:01.320
is the sine qua non for the
universities and colleges.

01:46:01.320 --> 01:46:03.310
Making the partnership
work better

01:46:03.310 --> 01:46:07.350
is in the national interest,
and deserves our unstinting,

01:46:07.350 --> 01:46:12.660
steadily applied best efforts,
for on that partnership depends

01:46:12.660 --> 01:46:14.940
the well-being of
higher education

01:46:14.940 --> 01:46:17.280
and the nation it serves
in the next century.

01:46:17.280 --> 01:46:18.747
Thank you very much.

01:46:18.747 --> 01:46:22.659
[APPLAUSE]

01:46:34.420 --> 01:46:36.310
I now invite Lester
Thurow to the lectern.

01:46:40.246 --> 01:46:44.182
[APPLAUSE]

01:46:52.080 --> 01:46:54.750
THUROW: If you think
about a major war,

01:46:54.750 --> 01:46:58.090
what a major war does
is shake the system.

01:46:58.090 --> 01:47:02.720
And it shakes the system in one
fundamental sociological way.

01:47:02.720 --> 01:47:05.400
And that is people are
willing to do things

01:47:05.400 --> 01:47:09.049
that they're not willing to
do in any other circumstance.

01:47:09.049 --> 01:47:10.590
And if you look at
World War II, it's

01:47:10.590 --> 01:47:12.570
important to put it
together with the fact

01:47:12.570 --> 01:47:15.360
that, in the 1930s
and the 1940s,

01:47:15.360 --> 01:47:17.850
the American social
system was grievously

01:47:17.850 --> 01:47:20.980
shocked by two earthquakes.

01:47:20.980 --> 01:47:23.390
The first, of course,
was the Great Depression,

01:47:23.390 --> 01:47:26.080
and the second was World War II.

01:47:26.080 --> 01:47:28.996
And those two events did not
accelerate economic change,

01:47:28.996 --> 01:47:30.370
although they may
have done that,

01:47:30.370 --> 01:47:32.380
and accelerate social
change, although they

01:47:32.380 --> 01:47:33.610
may have done that.

01:47:33.610 --> 01:47:37.462
They set the system off in a
completely different direction.

01:47:37.462 --> 01:47:40.180
And it's important, if you look
at the economics of wartime

01:47:40.180 --> 01:47:42.580
and what happens
afterwards, is people never

01:47:42.580 --> 01:47:45.520
go back to the status quo ante.

01:47:45.520 --> 01:47:48.446
19 million women went to
work, as Doris mentioned.

01:47:48.446 --> 01:47:50.570
At the end of the war, they
went back a little bit,

01:47:50.570 --> 01:47:52.900
but very quickly the
employment of females

01:47:52.900 --> 01:47:54.880
was back up to 19 million.

01:47:54.880 --> 01:47:57.250
And today, if you go to
America's law schools

01:47:57.250 --> 01:47:59.860
and medical schools, you
will find that more than half

01:47:59.860 --> 01:48:01.840
of the students are female.

01:48:01.840 --> 01:48:04.210
That simply would
not have happened

01:48:04.210 --> 01:48:07.150
without the double shake of the
Great Depression and World War

01:48:07.150 --> 01:48:08.257
II.

01:48:08.257 --> 01:48:10.840
It's also important to remember
that, in the Great Depression,

01:48:10.840 --> 01:48:14.320
Karl Marx came very
close to being correct.

01:48:14.320 --> 01:48:15.820
We could be here
today in a world

01:48:15.820 --> 01:48:17.760
where capitalism does not exist.

01:48:17.760 --> 01:48:20.119
And it would be
very easy to happen.

01:48:20.119 --> 01:48:22.660
You remember that he predicted
that capitalism would collapse

01:48:22.660 --> 01:48:24.670
in a great financial crisis.

01:48:24.670 --> 01:48:25.810
He was right.

01:48:25.810 --> 01:48:30.190
Capitalism did collapse
in 1929 to 1932.

01:48:30.190 --> 01:48:32.590
And in December 1941,
when World War II

01:48:32.590 --> 01:48:35.140
began for the United
States, essentially

01:48:35.140 --> 01:48:37.210
there was one and a half
capitalist countries

01:48:37.210 --> 01:48:41.140
left in the world, the United
States and a gravely weakened,

01:48:41.140 --> 01:48:43.480
about to be defeated
Great Britain.

01:48:43.480 --> 01:48:47.800
Everybody else was a colony,
fascist, or communist.

01:48:47.800 --> 01:48:50.080
And they were that
for very good reasons.

01:48:50.080 --> 01:48:53.437
In 1939, there was not a
single shred of evidence

01:48:53.437 --> 01:48:56.020
that the United States would be
able to recover from the Great

01:48:56.020 --> 01:48:57.550
Depression.

01:48:57.550 --> 01:48:59.260
As Doris mentioned,
the unemployment rate

01:48:59.260 --> 01:49:04.300
was still 17%, and there
was no recovery light

01:49:04.300 --> 01:49:06.470
at the end of the tunnel.

01:49:06.470 --> 01:49:09.190
And the country was
becoming radicalized.

01:49:09.190 --> 01:49:11.530
The conservatives didn't like
it that Franklin Roosevelt

01:49:11.530 --> 01:49:15.760
was using the phrase "the
malefactors of great wealth."

01:49:15.760 --> 01:49:18.700
The little town that I
lived in in Montana in 1937

01:49:18.700 --> 01:49:20.680
elected a communist
city government,

01:49:20.680 --> 01:49:22.340
and they were not alone.

01:49:22.340 --> 01:49:23.980
It was a mining community.

01:49:23.980 --> 01:49:28.110
The radicals of the
wobblies were still there.

01:49:28.110 --> 01:49:33.330
With a few missteps in the
1930s and after World War II,

01:49:33.330 --> 01:49:37.032
we could be living in a
very different world today.

01:49:37.032 --> 01:49:39.490
And if you say, what did the
Great Depression and World War

01:49:39.490 --> 01:49:43.240
II do for us, it gave us a
completely different vision

01:49:43.240 --> 01:49:47.260
of what was important
and what was unimportant.

01:49:47.260 --> 01:49:50.290
In 1932, at the end of
the Hoover administration,

01:49:50.290 --> 01:49:52.660
the then head of the
Federal Reserve Board,

01:49:52.660 --> 01:49:55.330
appointed by Mr.
Hoover, stood up

01:49:55.330 --> 01:49:59.050
and, if you can imagine this, in
1932, when unemployment was 25%

01:49:59.050 --> 01:50:02.590
and prices were
falling by 10%, said

01:50:02.590 --> 01:50:07.790
the most important problem in
America is fighting inflation.

01:50:07.790 --> 01:50:11.770
Think about that.

01:50:11.770 --> 01:50:14.680
Because the problem is,
in normal circumstance,

01:50:14.680 --> 01:50:17.320
the past, and the
problems of the past,

01:50:17.320 --> 01:50:20.080
have a lock on the
present and the future.

01:50:20.080 --> 01:50:23.530
And what happens in wartime
is that lock is broken.

01:50:23.530 --> 01:50:26.524
And then the question is
what direction do we go.

01:50:26.524 --> 01:50:27.940
And that's the
place where I think

01:50:27.940 --> 01:50:31.990
Roosevelt and Churchill played
a remarkable role, because they

01:50:31.990 --> 01:50:35.890
didn't go with the flow,
they created the flow.

01:50:35.890 --> 01:50:37.997
Somebody once remarked,
how many times

01:50:37.997 --> 01:50:39.580
in the morning when
you woke up do you

01:50:39.580 --> 01:50:41.950
think that President Truman,
the first thing he did

01:50:41.950 --> 01:50:45.250
was consult a
public opinion poll?

01:50:45.250 --> 01:50:47.800
The answer is Harry
Truman never did that.

01:50:47.800 --> 01:50:49.520
[APPLAUSE]

01:50:49.520 --> 01:50:51.314
Because that's what
leadership's all about.

01:50:51.314 --> 01:50:53.230
And of course, the problem
in the modern world

01:50:53.230 --> 01:50:56.110
is every politician has
an easy alternative.

01:50:56.110 --> 01:50:59.320
Public opinion polls are so
good that you can basically

01:50:59.320 --> 01:51:01.792
figure out where the
public parade is going,

01:51:01.792 --> 01:51:04.000
jump to the front of it,
and look like a leader, when

01:51:04.000 --> 01:51:06.090
in fact you're a follower.

01:51:06.090 --> 01:51:08.500
But at this period of time,
partly because of the nature

01:51:08.500 --> 01:51:10.000
of the people leading
our countries,

01:51:10.000 --> 01:51:13.120
and partly because the public
opinion polls were not so good,

01:51:13.120 --> 01:51:14.950
you had to create the parade.

01:51:14.950 --> 01:51:16.690
Otherwise, it would
never be behind you.

01:51:16.690 --> 01:51:18.630
You couldn't find it.

01:51:18.630 --> 01:51:22.252
And the question is, what
kind of a parade was created?

01:51:22.252 --> 01:51:23.960
One of the interesting
things, of course,

01:51:23.960 --> 01:51:26.810
is we think of the United
States as a middle class nation.

01:51:26.810 --> 01:51:31.580
It wasn't a middle class
nation in 1936, '37, '38.

01:51:31.580 --> 01:51:34.160
It was a nation divided
into very poor people

01:51:34.160 --> 01:51:35.850
and very rich people.

01:51:35.850 --> 01:51:37.850
And it's important
to remember that.

01:51:37.850 --> 01:51:40.000
One of the things that
was done in World War II,

01:51:40.000 --> 01:51:42.520
you remember that we had
wage and price controls.

01:51:42.520 --> 01:51:44.270
The people running
wage and price controls

01:51:44.270 --> 01:51:46.220
had to set wages.

01:51:46.220 --> 01:51:47.480
What did they do?

01:51:47.480 --> 01:51:50.090
They felt, correctly, that,
since we were fighting

01:51:50.090 --> 01:51:52.460
in a war, and you were asking
12 million people to take

01:51:52.460 --> 01:51:56.690
the risk of dying, that was
inherently an egalitarian

01:51:56.690 --> 01:52:00.170
problem, and that therefore
they would very severely narrow

01:52:00.170 --> 01:52:02.330
wage differentials
as they were setting

01:52:02.330 --> 01:52:05.360
wages and prices in their
wage and price controls.

01:52:05.360 --> 01:52:06.426
And they did that.

01:52:06.426 --> 01:52:09.050
And the interesting thing about
those narrow wage differentials

01:52:09.050 --> 01:52:11.091
is, first of all, that
they maintained themselves

01:52:11.091 --> 01:52:12.800
after World War II.

01:52:12.800 --> 01:52:15.920
And secondly, if anything, they
contributed to productivity,

01:52:15.920 --> 01:52:18.170
as opposed to subtracting
from productivity,

01:52:18.170 --> 01:52:20.840
because before the war everybody
would have said, if you make

01:52:20.840 --> 01:52:23.550
wages that equal, everybody
will quit working.

01:52:23.550 --> 01:52:25.790
In fact, they worked
harder, they invested more

01:52:25.790 --> 01:52:28.370
in themselves, and they
became a better workforce

01:52:28.370 --> 01:52:31.370
with a more egalitarian wage
structure, rather than a worse

01:52:31.370 --> 01:52:33.480
workforce.

01:52:33.480 --> 01:52:38.430
Second thing we did is we said
that capitalism has a defect.

01:52:38.430 --> 01:52:40.340
We started to practice
Keynesian economics,

01:52:40.340 --> 01:52:42.673
because what we learned at
the beginning of World War II

01:52:42.673 --> 01:52:44.180
is, if you have a
Great Depression

01:52:44.180 --> 01:52:45.620
and the government
simply goes out

01:52:45.620 --> 01:52:47.450
and pushes demand
into the system,

01:52:47.450 --> 01:52:49.970
it is, in fact, possible
to bring everybody back

01:52:49.970 --> 01:52:51.050
to employment.

01:52:51.050 --> 01:52:52.760
And at the peak of
World War II, instead

01:52:52.760 --> 01:52:56.870
of having 25% of the population
unemployed, we had less than 1%

01:52:56.870 --> 01:52:59.380
of the population unemployed.

01:52:59.380 --> 01:53:02.590
We also learned in World
War II that science pays.

01:53:02.590 --> 01:53:04.720
That's what the
atomic bomb taught us.

01:53:04.720 --> 01:53:07.300
Long-run research pays
off, and it pays off

01:53:07.300 --> 01:53:08.890
in a very simple way.

01:53:08.890 --> 01:53:10.570
Harry Truman was
told that a million

01:53:10.570 --> 01:53:12.880
people would be casualties
if the Japanese islands were

01:53:12.880 --> 01:53:14.380
invaded.

01:53:14.380 --> 01:53:17.680
In fact, with technology,
none of those deaths

01:53:17.680 --> 01:53:19.990
were necessary on
the American side.

01:53:19.990 --> 01:53:22.630
Science, in some sense,
became the black box

01:53:22.630 --> 01:53:24.310
out of which good things came.

01:53:24.310 --> 01:53:27.280
And our support for science
came out of that effort,

01:53:27.280 --> 01:53:30.190
because it was
probably true, in 1939,

01:53:30.190 --> 01:53:33.220
if you were told as a scientist
that you had to give up

01:53:33.220 --> 01:53:36.490
your subscription to the German
Physical Review or the American

01:53:36.490 --> 01:53:39.250
Physical Review, you would
have given up your subscription

01:53:39.250 --> 01:53:42.911
to the American Physical Review,
because in the '20s and '30s,

01:53:42.911 --> 01:53:44.410
the leader of world
science was not,

01:53:44.410 --> 01:53:45.880
in fact, the United States.

01:53:45.880 --> 01:53:46.672
It was Germany.

01:53:46.672 --> 01:53:48.130
That's where graduate
students went

01:53:48.130 --> 01:53:50.290
to study if you
were really smart,

01:53:50.290 --> 01:53:52.445
not coming to the United States.

01:53:52.445 --> 01:53:54.070
We also learned in
World War II that it

01:53:54.070 --> 01:53:57.400
was possible to engineer
a rapidly growing economy.

01:53:57.400 --> 01:53:59.520
Growth was the goal.

01:53:59.520 --> 01:54:01.930
And we learned something
that has a double message.

01:54:01.930 --> 01:54:04.480
We learned you can collect
taxes and the world

01:54:04.480 --> 01:54:07.750
doesn't fall apart, because
it's important to remember

01:54:07.750 --> 01:54:10.990
that the tax system was
built in World War II.

01:54:10.990 --> 01:54:13.690
In the 1930s, the maximum
tax rate in the United States

01:54:13.690 --> 01:54:16.630
was 10%, and very
few people paid it.

01:54:16.630 --> 01:54:19.540
During World War II, the
maximum tax rate was 90%,

01:54:19.540 --> 01:54:23.270
and lots of people paid it, and
the world didn't fall apart.

01:54:23.270 --> 01:54:25.180
And what that allowed
us to do after the war

01:54:25.180 --> 01:54:26.900
was build the social
welfare state,

01:54:26.900 --> 01:54:29.510
which I'll come back
and talk to in a moment.

01:54:29.510 --> 01:54:31.230
So if you think
of the big picture

01:54:31.230 --> 01:54:32.860
that we learned in
World War II, we

01:54:32.860 --> 01:54:34.943
learned that you could
make a very different world

01:54:34.943 --> 01:54:37.920
work than the world
we had in the 1920s.

01:54:37.920 --> 01:54:39.700
And we also came out
of that experience,

01:54:39.700 --> 01:54:42.160
of course, if you had been
running the federal government

01:54:42.160 --> 01:54:47.740
in 1945, as the war was winding
down, what was your big worry?

01:54:47.740 --> 01:54:51.140
The Great Depression was
going to start again,

01:54:51.140 --> 01:54:54.560
because that was a real fear
inside the American government

01:54:54.560 --> 01:54:58.820
in 1945, that the war would
simply be a blip in the United

01:54:58.820 --> 01:55:01.790
States economy, and would
promptly collapse immediately

01:55:01.790 --> 01:55:04.446
after the war was over.

01:55:04.446 --> 01:55:06.820
Now, if you look at the vision
we had after World War II,

01:55:06.820 --> 01:55:09.450
it is a vision that came
out of World War II.

01:55:09.450 --> 01:55:12.130
And in some sense,
the last 50 years

01:55:12.130 --> 01:55:15.280
is, if you'd like to think of
it, the half life of World War

01:55:15.280 --> 01:55:16.510
II.

01:55:16.510 --> 01:55:18.421
It's a vision that we lived on.

01:55:18.421 --> 01:55:20.170
And as I shall point
out in a few minutes,

01:55:20.170 --> 01:55:22.270
it's a vision which
has now dissolved,

01:55:22.270 --> 01:55:25.860
and we're going to be asked
to create a new division.

01:55:25.860 --> 01:55:27.810
What was that
vision made out of?

01:55:27.810 --> 01:55:30.450
First of all, the vision for
the post World War II economy

01:55:30.450 --> 01:55:33.240
was made out of the experience
in World War II is we can

01:55:33.240 --> 01:55:36.460
and we will run a full
employment economy.

01:55:36.460 --> 01:55:38.700
Government will not
allow the economy

01:55:38.700 --> 01:55:41.940
to slide into a Great Depression
with a 25% unemployment

01:55:41.940 --> 01:55:43.777
rate and financial collapses.

01:55:43.777 --> 01:55:45.360
And if you look at
the recessions that

01:55:45.360 --> 01:55:46.920
occurred after World
War II until we

01:55:46.920 --> 01:55:49.410
get into the 1990s,
each and every time

01:55:49.410 --> 01:55:51.690
you see government
running to the rescue.

01:55:51.690 --> 01:55:55.470
These same thing's true with
incipient financial crises.

01:55:55.470 --> 01:56:00.180
The stock market crash of 1987
was not allowed to continue.

01:56:00.180 --> 01:56:02.640
In the foreign policy,
area we essentially shifted

01:56:02.640 --> 01:56:04.200
from the fascists
to the communists,

01:56:04.200 --> 01:56:06.880
and we instituted a
policy of containment.

01:56:06.880 --> 01:56:08.160
We remembered Munich.

01:56:08.160 --> 01:56:11.940
We remembered that you
have to contain aggressors.

01:56:11.940 --> 01:56:13.680
At the end of World
War II, you remember

01:56:13.680 --> 01:56:17.160
that people sat around expecting
economic growth to occur

01:56:17.160 --> 01:56:19.230
and capitalism to
reestablish itself

01:56:19.230 --> 01:56:22.080
in countries like France,
Germany, Great Britain, where

01:56:22.080 --> 01:56:23.910
either it had been
severely weakened

01:56:23.910 --> 01:56:26.460
or it had not established--
had been run out of business

01:56:26.460 --> 01:56:28.170
by the fascists earlier.

01:56:28.170 --> 01:56:29.940
It didn't happen.

01:56:29.940 --> 01:56:33.300
In 1948, three years after
the war was over in Europe,

01:56:33.300 --> 01:56:35.220
there was no recovery
in France, there

01:56:35.220 --> 01:56:38.160
was no recovery in Germany,
and the British economy

01:56:38.160 --> 01:56:40.670
was, in fact, an
incipient collapse.

01:56:40.670 --> 01:56:42.720
And it was the
incipient collapse

01:56:42.720 --> 01:56:44.910
of the British economy
that led to the formation

01:56:44.910 --> 01:56:48.720
of the Marshall Plan, of putting
capitalism back together again,

01:56:48.720 --> 01:56:50.520
because we came
to the conclusion

01:56:50.520 --> 01:56:52.440
that it wouldn't
happen automatically

01:56:52.440 --> 01:56:54.790
by spontaneous combustion.

01:56:54.790 --> 01:56:58.680
Now, today, Americans, in
real dollar terms per capita,

01:56:58.680 --> 01:57:02.970
are two and a half times as
wealthy as they were in 1948.

01:57:02.970 --> 01:57:06.420
But think about what we
were willing to do in 1948.

01:57:06.420 --> 01:57:09.480
In 1948, we put 3%
of the American GNP

01:57:09.480 --> 01:57:11.190
into the Marshall Plan.

01:57:11.190 --> 01:57:12.960
In today's dollars,
that would mean

01:57:12.960 --> 01:57:17.906
giving foreign aid of more
than $200 billion a year.

01:57:17.906 --> 01:57:19.530
What do you think we
Americans actually

01:57:19.530 --> 01:57:22.650
do in terms of economic
foreign aid today?

01:57:22.650 --> 01:57:23.700
Less than $7 billion.

01:57:23.700 --> 01:57:26.910
For all practical
purposes, nothing.

01:57:26.910 --> 01:57:30.000
We give $8 or $9 billion worth
of military aid, but almost

01:57:30.000 --> 01:57:32.910
no economic foreign aid.

01:57:32.910 --> 01:57:37.500
World War II led to the end
of the colonial empires.

01:57:37.500 --> 01:57:40.830
When I was a student in the
'40s and '50s in school,

01:57:40.830 --> 01:57:42.630
they still had the
old maps where,

01:57:42.630 --> 01:57:44.160
other than Europe
and North America,

01:57:44.160 --> 01:57:46.250
everything was
either green or red--

01:57:46.250 --> 01:57:48.180
red if you were in
the British Empire,

01:57:48.180 --> 01:57:50.322
green if you were in
the French Empire.

01:57:50.322 --> 01:57:51.780
If it hadn't been
for World War II,

01:57:51.780 --> 01:57:53.730
those empires might
be there today.

01:57:53.730 --> 01:57:57.240
They certainly would have lasted
a much longer period of time.

01:57:57.240 --> 01:57:59.550
It's interesting,
the whole concept

01:57:59.550 --> 01:58:02.970
that poor people can get rich
came out of World War II.

01:58:02.970 --> 01:58:06.990
The word foreign aid
never left anybody's lips

01:58:06.990 --> 01:58:10.410
before World War II, because
the purpose of the Third World

01:58:10.410 --> 01:58:12.180
was to make the
First World rich.

01:58:12.180 --> 01:58:15.897
India was supposed to make
Britain rich, not vice versa.

01:58:15.897 --> 01:58:17.730
Now, there's a big
argument among historians

01:58:17.730 --> 01:58:19.854
about whether the British
put more money into India

01:58:19.854 --> 01:58:21.840
or took more money out
of India, but there

01:58:21.840 --> 01:58:24.450
is no argument about what the
British were trying to do.

01:58:24.450 --> 01:58:26.950
[LAUGHTER]

01:58:26.950 --> 01:58:30.300
[APPLAUSE]

01:58:30.300 --> 01:58:34.160
The purpose was to take
Third World countries

01:58:34.160 --> 01:58:36.410
and use them to
make yourself rich.

01:58:36.410 --> 01:58:39.470
And the whole concept that
everybody in the world

01:58:39.470 --> 01:58:42.890
could, in fact, become rich, and
that the rich people might even

01:58:42.890 --> 01:58:44.930
help them a little
bit to become rich

01:58:44.930 --> 01:58:47.790
was something that came
out of this period of time.

01:58:47.790 --> 01:58:50.300
The same thing is true of
the social welfare state.

01:58:50.300 --> 01:58:51.830
And whatever its
current problems

01:58:51.830 --> 01:58:53.870
is, what the social
welfare state basically

01:58:53.870 --> 01:58:56.360
said is, when you're
sick, when you're old,

01:58:56.360 --> 01:58:58.100
when you're unemployed,
you will not

01:58:58.100 --> 01:59:00.200
be thrown off the ship of state.

01:59:00.200 --> 01:59:02.330
You will be allowed to live.

01:59:02.330 --> 01:59:05.810
Because there were people prior
to this time, and people now,

01:59:05.810 --> 01:59:07.400
who are basically
practicing what

01:59:07.400 --> 01:59:10.230
is called survival of
the fittest capitalism.

01:59:10.230 --> 01:59:11.960
And I'll remind you
a bit of history.

01:59:11.960 --> 01:59:15.680
Darwin did not invent the
phrase survival of the fittest.

01:59:15.680 --> 01:59:17.810
It was invented by a
19th century economist

01:59:17.810 --> 01:59:19.820
by the name of
Spencer, who believed

01:59:19.820 --> 01:59:22.580
it was the duty of
the economically fit

01:59:22.580 --> 01:59:25.580
to drive the economically
unfit into starvation

01:59:25.580 --> 01:59:27.770
and out of the economy.

01:59:27.770 --> 01:59:30.440
Spencer is having
a revival today,

01:59:30.440 --> 01:59:34.070
but he was not popular in
the 1940s and the 1950s,

01:59:34.070 --> 01:59:36.920
because people remembered
the '30s and the '40s,

01:59:36.920 --> 01:59:40.130
and they remembered that too
many people would be thrown off

01:59:40.130 --> 01:59:43.160
the ship when they became
old, when they became sick,

01:59:43.160 --> 01:59:45.410
when they became unemployed.

01:59:45.410 --> 01:59:48.500
We also had a decision to make
at the time of the Marshall

01:59:48.500 --> 01:59:49.430
Plan.

01:59:49.430 --> 01:59:53.960
Does the United States want to
retreat back into isolationism,

01:59:53.960 --> 01:59:57.734
or does the United States want
to create a world economy.

01:59:57.734 --> 01:59:59.900
Partly because of the
challenge of the Soviet Union,

01:59:59.900 --> 02:00:02.840
where there was a world
vision of communism conquering

02:00:02.840 --> 02:00:07.080
the world, the United States was
forced to become international.

02:00:07.080 --> 02:00:10.760
And in 1944, before the World
War was over, up here north

02:00:10.760 --> 02:00:12.320
in Bretton Woods,
New Hampshire we

02:00:12.320 --> 02:00:13.934
created the GATT
Bretton Woods system,

02:00:13.934 --> 02:00:16.100
which is the system we've
lived with for the last 50

02:00:16.100 --> 02:00:17.540
years in the world.

02:00:17.540 --> 02:00:20.990
That was a system which said
that the great open market

02:00:20.990 --> 02:00:23.800
for the world would
be the United States.

02:00:23.800 --> 02:00:25.640
And every single
country in the world

02:00:25.640 --> 02:00:27.656
that's become rich
in the last 50 years

02:00:27.656 --> 02:00:29.030
has gone through
a period of time

02:00:29.030 --> 02:00:32.510
when at least half their exports
came to the United States.

02:00:32.510 --> 02:00:36.610
That was true of Europe in
the '50s, Japan in the '60s,

02:00:36.610 --> 02:00:39.380
and in the '80s that was
true of Korea, Taiwan, Hong

02:00:39.380 --> 02:00:41.600
Kong, Singapore,
and in the '90s it's

02:00:41.600 --> 02:00:44.390
true of communist
China, the PRC.

02:00:44.390 --> 02:00:47.052
We would be the
locomotive for the world.

02:00:47.052 --> 02:00:49.010
If the world economy
slowed down and threatened

02:00:49.010 --> 02:00:51.860
to slip into a recession,
it was our responsibility

02:00:51.860 --> 02:00:54.230
to engineer a recovery.

02:00:54.230 --> 02:00:56.300
We would be the
manager for the system,

02:00:56.300 --> 02:00:58.703
taking responsibility
to give it leadership.

02:01:02.432 --> 02:01:04.140
One of the problems,
of course, with this

02:01:04.140 --> 02:01:07.440
is we sold a whole set of
things as anticommunism,

02:01:07.440 --> 02:01:09.570
and then the question
is, do you buy them

02:01:09.570 --> 02:01:12.000
when communism goes away?

02:01:12.000 --> 02:01:14.040
One of the things we
sold as anticommunism

02:01:14.040 --> 02:01:16.800
was basically creating
the middle class.

02:01:16.800 --> 02:01:19.840
The GI Bill was central in
creating the middle class.

02:01:19.840 --> 02:01:23.490
As I mentioned, there was no
middle class in the 1930s.

02:01:23.490 --> 02:01:25.050
The wage and price
controls of World

02:01:25.050 --> 02:01:27.000
War II and setting a
new structure of wages

02:01:27.000 --> 02:01:30.100
was essential in committing
the middle class.

02:01:30.100 --> 02:01:32.250
And with a combination
of unionization

02:01:32.250 --> 02:01:34.110
and social pressure,
we basically

02:01:34.110 --> 02:01:36.000
created what I would
call the post World War

02:01:36.000 --> 02:01:39.510
II social contract between
business and labor.

02:01:39.510 --> 02:01:41.010
And what that
social contract said

02:01:41.010 --> 02:01:43.680
is, if you're running a
company, and your profits go up

02:01:43.680 --> 02:01:46.130
and your sales go up, you
will share some of that gain

02:01:46.130 --> 02:01:47.196
with your employees.

02:01:50.820 --> 02:01:54.200
That's what created
the middle class.

02:01:54.200 --> 02:01:57.220
We also sold investment
as anticommunism.

02:01:57.220 --> 02:01:59.470
It's important to remember
that the interstate highway

02:01:59.470 --> 02:02:02.230
system wasn't sold
as a highway system,

02:02:02.230 --> 02:02:03.836
it was sold as a
system necessary

02:02:03.836 --> 02:02:05.710
because we were going
to move mobile missiles

02:02:05.710 --> 02:02:07.584
around the United States,
and all the bridges

02:02:07.584 --> 02:02:10.510
had to be high enough to get
the missile trucks under them.

02:02:10.510 --> 02:02:14.080
And if you go back and read the
original legislation in 1957,

02:02:14.080 --> 02:02:16.360
the words national
defense appear in the act

02:02:16.360 --> 02:02:18.740
for the interstate highways.

02:02:18.740 --> 02:02:21.790
It was already mentioned the
National Defense Education Act.

02:02:21.790 --> 02:02:24.340
Many of us who got
our PhDs in the 1960s

02:02:24.340 --> 02:02:27.410
got them on the National
Defense Education Act.

02:02:27.410 --> 02:02:30.260
The space program, one of
the most exciting things

02:02:30.260 --> 02:02:32.120
human beings have
ever done, had to be

02:02:32.120 --> 02:02:35.269
justified as a military
race with the Russians.

02:02:35.269 --> 02:02:37.310
And we probably wouldn't
have played in that race

02:02:37.310 --> 02:02:39.170
unless we thought it
was a military race

02:02:39.170 --> 02:02:40.790
with the Russians.

02:02:40.790 --> 02:02:43.820
We sold internationalism
as anticommunism.

02:02:43.820 --> 02:02:47.210
That was why it was
correct for Americans

02:02:47.210 --> 02:02:49.190
to intervene
elsewhere in the world

02:02:49.190 --> 02:02:53.900
to try and preserve democracy
and keep communism out.

02:02:53.900 --> 02:02:55.430
And so if you look
at World War II,

02:02:55.430 --> 02:02:59.150
I would argue to you that it
had the most profound effects

02:02:59.150 --> 02:03:01.970
on the American social
and economic system,

02:03:01.970 --> 02:03:05.827
and it had a half life of about
50 years, where we are today.

02:03:05.827 --> 02:03:07.410
Because where we are
today, of course,

02:03:07.410 --> 02:03:11.150
is with the implosion of
the Soviet Union in 1991,

02:03:11.150 --> 02:03:14.000
and the fall of the
Berlin Wall in 1989,

02:03:14.000 --> 02:03:16.490
is all of the anchor
points have gone.

02:03:16.490 --> 02:03:18.630
There is no enemy out there.

02:03:18.630 --> 02:03:21.260
The United States is
threatened by no one.

02:03:21.260 --> 02:03:23.530
There is no military
threat to us.

02:03:23.530 --> 02:03:25.700
And the question is, in
this brand-new world,

02:03:25.700 --> 02:03:28.190
what is our vision, and
what do we want to do?

02:03:28.190 --> 02:03:29.660
The one third of
humanity who used

02:03:29.660 --> 02:03:32.210
to live in the communist
world has basically

02:03:32.210 --> 02:03:35.430
said we want to join you.

02:03:35.430 --> 02:03:37.940
And of course, the problem
is that, when they join us,

02:03:37.940 --> 02:03:40.220
it isn't just they that
will have to change,

02:03:40.220 --> 02:03:43.730
it is partly we who
will have to change.

02:03:43.730 --> 02:03:46.120
In many parts of the world,
it's important to understand,

02:03:46.120 --> 02:03:49.550
in the post World War II,
borders move very seldom,

02:03:49.550 --> 02:03:51.710
because the one thing
America and the Soviet Union

02:03:51.710 --> 02:03:54.230
could agree on is never move
a border between any two

02:03:54.230 --> 02:03:57.740
countries, because it was too
likely that one was our ally,

02:03:57.740 --> 02:03:59.150
the other was
their ally, whoever

02:03:59.150 --> 02:04:01.440
would lose would call
his big brother to help,

02:04:01.440 --> 02:04:03.398
and there was this fear
that the missiles would

02:04:03.398 --> 02:04:06.830
fly from Moscow to Washington
if you moved a border in Africa.

02:04:06.830 --> 02:04:09.590
In most of human
history, borders move.

02:04:09.590 --> 02:04:11.360
And we're entering
an era where borders

02:04:11.360 --> 02:04:13.280
are going to move everywhere.

02:04:13.280 --> 02:04:15.290
15 countries in the
old Soviet Union,

02:04:15.290 --> 02:04:17.180
seven countries in
the old Yugoslavia,

02:04:17.180 --> 02:04:20.210
two countries in the
old Czechoslovakia.

02:04:20.210 --> 02:04:22.100
Every border in Africa
is going to move,

02:04:22.100 --> 02:04:24.410
because they're simply where the
British and French armies met

02:04:24.410 --> 02:04:25.826
in the colonial
era, and they make

02:04:25.826 --> 02:04:28.160
no sense in terms of
economics, no sense in terms

02:04:28.160 --> 02:04:31.010
of ethnic or language results.

02:04:31.010 --> 02:04:33.590
India is a big country,
which of course has never

02:04:33.590 --> 02:04:35.270
been one country,
except when conquered

02:04:35.270 --> 02:04:38.099
by an outsider invader,
the Moguls or the British.

02:04:38.099 --> 02:04:39.890
And it's already split
into three countries

02:04:39.890 --> 02:04:41.609
and threatening to
split into more.

02:04:41.609 --> 02:04:43.400
And even north of our
border, we may end up

02:04:43.400 --> 02:04:47.990
with two countries, Quebec
and the rest of Canada.

02:04:47.990 --> 02:04:50.520
We have a very simple problem.

02:04:50.520 --> 02:04:54.290
The problem is the post
World War II period is over.

02:04:54.290 --> 02:04:56.630
The impetus of World
War II is over.

02:04:56.630 --> 02:04:59.780
The question is, what is
our vision for the future,

02:04:59.780 --> 02:05:02.662
and can we march
to get that vision?

02:05:02.662 --> 02:05:04.370
When I was dean of
the management school,

02:05:04.370 --> 02:05:06.890
I always used to
tell our students

02:05:06.890 --> 02:05:10.410
that it's very easy to
be a manager in a crisis,

02:05:10.410 --> 02:05:12.310
because it's in a crisis
it's obvious you've

02:05:12.310 --> 02:05:14.189
got to do something.

02:05:14.189 --> 02:05:15.730
It's not obvious
what you have to do,

02:05:15.730 --> 02:05:17.604
but that's usually pretty
easy to figure out.

02:05:17.604 --> 02:05:20.370
The hard thing is to persuade
people to do something.

02:05:20.370 --> 02:05:22.750
And the crisis does it for you.

02:05:22.750 --> 02:05:25.380
The problem at the moment
is there is no crisis.

02:05:25.380 --> 02:05:27.580
The question is,
what is the vision?

02:05:27.580 --> 02:05:30.234
And the answer is we don't
know what the vision is.

02:05:30.234 --> 02:05:31.650
And that's our
problem, of course,

02:05:31.650 --> 02:05:33.892
in Bosnia, Rwanda,
Somalia, and lots

02:05:33.892 --> 02:05:35.100
of other places in the world.

02:05:35.100 --> 02:05:36.986
Does this call for
an American response

02:05:36.986 --> 02:05:38.610
and American leadership,
or does it not

02:05:38.610 --> 02:05:41.634
call for an American response
and American leadership?

02:05:41.634 --> 02:05:43.050
And you can't
answer that question

02:05:43.050 --> 02:05:44.917
until you know
what the vision is.

02:05:44.917 --> 02:05:47.250
The same thing is true when
you come to American science

02:05:47.250 --> 02:05:47.860
policy.

02:05:47.860 --> 02:05:49.260
What's the vision?

02:05:49.260 --> 02:05:51.390
Does America want to be
the world's science leader,

02:05:51.390 --> 02:05:53.640
or does it not want to be
the world's science leader?

02:05:53.640 --> 02:05:58.670
What are the benefits and the
costs of those two options?

02:05:58.670 --> 02:06:01.010
And in some sense, I
would argue that today's

02:06:01.010 --> 02:06:04.160
political leadership has
a much more tough task

02:06:04.160 --> 02:06:07.010
than those in the
1930s and the 1940s,

02:06:07.010 --> 02:06:08.780
because there is no crisis.

02:06:08.780 --> 02:06:11.690
Can we change without
a crisis, and can we

02:06:11.690 --> 02:06:14.069
create that new vision
without a crisis?

02:06:14.069 --> 02:06:15.860
And that's what we're
going to be tested on

02:06:15.860 --> 02:06:18.560
in the next 10 to 15 years.

02:06:18.560 --> 02:06:21.110
At the height of
imperial Rome, one

02:06:21.110 --> 02:06:22.970
of the people who lived
in the Roman Empire

02:06:22.970 --> 02:06:26.752
but was not a Roman said the
following about the Romans.

02:06:26.752 --> 02:06:29.470
"The Romans are not the
smartest in the world.

02:06:29.470 --> 02:06:31.690
The Greeks are smarter.

02:06:31.690 --> 02:06:34.010
The Romans are not the
most numerous in the world.

02:06:34.010 --> 02:06:36.490
The Spanish are more numerous.

02:06:36.490 --> 02:06:38.320
The Romans are
not the strongest.

02:06:38.320 --> 02:06:39.955
The Germans are stronger."

02:06:39.955 --> 02:06:41.980
"The Romans are
not the wealthiest.

02:06:41.980 --> 02:06:44.530
The North Africans
are wealthier.

02:06:44.530 --> 02:06:47.680
The Romans have not a single
piece of military equipment

02:06:47.680 --> 02:06:51.910
that every army in that
world doesn't also have.

02:06:51.910 --> 02:06:54.550
The Romans have one
and only one virtue--

02:06:54.550 --> 02:06:58.540
they have a vision, and the
ability to march toward it."

02:06:58.540 --> 02:07:01.990
The question is, do we have
a vision and the ability

02:07:01.990 --> 02:07:02.811
to march toward it?

02:07:02.811 --> 02:07:04.284
Thank you very much.

02:07:04.284 --> 02:07:08.212
[APPLAUSE]

02:07:27.900 --> 02:07:31.170
VEST: Not only do I have
the unenviable task of being

02:07:31.170 --> 02:07:35.850
sandwiched between that least
dismal of all economists

02:07:35.850 --> 02:07:39.430
and a flyby of World War II
aircraft, which, by the way,

02:07:39.430 --> 02:07:42.660
has been postponed a few
minutes, so don't fear,

02:07:42.660 --> 02:07:44.820
but I also have
the uncanny feeling

02:07:44.820 --> 02:07:46.650
that Dr. Goodwin
has been listening

02:07:46.650 --> 02:07:51.440
in to the dinner conversation
at the Vest household.

02:07:51.440 --> 02:07:57.060
Males driving endlessly around
for fear of asking directions?

02:07:57.060 --> 02:07:57.680
Imagine that.

02:07:57.680 --> 02:08:00.030
[LAUGHTER]

02:08:03.320 --> 02:08:06.050
Technology Day is
traditionally a showcase

02:08:06.050 --> 02:08:10.100
for cutting-edge work of this
university, a great institution

02:08:10.100 --> 02:08:12.830
dedicated to shaping the future.

02:08:12.830 --> 02:08:14.420
This morning,
however, you have been

02:08:14.420 --> 02:08:19.070
invited to look back, to think
about the intersection of MIT

02:08:19.070 --> 02:08:22.160
with the world's
most cataclysmic war,

02:08:22.160 --> 02:08:25.640
and to ponder the reverberations
of that [? collision ?]

02:08:25.640 --> 02:08:28.340
over the past half century.

02:08:28.340 --> 02:08:30.410
This wide-angle
focus, this attention

02:08:30.410 --> 02:08:33.980
to the lessons of history
could not be more timely,

02:08:33.980 --> 02:08:37.390
as has just been pointed out.

02:08:37.390 --> 02:08:43.000
The years represented by the
classes of 1945 and of 1970

02:08:43.000 --> 02:08:45.710
were periods when universities
were integral players

02:08:45.710 --> 02:08:48.510
in a tumultuous world
and national events

02:08:48.510 --> 02:08:52.600
that modified our agenda
and transformed our nature.

02:08:52.600 --> 02:08:55.420
It appears to me that the
closing years of this century

02:08:55.420 --> 02:08:58.180
will be another such
era, a time when

02:08:58.180 --> 02:09:00.250
remaining aloof from
the public arena

02:09:00.250 --> 02:09:03.790
will be neither
appropriate nor possible.

02:09:03.790 --> 02:09:06.070
The relationship between
the American people

02:09:06.070 --> 02:09:08.230
and their government
is changing.

02:09:08.230 --> 02:09:10.750
Partnerships of every
kind between branches

02:09:10.750 --> 02:09:13.990
of the federal government
and involved constituencies

02:09:13.990 --> 02:09:16.450
are coming under
the budgetary axe.

02:09:16.450 --> 02:09:20.940
And that most certainly includes
our research universities.

02:09:20.940 --> 02:09:23.330
It's ironic that the threat
to our research effort

02:09:23.330 --> 02:09:26.330
and to our ability to
provide affordable education

02:09:26.330 --> 02:09:29.570
to an undiminished
stream of gifted students

02:09:29.570 --> 02:09:32.480
comes at a time when
tremendous breakthroughs are

02:09:32.480 --> 02:09:37.210
within grasp in countless areas
of science and technology.

02:09:37.210 --> 02:09:41.160
The spirit of the
Radiation Lab is not dead.

02:09:41.160 --> 02:09:43.140
It is the thought
of opportunities

02:09:43.140 --> 02:09:47.170
that may be missed
that fuels our concern.

02:09:47.170 --> 02:09:50.420
The parties in power in the
White House and on Capitol Hill

02:09:50.420 --> 02:09:53.870
have changed many times
since World War II.

02:09:53.870 --> 02:09:55.730
The concern of the
university community

02:09:55.730 --> 02:09:58.730
is not so much which
party holds the reins,

02:09:58.730 --> 02:10:00.980
but whether Washington
decision-makers have

02:10:00.980 --> 02:10:03.620
the political will
and insight to serve

02:10:03.620 --> 02:10:08.510
society's long-term need for
new knowledge, new technologies,

02:10:08.510 --> 02:10:13.090
and, above all, for superbly
educated young men and women.

02:10:13.090 --> 02:10:16.080
So my message this
morning will be twofold,

02:10:16.080 --> 02:10:19.455
that MIT's future can be just
as exciting and adventurous

02:10:19.455 --> 02:10:24.090
as in the past, and that real
and immediate threats could

02:10:24.090 --> 02:10:27.310
cause us to fall far short.

02:10:27.310 --> 02:10:30.820
First, let's look at some
indicators of excellence.

02:10:30.820 --> 02:10:34.000
MIT continues to recruit the
most gifted and committed

02:10:34.000 --> 02:10:35.660
young faculty.

02:10:35.660 --> 02:10:38.240
Their work is of the first rank.

02:10:38.240 --> 02:10:40.670
They've transformed our
knowledge of the oceans,

02:10:40.670 --> 02:10:43.220
created the first
self-replicating synthetic

02:10:43.220 --> 02:10:47.480
molecule, observed the wave
function of a single atom,

02:10:47.480 --> 02:10:51.980
gained deep insight into the
pervasive tumor suppressor gene

02:10:51.980 --> 02:10:56.120
notated P53, and created
the worldwide web

02:10:56.120 --> 02:10:58.190
that has electronically
connected people

02:10:58.190 --> 02:11:01.070
and information throughout
the world in a simply

02:11:01.070 --> 02:11:03.200
astounding manner.

02:11:03.200 --> 02:11:06.410
The excitement of such work
informs their teaching,

02:11:06.410 --> 02:11:09.410
and it certainly
engages their students,

02:11:09.410 --> 02:11:12.020
which is not to say that we
have achieved all our goals

02:11:12.020 --> 02:11:14.210
with respect to the faculty.

02:11:14.210 --> 02:11:16.910
We still have difficulty
hiring the numbers of women

02:11:16.910 --> 02:11:18.950
and minority faculty
that we need to better

02:11:18.950 --> 02:11:22.280
reflect and serve
a changing America,

02:11:22.280 --> 02:11:25.010
and our efforts to support
a greater portion of faculty

02:11:25.010 --> 02:11:27.770
salaries from General
Institute funds

02:11:27.770 --> 02:11:31.040
so that they are less dependent
on the fluctuating research

02:11:31.040 --> 02:11:34.070
support still involve
a lot of heavy lifting

02:11:34.070 --> 02:11:36.660
in the fundraising arena.

02:11:36.660 --> 02:11:39.460
The second indicator of
our promise, of course,

02:11:39.460 --> 02:11:42.660
is the most important,
namely our students.

02:11:42.660 --> 02:11:45.960
Applications for admission
to our undergraduate programs

02:11:45.960 --> 02:11:50.850
have increased by 25%
during the last two years.

02:11:50.850 --> 02:11:53.040
The attraction of
an MIT education

02:11:53.040 --> 02:11:54.840
has never been greater.

02:11:54.840 --> 02:11:57.660
In fact, we were caught
off-guard by a sudden increase

02:11:57.660 --> 02:11:59.340
in the fraction of
students admitted

02:11:59.340 --> 02:12:03.340
to this year's freshman class
who've accepted our offer.

02:12:03.340 --> 02:12:05.820
Response is so great,
we are likely to end up

02:12:05.820 --> 02:12:08.640
with a bit of crowding in
our dormitories in the fall.

02:12:08.640 --> 02:12:11.292
But that's a high-class problem.

02:12:11.292 --> 02:12:13.290
And the third
indicator is the spirit

02:12:13.290 --> 02:12:15.870
of innovation and
inventiveness that permeates

02:12:15.870 --> 02:12:20.250
our academic programs.

02:12:20.250 --> 02:12:22.470
We have led the nation,
as Paul pointed out,

02:12:22.470 --> 02:12:25.830
in designing new five-year
integrated bachelor, masters

02:12:25.830 --> 02:12:28.920
engineering curricula,
thereby defining

02:12:28.920 --> 02:12:31.410
a new master of
engineering degree,

02:12:31.410 --> 02:12:35.060
as the first professional
engineering degree.

02:12:35.060 --> 02:12:37.000
The Sloan School's
MBA curriculum

02:12:37.000 --> 02:12:40.120
has been fundamentally
restructured, restructured

02:12:40.120 --> 02:12:43.840
for a new era of global,
technologically intensive

02:12:43.840 --> 02:12:46.570
business organizations.

02:12:46.570 --> 02:12:48.880
Implementations will
begin this September

02:12:48.880 --> 02:12:51.070
of a radical new
pedagogical approach

02:12:51.070 --> 02:12:55.170
to undergraduate education
in mechanical engineering.

02:12:55.170 --> 02:12:57.980
We've just established
molecular and cell biology

02:12:57.980 --> 02:13:01.430
as a core degree requirement,
and the faculty recently

02:13:01.430 --> 02:13:04.310
approved our first truly
interdisciplinary minor

02:13:04.310 --> 02:13:08.180
program, one in
biomedical engineering.

02:13:08.180 --> 02:13:12.380
New information technology,
such as computer simulation,

02:13:12.380 --> 02:13:17.590
interactive CD-ROMs, hypertext
educational materials,

02:13:17.590 --> 02:13:19.940
and instructional uses
of the worldwide web

02:13:19.940 --> 02:13:22.670
are being applied in subjects
ranging from language

02:13:22.670 --> 02:13:25.640
learning to astronomy.

02:13:25.640 --> 02:13:30.080
If MIT is to remain a
world-class institution,

02:13:30.080 --> 02:13:33.350
this inventiveness,
intellectual leadership,

02:13:33.350 --> 02:13:37.400
and absolute excellence
are necessary conditions,

02:13:37.400 --> 02:13:41.440
but they are no longer
sufficient ones.

02:13:41.440 --> 02:13:43.130
The present threat
to the viability

02:13:43.130 --> 02:13:46.070
of our national research
effort did not suddenly

02:13:46.070 --> 02:13:49.040
burst upon the
scene last November.

02:13:49.040 --> 02:13:52.440
For the last decade,
as Paul has discussed,

02:13:52.440 --> 02:13:56.970
forces have been chipping away
at the partnership created

02:13:56.970 --> 02:13:59.350
by Vannevar Bush.

02:13:59.350 --> 02:14:02.530
The process was accelerated by
the end of the Cold War, whose

02:14:02.530 --> 02:14:06.280
military needs and culture
of superpower competition

02:14:06.280 --> 02:14:09.910
had created a climate of
support for advanced education

02:14:09.910 --> 02:14:12.010
and for research.

02:14:12.010 --> 02:14:15.970
We won a cold peace, but the
supportive climate for research

02:14:15.970 --> 02:14:18.660
universities has eroded.

02:14:18.660 --> 02:14:21.840
And then, on the economic
front, global competition

02:14:21.840 --> 02:14:23.910
hit us like a ton of bricks.

02:14:23.910 --> 02:14:26.610
In a span of 50 years,
the United States

02:14:26.610 --> 02:14:29.040
has moved from producing
more than half the world's

02:14:29.040 --> 02:14:34.170
gross domestic product to
producing just about 20%.

02:14:34.170 --> 02:14:37.410
The drop was as much a
blow to the national psyche

02:14:37.410 --> 02:14:40.640
as it was to the
national balance sheet.

02:14:40.640 --> 02:14:42.830
There were many factors
behind the problems

02:14:42.830 --> 02:14:44.740
in American industry.

02:14:44.740 --> 02:14:46.760
The university
certainly must accept

02:14:46.760 --> 02:14:49.010
their share of the blame.

02:14:49.010 --> 02:14:51.740
In our academic
programs, we forgot

02:14:51.740 --> 02:14:55.990
to pay enough attention to
process, to manufacturing,

02:14:55.990 --> 02:14:59.960
to improving product quality,
to systems perspectives.

02:14:59.960 --> 02:15:02.060
Our engineering schools
grew too distant

02:15:02.060 --> 02:15:04.940
from the realities of
industrial practice.

02:15:04.940 --> 02:15:07.400
Our management schools
forgot to pay attention

02:15:07.400 --> 02:15:11.420
to production, and to the
understanding of customers.

02:15:11.420 --> 02:15:14.000
At the same time, our
costs and our tuitions

02:15:14.000 --> 02:15:16.820
were growing out of control.

02:15:16.820 --> 02:15:19.550
This year, we began
moving aggressively

02:15:19.550 --> 02:15:22.190
to improve our own customer
service, if you will,

02:15:22.190 --> 02:15:25.580
and to begin to re-engineer
ourselves into a leaner, more

02:15:25.580 --> 02:15:27.470
modern enterprise.

02:15:27.470 --> 02:15:31.620
But we were too long getting
off the starting blocks.

02:15:31.620 --> 02:15:34.860
Outside our walls, more
trouble was brewing.

02:15:34.860 --> 02:15:36.840
As the economy
became stressed, we

02:15:36.840 --> 02:15:39.420
witnessed the development
of a populist distrust

02:15:39.420 --> 02:15:43.650
of institutions and groups
that aspired to excellence.

02:15:43.650 --> 02:15:47.640
Elite became a pejorative term.

02:15:47.640 --> 02:15:50.180
Gone was the spirit
that infused the nation

02:15:50.180 --> 02:15:53.270
in the aftermath of
Sputnik, and that sense

02:15:53.270 --> 02:15:56.180
that we will not be outdone.

02:15:56.180 --> 02:15:59.900
Communities that once were proud
of their educational systems

02:15:59.900 --> 02:16:02.720
dumped society's
problems at the feet

02:16:02.720 --> 02:16:06.020
of their ill-prepared teachers
and shamelessly sliced

02:16:06.020 --> 02:16:08.540
into the muscle and
bone of school budgets,

02:16:08.540 --> 02:16:12.440
as aging populations turned
their back on the young.

02:16:12.440 --> 02:16:14.630
Politicians found they
could make headlines

02:16:14.630 --> 02:16:18.710
by attacking the country's most
distinguished universities.

02:16:18.710 --> 02:16:21.140
Any one of these
changes alone would

02:16:21.140 --> 02:16:24.470
have created a degree
of difficulty for us,

02:16:24.470 --> 02:16:26.150
and any one of them
could be handled

02:16:26.150 --> 02:16:29.120
without doing major damage
to our system of research

02:16:29.120 --> 02:16:30.890
universities.

02:16:30.890 --> 02:16:35.059
The real difficulty stems
from the cumulative effect

02:16:35.059 --> 02:16:40.889
this damaging cascade of
policy and budgetary change.

02:16:40.889 --> 02:16:44.520
But beyond financial matters,
this darkening climate

02:16:44.520 --> 02:16:47.190
has spawned three
disastrous policy errors

02:16:47.190 --> 02:16:49.920
that threaten our nation's
continuing achievement

02:16:49.920 --> 02:16:53.190
in science and technology.

02:16:53.190 --> 02:16:55.950
The first is an inaccurate,
unhelpful, and somewhat

02:16:55.950 --> 02:17:00.830
partisan categorization of
research as basic, on one hand,

02:17:00.830 --> 02:17:04.650
or applied or strategic on
the other, and the setting up

02:17:04.650 --> 02:17:09.420
of an adversarial relationship
among these categories.

02:17:09.420 --> 02:17:14.219
Second is the separation of the
goals of research and education

02:17:14.219 --> 02:17:17.879
in university funding,
and third is a failure

02:17:17.879 --> 02:17:21.680
to recognize research
funding as an investment.

02:17:21.680 --> 02:17:23.450
Each of these
errors has a history

02:17:23.450 --> 02:17:25.850
of accumulated political
baggage, and a set

02:17:25.850 --> 02:17:29.290
of disturbing consequences.

02:17:29.290 --> 02:17:31.150
Early in the Clinton
administration,

02:17:31.150 --> 02:17:32.770
the federal government
was caught up

02:17:32.770 --> 02:17:36.961
in a debate about what was
called strategic research.

02:17:36.961 --> 02:17:39.889
The term referred to
projects which had clearly

02:17:39.889 --> 02:17:42.620
foreseeable commercial value.

02:17:42.620 --> 02:17:44.450
Its supporters in
Congress believed

02:17:44.450 --> 02:17:46.400
that the nation's
research establishment

02:17:46.400 --> 02:17:50.360
had a responsibility to provide
direct aid to industries

02:17:50.360 --> 02:17:54.570
that were being clobbered in
the international marketplace.

02:17:54.570 --> 02:17:56.830
A number of funding
agencies dramatically

02:17:56.830 --> 02:17:58.900
shifted their focus
to proposals that

02:17:58.900 --> 02:18:02.770
involved industrial partners,
together with universities

02:18:02.770 --> 02:18:06.459
that might have a rapid
commercial payoff.

02:18:06.459 --> 02:18:10.629
But only two years later, just
two years later, the pendulum

02:18:10.629 --> 02:18:13.360
is at the other
end of its swing.

02:18:13.360 --> 02:18:16.299
Many of the most powerful
members of the House

02:18:16.299 --> 02:18:18.100
now declare that
the proper function

02:18:18.100 --> 02:18:21.760
of the federal government is
only to fund basic university

02:18:21.760 --> 02:18:23.080
research.

02:18:23.080 --> 02:18:26.440
They see things called applied
research as the responsibility

02:18:26.440 --> 02:18:28.420
of industry alone.

02:18:28.420 --> 02:18:31.090
Collaboration between
corporations, government,

02:18:31.090 --> 02:18:34.510
and universities suddenly
is being discouraged.

02:18:34.510 --> 02:18:38.260
There is an implicit threat
to education and research

02:18:38.260 --> 02:18:42.580
in the field of engineering
in all of this discussion.

02:18:42.580 --> 02:18:44.620
What money is left in
the research budget

02:18:44.620 --> 02:18:48.340
is to be focused on more
fundamental science, which,

02:18:48.340 --> 02:18:51.340
though of course
absolutely essential,

02:18:51.340 --> 02:18:55.540
should not be the entire
portfolio of any nation that

02:18:55.540 --> 02:18:58.190
desires a vibrant future.

02:18:58.190 --> 02:19:00.480
What's wrong with this picture?

02:19:00.480 --> 02:19:03.600
For one thing, it's
predicated on the notion

02:19:03.600 --> 02:19:07.799
that research enterprises can
be turned around on a dime.

02:19:07.799 --> 02:19:10.080
Today we make better
toasters, tomorrow we

02:19:10.080 --> 02:19:12.600
decode the human genome.

02:19:12.600 --> 02:19:14.790
I don't need to belabor
for this audience

02:19:14.790 --> 02:19:17.370
how absurd this idea is.

02:19:17.370 --> 02:19:21.209
Science, technology, and
advanced education cannot be

02:19:21.209 --> 02:19:25.559
turned on and off at will, nor
can they undergo major changes

02:19:25.559 --> 02:19:29.000
of direction every two years.

02:19:29.000 --> 02:19:32.420
Research and development
is a continuum, of course.

02:19:32.420 --> 02:19:35.389
Indeed, research, development,
and product commercialization

02:19:35.389 --> 02:19:38.900
is now a very different
process than it once was.

02:19:38.900 --> 02:19:40.879
Its nonlinear
workings will not be

02:19:40.879 --> 02:19:44.430
enhanced by artificial
partitioning of research,

02:19:44.430 --> 02:19:47.600
especially when our parallel
objective is educating

02:19:47.600 --> 02:19:50.150
scientists, engineers,
and managers

02:19:50.150 --> 02:19:53.000
who have the integrated
perspective needed

02:19:53.000 --> 02:19:55.460
for the next century.

02:19:55.460 --> 02:19:58.460
Now, this leads me to
the second, and really

02:19:58.460 --> 02:20:02.440
most fundamental, policy
error, one that truly could

02:20:02.440 --> 02:20:05.070
sabotage our future, I believe.

02:20:05.070 --> 02:20:09.530
And that's the separation
of education and research

02:20:09.530 --> 02:20:12.160
in federal funding policy.

02:20:12.160 --> 02:20:16.060
Preparing the next generations
of scientists and engineers

02:20:16.060 --> 02:20:19.090
has long been a central feature
of the American research

02:20:19.090 --> 02:20:20.800
university.

02:20:20.800 --> 02:20:23.320
As Paul so eloquently
pointed out,

02:20:23.320 --> 02:20:25.090
Vannevar Bush
envisioned a system

02:20:25.090 --> 02:20:28.330
in which every dollar invested
in university research

02:20:28.330 --> 02:20:30.130
did double duty.

02:20:30.130 --> 02:20:33.970
It met the scientific or
technical goals of the project,

02:20:33.970 --> 02:20:39.400
and it supported the education
of our graduate students.

02:20:39.400 --> 02:20:42.880
Bush's vision and policy
was spectacularly successful

02:20:42.880 --> 02:20:44.380
at MIT.

02:20:44.380 --> 02:20:47.064
The graduate enrollment
grew from 1,600

02:20:47.064 --> 02:20:52.190
in 1950 to 5,300 this year.

02:20:52.190 --> 02:20:54.020
Under this policy,
the involvement

02:20:54.020 --> 02:20:58.160
of graduate students, and indeed
of undergraduates, in research

02:20:58.160 --> 02:21:04.150
has become a defining element of
MIT's institutional excellence.

02:21:04.150 --> 02:21:07.270
And yet, since the late
1980s, the government

02:21:07.270 --> 02:21:10.180
has been withdrawing from
its long-term commitment

02:21:10.180 --> 02:21:12.700
to pay the full costs
of research, which

02:21:12.700 --> 02:21:16.540
include those expenses
incurred by graduate research

02:21:16.540 --> 02:21:18.160
assistants.

02:21:18.160 --> 02:21:21.370
The size of federally
funded graduate stipends,

02:21:21.370 --> 02:21:23.200
and even of prestigious
fellowships,

02:21:23.200 --> 02:21:26.860
has dropped below any pretense
of covering the real costs

02:21:26.860 --> 02:21:29.060
of graduate support.

02:21:29.060 --> 02:21:31.520
Just this last
week, the director

02:21:31.520 --> 02:21:33.410
of the National
Institutes of Health

02:21:33.410 --> 02:21:39.520
said flatly that NIH is about
research, not about education.

02:21:39.520 --> 02:21:41.650
The view now holding
sway in Washington

02:21:41.650 --> 02:21:43.810
that the country's
requirement for new knowledge

02:21:43.810 --> 02:21:46.270
can be met over
time by providing

02:21:46.270 --> 02:21:48.400
only the cost of
current projects

02:21:48.400 --> 02:21:51.190
is myopic in the extreme.

02:21:51.190 --> 02:21:52.990
What could be more
efficient and more

02:21:52.990 --> 02:21:57.130
cost-effective than the double
value of research and education

02:21:57.130 --> 02:22:00.430
that can be credited to
each federal dollar spent

02:22:00.430 --> 02:22:03.950
in an American
research university.

02:22:03.950 --> 02:22:06.410
Continuing to talk about
the value of research

02:22:06.410 --> 02:22:08.120
while making
inadequate provisions

02:22:08.120 --> 02:22:10.760
for graduate education
simply amounts

02:22:10.760 --> 02:22:13.540
to eating our seed corn.

02:22:13.540 --> 02:22:17.480
The third policy error I see
is the increasing tendency

02:22:17.480 --> 02:22:23.680
to define research and
development as a cost.

02:22:23.680 --> 02:22:25.760
Research is not a cost.

02:22:25.760 --> 02:22:27.410
It's an investment.

02:22:27.410 --> 02:22:30.280
It's an investment with a
remarkable financial return,

02:22:30.280 --> 02:22:34.500
even beyond its payoff
in human resources.

02:22:34.500 --> 02:22:37.480
Even the General
Accounting Office,

02:22:37.480 --> 02:22:39.970
after reviewing the modest
literature of the subject,

02:22:39.970 --> 02:22:43.390
agrees that the annual return on
federal investment in research

02:22:43.390 --> 02:22:47.800
and development is of
the order of 25% to 50%.

02:22:47.800 --> 02:22:51.310
Michael Dertouzos, head of our
laboratory for computer science

02:22:51.310 --> 02:22:55.660
here at MIT, makes a
very persuasive case,

02:22:55.660 --> 02:23:00.130
that we have realized a
3,000% return to America

02:23:00.130 --> 02:23:03.790
on the federal investment in
computer science and technology

02:23:03.790 --> 02:23:07.700
of $5 billion over
the last 30 years.

02:23:07.700 --> 02:23:09.830
A look at the federal
budget proposals

02:23:09.830 --> 02:23:11.420
that are emerging
from the Congress

02:23:11.420 --> 02:23:14.810
and from the administration
show little appreciation

02:23:14.810 --> 02:23:17.700
for facts like this.

02:23:17.700 --> 02:23:23.550
What are we to do about this
dramatic change in our climate?

02:23:23.550 --> 02:23:27.860
One thing we cannot do is to sit
by waiting for our fate to be

02:23:27.860 --> 02:23:29.570
decided.

02:23:29.570 --> 02:23:32.540
More than at any time
since World War II,

02:23:32.540 --> 02:23:34.970
my colleagues in the
faculty and staff and I

02:23:34.970 --> 02:23:38.360
are working to make our
case in Washington, our case

02:23:38.360 --> 02:23:43.220
that research universities in
general, and MIT in particular,

02:23:43.220 --> 02:23:46.320
represent not a drain
on the national purse,

02:23:46.320 --> 02:23:50.650
but an investment in the
nation's future well-being.

02:23:50.650 --> 02:23:53.680
It is not enough for us
to just declare our value

02:23:53.680 --> 02:23:55.360
to the nation, however.

02:23:55.360 --> 02:23:57.672
We of course have
to demonstrate it.

02:23:57.672 --> 02:23:59.940
We are speaking in
every possible forum

02:23:59.940 --> 02:24:03.540
about the current research
at MIT, and about the promise

02:24:03.540 --> 02:24:05.100
that it holds.

02:24:05.100 --> 02:24:09.660
We need your help in delivering
and demonstrating this message.

02:24:09.660 --> 02:24:12.150
It's up to us, to
all of us, working

02:24:12.150 --> 02:24:14.690
with our colleagues
in other universities,

02:24:14.690 --> 02:24:18.150
the national associations,
and in business and industry

02:24:18.150 --> 02:24:21.300
to communicate with our
legislators, with the press,

02:24:21.300 --> 02:24:23.400
and with the public at large.

02:24:23.400 --> 02:24:26.760
We must persuade them
that knowledge, knowledge

02:24:26.760 --> 02:24:30.060
and a population skilled
in its creative use,

02:24:30.060 --> 02:24:35.230
are America's most important
resources for the 21st century.

02:24:35.230 --> 02:24:38.440
Members of Congress
still listen most closely

02:24:38.440 --> 02:24:40.480
to their own constituents.

02:24:40.480 --> 02:24:42.550
Talk to them personally.

02:24:42.550 --> 02:24:45.700
With your help, MIT and the
nation's other leading public

02:24:45.700 --> 02:24:48.130
and private research
universities

02:24:48.130 --> 02:24:52.390
will continue their tradition
of creativity and leadership

02:24:52.390 --> 02:24:55.850
and service to our society.

02:24:55.850 --> 02:25:00.260
The late 1990s will be a time
of reinvention and redefinition

02:25:00.260 --> 02:25:02.640
of the research university,
for all the reasons

02:25:02.640 --> 02:25:05.370
that Lester has pointed out.

02:25:05.370 --> 02:25:07.290
I'm confident that
we will emerge

02:25:07.290 --> 02:25:10.470
from this troubled time
rededicated to serving

02:25:10.470 --> 02:25:13.140
our nation and our world.

02:25:13.140 --> 02:25:16.140
I'm not pleading
for the status quo.

02:25:16.140 --> 02:25:19.110
Rather, I challenge you,
as alumni and alumnae,

02:25:19.110 --> 02:25:21.150
and I challenge our
federal government,

02:25:21.150 --> 02:25:24.030
and especially I
challenge our industries

02:25:24.030 --> 02:25:26.550
to join with us
in a reinvigorated

02:25:26.550 --> 02:25:29.100
and redefined partnership.

02:25:29.100 --> 02:25:32.190
This partnership must
establish a new national R&amp;D

02:25:32.190 --> 02:25:35.760
system and a new national
innovation system

02:25:35.760 --> 02:25:40.130
to meet the challenges of the
coming economic competition.

02:25:40.130 --> 02:25:42.500
The next round, I
believe, will be

02:25:42.500 --> 02:25:46.310
based on excellence in
design and innovation,

02:25:46.310 --> 02:25:48.320
and on successfully
addressing broader

02:25:48.320 --> 02:25:52.280
social and environmental
issues, just as the competition

02:25:52.280 --> 02:25:55.520
of this last decade
required us to learn

02:25:55.520 --> 02:25:58.620
new ways of manufacturing
at low cost,

02:25:58.620 --> 02:26:03.130
high quality, and decreased
product cycle times.

02:26:03.130 --> 02:26:06.210
So let us affix our seals
to a new social contract

02:26:06.210 --> 02:26:09.390
for education and the
advancement of human knowledge

02:26:09.390 --> 02:26:11.310
and capability.

02:26:11.310 --> 02:26:14.100
Through this new partnership,
with your guidance

02:26:14.100 --> 02:26:17.190
and your financial
support, MIT will

02:26:17.190 --> 02:26:20.820
continue leading the revolution
in molecular biology,

02:26:20.820 --> 02:26:25.650
and advance the promise
of biotechnology.

02:26:25.650 --> 02:26:29.150
We will come to understand the
workings of the human brain

02:26:29.150 --> 02:26:34.640
and the nature of intelligence,
of learning, of memory.

02:26:34.640 --> 02:26:37.070
We will bring the
MIT quality of mind

02:26:37.070 --> 02:26:41.060
to assessing and ameliorating
the impact of humankind

02:26:41.060 --> 02:26:43.540
on the Earth's environment.

02:26:43.540 --> 02:26:46.910
We will secure the advances
of computers, communication

02:26:46.910 --> 02:26:50.270
technology, and the
information marketplace

02:26:50.270 --> 02:26:52.550
for the social good.

02:26:52.550 --> 02:26:56.270
We will combine the aesthetic,
the humane, and the technical

02:26:56.270 --> 02:26:58.610
in the design of the
physical environment

02:26:58.610 --> 02:27:01.810
and the creation of
more livable cities.

02:27:01.810 --> 02:27:05.260
We will better understand
organizations and businesses,

02:27:05.260 --> 02:27:06.880
and how to make
them more effective

02:27:06.880 --> 02:27:10.360
in building vital and
sustainable 21st century

02:27:10.360 --> 02:27:12.410
economies.

02:27:12.410 --> 02:27:14.650
And we will enrich
the liberal, visual,

02:27:14.650 --> 02:27:17.800
and performing arts
that, in large measure,

02:27:17.800 --> 02:27:21.960
define what it is to be human.

02:27:21.960 --> 02:27:26.800
In sum, with your help, and
with a new partnership secured,

02:27:26.800 --> 02:27:30.840
we can be confident that
education and research at MIT

02:27:30.840 --> 02:27:33.930
will contribute to our
nation and to the world

02:27:33.930 --> 02:27:38.894
a future that is more secure,
prosperous, and exciting

02:27:38.894 --> 02:27:39.852
than any we have known.

02:27:39.852 --> 02:27:41.328
Thank you very much.

02:27:41.328 --> 02:27:45.264
[APPLAUSE]

02:27:55.610 --> 02:28:00.380
And now, I have the pleasure
to invite all of you

02:28:00.380 --> 02:28:03.230
to step outside for an
event I'm sure you're

02:28:03.230 --> 02:28:07.820
anticipating, possibly after
a rapid stop at the restroom.

02:28:07.820 --> 02:28:11.810
And that will be the flyby
of six vintage World War

02:28:11.810 --> 02:28:13.700
II airplanes.

02:28:13.700 --> 02:28:16.850
They will be led
by a B-25 similar

02:28:16.850 --> 02:28:20.900
to the one flown by MIT's
own general Jimmy Doolittle.

02:28:20.900 --> 02:28:24.200
The planes will fly
down the Charles River,

02:28:24.200 --> 02:28:26.150
circle over Boston
Harbor, and then

02:28:26.150 --> 02:28:29.180
return back up the Charles.

02:28:29.180 --> 02:28:33.500
This event is not a celebration
of military hardware,

02:28:33.500 --> 02:28:36.620
even of antique
military hardware.

02:28:36.620 --> 02:28:38.630
Rather, it is a tribute.

02:28:38.630 --> 02:28:41.210
It is intended to be a
tribute to all the members

02:28:41.210 --> 02:28:45.260
of this community who have
given their lives in service

02:28:45.260 --> 02:28:47.150
to their country.

02:28:47.150 --> 02:28:49.520
We've been told that the
best view of the aircraft

02:28:49.520 --> 02:28:52.310
will be from the grassy
area between the auditorium

02:28:52.310 --> 02:28:53.790
and the parking lot.

02:28:53.790 --> 02:28:56.680
Between the auditorium
and the parking lot,

02:28:56.680 --> 02:28:59.680
or also from the
parking lot itself.

02:28:59.680 --> 02:29:01.970
Now, after the planes
have completed their loop

02:29:01.970 --> 02:29:06.740
over the Charles, please adjourn
to the Johnson Athletic Center.

02:29:06.740 --> 02:29:10.160
I remind you that serving
lunch to 1,200 people

02:29:10.160 --> 02:29:14.330
is a task requiring an
almost military organization,

02:29:14.330 --> 02:29:16.580
although I'm sure
the veterans among us

02:29:16.580 --> 02:29:20.210
will attest that the food is
hopefully a little better here.

02:29:20.210 --> 02:29:23.060
Your cooperation by being
in your places somewhere

02:29:23.060 --> 02:29:26.450
around 12:45 when
lunch service begins

02:29:26.450 --> 02:29:28.400
will be much appreciated.

02:29:28.400 --> 02:29:30.890
My understanding is
that the flyby now

02:29:30.890 --> 02:29:33.470
is scheduled for
12:20, so we have

02:29:33.470 --> 02:29:36.088
time to make our way orderly
out and get in position.

02:29:36.088 --> 02:29:38.080
Thank you again very much.

02:29:38.080 --> 02:29:41.566
[APPLAUSE]

