02:31:58.210 --> 02:31:59.440
- And here they come.

02:31:59.440 --> 02:32:01.576
I see airplanes.

02:32:01.576 --> 02:32:05.069
[MUSIC PLAYING]

02:33:48.450 --> 02:33:50.640
- And the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

02:33:50.640 --> 02:33:53.585
remembers World War
II 50 years later.

02:33:53.585 --> 02:33:54.555
Stay with us.

02:33:54.555 --> 02:33:57.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

02:33:58.920 --> 02:34:02.220
- This is New
England Cable News.

02:34:02.220 --> 02:34:05.610
- It was a day back in
time, some 50 years in time,

02:34:05.610 --> 02:34:06.660
yesterday in Boston.

02:34:06.660 --> 02:34:09.840
Eight World War II aircraft
flew over the campus

02:34:09.840 --> 02:34:12.567
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology.

02:34:12.567 --> 02:34:14.400
New England Cable News
reporter Tom Melville

02:34:14.400 --> 02:34:16.620
has the story of the
commemoration of MIT's war

02:34:16.620 --> 02:34:18.548
effort half a century later.

02:34:24.830 --> 02:34:27.350
- At Hanscom Field in
Lincoln, Massachusetts,

02:34:27.350 --> 02:34:29.540
a worker fueled up a
sparkling collection

02:34:29.540 --> 02:34:31.926
of American technology
from World War II.

02:34:35.314 --> 02:34:40.870
Engines revved,
and then the planes

02:34:40.870 --> 02:34:44.290
roared into the sky over eastern
Massachusetts for a flight

02:34:44.290 --> 02:34:47.320
back in time, a flight
to honor the World War II

02:34:47.320 --> 02:34:50.560
efforts of the students and
faculty of the Massachusetts

02:34:50.560 --> 02:34:51.910
Institute of Technology.

02:34:54.900 --> 02:35:00.700
148 MIT alumni died serving the
United States in World War II.

02:35:00.700 --> 02:35:04.074
This flight of eight World
War II planes in formation

02:35:04.074 --> 02:35:05.048
remembered them.

02:35:08.460 --> 02:35:11.910
As the formation passed
over MIT's Cambridge campus,

02:35:11.910 --> 02:35:14.160
hundreds of alumni
gathered on the ground,

02:35:14.160 --> 02:35:16.750
looked up, and remembered
their classmates.

02:35:16.750 --> 02:35:19.950
- This is a shining moment
for MIT, and in commemoration

02:35:19.950 --> 02:35:21.670
of all those men who
gave their lives,

02:35:21.670 --> 02:35:24.360
and in commemoration of the
men like Frank Reintjes here.

02:35:24.360 --> 02:35:27.060
- Professor Frank Reintjes
stayed on the ground.

02:35:27.060 --> 02:35:29.580
During World War II,
he stayed at home.

02:35:29.580 --> 02:35:33.960
But he and others at MIT helped
win the war for the Allies.

02:35:33.960 --> 02:35:39.480
Reintjes taught at the MIT
Radar School from 1943 to 1946.

02:35:39.480 --> 02:35:46.500
- We trained 3,000 Navy
officers, Air Corps officers

02:35:46.500 --> 02:35:49.010
and men, and Signal
Corps officers and men.

02:35:52.190 --> 02:35:54.770
- Radar technology
developed at MIT

02:35:54.770 --> 02:35:57.540
helped the United
States and its allies.

02:35:57.540 --> 02:36:01.810
- Radar, as operated
by the British,

02:36:01.810 --> 02:36:04.370
was vital to the
protection of London,

02:36:04.370 --> 02:36:12.380
because they could see German
aircraft rising on the mainland

02:36:12.380 --> 02:36:15.680
long before these were visible.

02:36:15.680 --> 02:36:18.860
- The flight, for those who
flew it and those who watched,

02:36:18.860 --> 02:36:22.100
highlighted MIT's role in
the development of a number

02:36:22.100 --> 02:36:23.990
of wartime technologies.

02:36:23.990 --> 02:36:26.180
- During World War
II, MIT was involved

02:36:26.180 --> 02:36:29.480
in several new technologies, and
was very critical to the war.

02:36:29.480 --> 02:36:31.010
The radar development,
which allowed

02:36:31.010 --> 02:36:34.670
the bombing to be taking
place through smoke and fog

02:36:34.670 --> 02:36:38.120
and clouds, and also
materials technology that

02:36:38.120 --> 02:36:40.940
ultimately resulted in the
jet engine turbine blades.

02:36:40.940 --> 02:36:43.940
- MIT also developed
the Draper gun sight,

02:36:43.940 --> 02:36:47.630
which allowed aircraft-mounted
guns to hit moving targets,

02:36:47.630 --> 02:36:48.265
enemy aircraft.

02:36:50.850 --> 02:36:53.550
B-25 bombers used the sights.

02:36:53.550 --> 02:36:56.010
This B-25 flew in the formation.

02:36:56.010 --> 02:36:59.160
This was the type of bomber
used by General Jimmy Doolittle,

02:36:59.160 --> 02:37:01.680
the MIT grad who led
the first bombing runs

02:37:01.680 --> 02:37:05.580
over mainland Japan, and
turned the tide of the war.

02:37:05.580 --> 02:37:08.730
What a joy to fly a
B-25 in peacetime,

02:37:08.730 --> 02:37:10.810
half a century after the war.

02:37:10.810 --> 02:37:12.900
- I just wonder why I had
the absolute best seat.

02:37:12.900 --> 02:37:14.860
I was in the
[INAUDIBLE] position,

02:37:14.860 --> 02:37:16.740
so I could see everybody around.

02:37:16.740 --> 02:37:18.750
I could see the two
fighters off on either side.

02:37:18.750 --> 02:37:19.916
And it was just spectacular.

02:37:19.916 --> 02:37:23.752
And coming around Boston as they
did, it was just unbelievable.

02:37:23.752 --> 02:37:24.960
- This brings it home to you.

02:37:24.960 --> 02:37:26.780
If you were in one
of those airplanes

02:37:26.780 --> 02:37:29.490
and flying, and feeling like
you're flying this thing

02:37:29.490 --> 02:37:33.810
and you're in the turbulent
airwaves behind it, you know,

02:37:33.810 --> 02:37:37.300
the air turbulence, being
shot at all at the same time,

02:37:37.300 --> 02:37:38.910
it brings it very close to home.

02:37:38.910 --> 02:37:42.090
- So much of the technology
developed during World War II

02:37:42.090 --> 02:37:43.720
is still in use today.

02:37:43.720 --> 02:37:46.740
This DC-3 aircraft served
in both the European

02:37:46.740 --> 02:37:49.230
and Mediterranean
theaters in World War II.

02:37:49.230 --> 02:37:52.890
On D-Day, it trailed gliders
carrying US servicemen

02:37:52.890 --> 02:37:53.790
into battle.

02:37:53.790 --> 02:37:56.030
After the war, it became
a corporate aircraft,

02:37:56.030 --> 02:37:58.770
and it still is.

02:37:58.770 --> 02:38:02.430
This flight through sunny blue
skies over a nation at peace,

02:38:02.430 --> 02:38:04.440
over the green patchwork
that is greater

02:38:04.440 --> 02:38:08.790
Boston, celebrated technology,
celebrated a school's efforts

02:38:08.790 --> 02:38:12.420
toward victory, remembered
a school's gift of its sons

02:38:12.420 --> 02:38:13.470
to a nation.

02:38:13.470 --> 02:38:15.677
Tom Melville, New
England Cable News.

